window.__DIYDB__=window.__DIYDB__||{data:{}};window.__DIYDB__.data["gladiator"]=[{"id":"jt-front-bumper","title":"Aftermarket Front Bumper for the Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"armor","type":"kit","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":499,"high":1800},"tools_required":["socket set (metric)","torque wrench","floor jack (for initial alignment)","wire stripper (if adding lights)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","basic 12V wiring for lights"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["armor_bumper_front","armor","bumper","winch-mount","recovery"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn Ascent Front Bumper \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":899},{"name":"Poison Spyder Brawler Pro Front Bumper \u2014 JT","vendor":"Poison Spyder","price_estimate_usd":649},{"name":"Fab Fours Premium JT Front Bumper with Guard","vendor":"Fab Fours","price_estimate_usd":1199},{"name":"ARB Sahara Front Bar \u2014 JT Gladiator","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":1399},{"name":"Rough Country Heavy-Duty Front Bumper \u2014 JT","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":499}],"citations":[{"title":"Warn Ascent JT Gladiator Bumper Fitment Guide","url":"https://www.warn.com"},{"title":"ARB JT Sahara Bar Overview","url":"https://www.arbusa.com"},{"title":"Fab Fours JT Premium Bumper Product Page","url":"https://www.fabfours.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-rear-bumper","jt-rocker-sliders"],"summary":"The factory JT front bumper is plastic-capped sheet metal \u2014 it looks reasonably rugged but won't withstand a direct ledge strike, and it doesn't offer a winch mount. An aftermarket steel bumper adds protection, a winch platform, and proper recovery point mounting in one upgrade.","description":"The JT Gladiator's factory bumper bolts to the frame rails at four points. All quality aftermarket bumpers use the same factory mounting holes \u2014 no drilling, no modification required. The bumper weight varies significantly by design: tube-style bumpers run 60\u201390 lbs; full-width steel plate bumpers run 100\u2013160 lbs. The extra weight sits at the extreme front of the vehicle and affects front suspension behavior. Account for the added weight when selecting shocks and, if significant, when choosing coil spring rates.\n\n**Factory parking sensor and camera compatibility**\n\nThe JT Gladiator includes front parking sensors on some trim levels and a forward-facing camera on some packages. Before ordering a bumper, confirm whether your JT has these sensors and whether the replacement bumper provides sensor mounting provisions. Most premium bumpers (Warn, ARB, Fab Fours) offer sensor provisions; budget bumpers often don't.\n\n**Bumper categories**\n\n**Tube-style (Poison Spyder, Rough Country):** Tubular steel construction \u2014 lighter, allows better approach angle visibility, still provides frame-mounted recovery points. The open design does not protect the radiator from direct debris strikes the way a plate bumper does, but it maintains airflow better and reduces the visual bulk. Correct for trail-oriented JTs where approach angle and weight matter.\n\n**Mid-width plate (Warn Ascent, Fab Fours):** A full-width or mid-width steel plate bumper that covers the factory bumper footprint. These typically include integrated winch mounts (the Warn Ascent includes a Warn winch platform factory-ready), full-width recovery point tabs, and provisions for auxiliary lights. The tradeoff: approach angle is slightly compromised versus a narrower tube bumper, and weight is higher.\n\n**Premium full-width (ARB Sahara, ~$1,399):** The benchmark for dual-use trail/street builds. ARB's Sahara design offers factory-quality fitment with provisions for OEM fog lights, parking sensors, and ARB Air Locker air lines. The Sahara is heavier than tube bumpers but integrates seamlessly with the factory look \u2014 on a JT that divides time between daily driving and trail work, the ARB balances protection and aesthetics well.\n\n**Winch compatibility**\n\nMost JT aftermarket bumpers that include a winch mount are compatible with winches in the 8,000\u201312,000 lb class. Confirm the bumper's winch plate dimensions before ordering a winch \u2014 a Warn M8000 (7.5\" wide winch body) fits most mid-size bumper fairleads, but a Warn 9.5xp (wider body) requires a bumper with a wider opening. The ARB Sahara is pre-drilled for specific ARB-spec winch mounting; Warn Ascent is optimized for Warn winch fitment.\n\n1. Remove the factory front bumper: typically six bolts \u2014 two per side mounting to the bumper reinforcement bar, plus two frame rail bolts. The reinforcement bar (the steel bar behind the plastic fascia) also needs to come off \u2014 it's bolted to the frame rails separately.\n2. On JTs with parking sensors: unplug the sensor harness connectors behind the bumper before removing. Label each connector if multiple sensors are present.\n3. Set the factory bumper and reinforcement aside.\n4. Thread the new bumper onto the frame rails. Most designs require two people \u2014 one to hold the bumper in position, one to start the mounting bolts.\n5. Start all bolts hand-tight before torquing any of them \u2014 this allows the bumper to self-align.\n6. Torque the mounting bolts: typically 75\u201390 ft-lbs for frame-rail mounting hardware.\n7. Wire auxiliary lights per the light manufacturer's instructions and the bumper's integrated harness provisions (if any).\n8. Install recovery hardware (D-rings, shackles) in the recovery point tabs. Torque shackle pins to hand-tight plus 1/4 turn. Mousing wire if available.\n\n- **Airbag sensor location**: Some JT trims have a crash sensor on the factory bumper reinforcement bar. This sensor must either be relocated or confirmed to not be present before removing the reinforcement. Check the service manual for your specific trim.\n- **Grille and hood clearance**: Heavier plate bumpers can sit slightly higher than the factory bumper line and may require the hood stop rubber to be adjusted for correct hood-to-grille gap.\n- **Weight and alignment**: Adding 60\u2013120 lbs to the front changes the front-to-rear weight balance. After bumper install, check that the caster and alignment are still within spec \u2014 and if adding a winch simultaneously, get an alignment check.\n\nJTs equipped with Adaptive Cruise Control carry a radar module mounted low in the center of the front fascia, and this is the most-missed compatibility item on bumper orders. The module has to end up at the correct height and angle behind the new bumper or ACC and forward-collision warning will fault out \u2014 a dash full of warnings on the first drive is the usual symptom of a bumper installed without the radar bracket. Premium bumpers (ARB, Warn, Fab Fours) list an ACC-compatible variant or include a relocation bracket; many budget bumpers ignore the radar entirely. Confirm your truck's equipment before ordering, and buy the bumper variant that matches. If the system throws faults after a correct mechanical install, the radar may need re-aiming with a dealer scan tool \u2014 budget for that possibility.\n\nA handful of manufacturers offer JT front bumpers in aluminum at roughly half the weight of the equivalent steel design \u2014 a full-width aluminum bumper can come in near tube-steel weight while offering plate-style coverage. The tradeoffs are real: aluminum costs several hundred dollars more for the same design, and it responds to rock strikes differently \u2014 steel dents and bends where aluminum can gouge or crack, and a cracked aluminum weld is a harder field repair. For a JT that carries a winch and sees regular rock contact, plate steel remains the default answer. For an overland build fighting front-axle weight \u2014 where the winch, bumper, and larger tires stack onto the front springs \u2014 aluminum buys back meaningful payload and steering feel. Pair either choice with the front spring-rate conversation in the [mid-tier lift guide](/db/gladiator/jt-mid-lift/), because a 140-lb bumper-and-winch package on stock coils drops the nose visibly.\n\nRough Country Heavy-Duty: ~$499. Entry-level, basic winch mount, no sensor provisions. Acceptable for an off-road-only JT.\n\nPoison Spyder Brawler Pro: ~$649. Better build quality, cleaner welds, sensor provisions available. The correct mid-tier choice.\n\nWarn Ascent: ~$899. The premium mid-tier. Winch platform and recovery points included. Warn's warranty and build quality are the reasons to pay the premium.\n\nFab Fours Premium with Guard: ~$1,199. Full-width protection with grille guard. Best protection-per-dollar in the full-width category.\n\nARB Sahara Bar: ~$1,399. The benchmark for dual-use builds. Highest integration with factory features and a track record that other brands chase."},{"id":"jt-rear-bumper","title":"Aftermarket Rear Bumper for the Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"armor","type":"kit","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":349,"high":1299},"tools_required":["socket set (metric)","torque wrench","floor jack (for initial alignment)","wire stripper (if adding backup sensors or lights)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["armor_bumper_rear","armor","bumper","recovery","hitch"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn Ascent Rear Bumper \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":799},{"name":"Poison Spyder Brawler Pro Rear Bumper \u2014 JT","vendor":"Poison Spyder","price_estimate_usd":549},{"name":"Fab Fours Premium JT Rear Bumper","vendor":"Fab Fours","price_estimate_usd":999},{"name":"ARB Rear Bar \u2014 JT Gladiator","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":1199},{"name":"Rough Country JT Rear Bumper with Hitch","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":349}],"citations":[{"title":"Warn Ascent JT Rear Bumper Product Page","url":"https://www.warn.com"},{"title":"ARB JT Rear Bar Overview","url":"https://www.arbusa.com"},{"title":"Fab Fours JT Rear Bumper Fitment","url":"https://www.fabfours.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-front-bumper","jt-tow-hook-upgrade","jt-recovery-gear-essentials","jt-winch"],"summary":"The JT Gladiator's rear is the truck section of the build \u2014 and the factory rear bumper is plastic-covered sheet metal that won't withstand a hard backing incident or a recovery strap attachment under load. An aftermarket rear bumper adds rated recovery points and, on the JT's truck platform, often retains the factory towing hitch receiver.","description":"The JT Gladiator's rear bumper sits lower than the JL Wrangler's because the truck has a tow hitch receiver and a longer tail section. This affects departure angle: the factory bumper and hitch receiver are the first contact points when descending a steep exit. A tubular or high-clearance aftermarket bumper raises the effective departure angle and moves contact points inward to the frame rails.\n\n**JT-specific rear bumper considerations**\n\nHitch retention: Most JT aftermarket rear bumpers include or integrate with a 2\" receiver hitch. The factory hitch is rated at 7,650 lbs tow capacity (Class III). Aftermarket bumpers with integrated hitches often carry a similar rating, but verify before towing \u2014 not all aftermarket hitches are rated to the same specification.\n\nSpare tire carrier: The JT doesn't carry a spare tire on the rear bumper (unlike the JL Wrangler) \u2014 the JT's spare is typically stored under the bed. This means JT rear bumpers don't need a swing-out spare carrier, which streamlines the design and reduces cost compared to JL Wrangler equivalents.\n\nBackup sensor provisions: If the JT has a factory backup camera (all trims), the camera mounts in the factory tailgate, not the bumper \u2014 so camera relocation isn't required for a rear bumper swap. Parking sensors on some Sport S and Rubicon models may be bumper-mounted. Confirm before ordering.\n\n**Bumper options**\n\n**Rough Country ($349):** The entry point. Basic steel construction, includes a 2\" hitch receiver, and provides rear recovery point tabs. Fit and finish is adequate; build quality is lighter gauge than premium brands. Correct for budget builds on a mild-trail JT.\n\n**Poison Spyder Brawler Pro ($549):** Frame-mounted with recovery point tabs rated at 4.75 tons. Tubular design improves departure angle versus full-width plate bumpers. Better construction than Rough Country \u2014 cleaner welds, heavier gauge, better powder coat.\n\n**Warn Ascent ($799):** Solid mid-premium option with full-width recovery points and hitch integration. Warn's hardware quality and finish consistency are the reasons to choose it over Poison Spyder at the $800 tier.\n\n**Fab Fours Premium ($999):** Full-width plate design with a tire step and recovery tabs. The full-width design provides more comprehensive protection against corner contact \u2014 relevant for a JT that backs into obstacles in technical terrain.\n\n**ARB Rear Bar ($1,199):** The premium tier. Factory-quality fitment with provisions for ARB-system air connections, integrated recovery tabs, and a hitch receiver rated for the factory tow spec. The ARB is the correct choice for a JT that will see sustained hard use and where fitment quality matters.\n\n1. Support the factory rear bumper with a floor jack before removing mounting hardware \u2014 the bumper is heavier than it looks.\n2. Remove the mounting bolts from the factory rear bumper: typically two frame rail bolts per side plus bumper reinforcement bolts. On the JT, the reinforcement bar may be integrated \u2014 remove the complete assembly.\n3. Disconnect backup sensor harness if equipped. Do not force any connector \u2014 squeeze the release tab.\n4. Position the new bumper against the frame mounting points. Use a helper or a floor jack to support the bumper during positioning.\n5. Start all mounting bolts hand-tight before torquing \u2014 allows self-alignment.\n6. Torque to spec: typically 60\u201380 ft-lbs for frame rail hardware.\n7. Verify hitch receiver is square and level. Test the connection with your tow ball or receiver insert before first towing use.\n\n- **Departure angle improvement**: Measure the factory bumper height at its lowest point and compare to the new bumper's lowest point. A 2\"\u20133\" gain in departure angle clearance is typical with a tubular high-clearance bumper \u2014 meaningful on a steep trail exit.\n- **Trailer wiring harness**: The factory 4-pin or 7-pin trailer wiring harness connects to a bracket on the factory rear bumper. Relocate the bracket to the new bumper's hitch area during install.\n- **Reverse light visibility**: A full-width rear bumper may partially block the factory reverse light angles. Check that backup visibility is acceptable after install; add supplemental LED reverse lights if not.\n\nA steel rear bumper is not a free upgrade on a truck you load. The factory rear bumper assembly weighs roughly 50 lbs; aftermarket steel replacements run 80-150 lbs depending on design (tubular designs like the Poison Spyder at the light end, full-width plate like the Fab Fours at the heavy end). That net 30-100 lbs comes directly out of payload \u2014 and on a Rubicon with ~1,200 lbs to start, armor at both ends plus a winch plus a bed full of gear eats it faster than most owners track. Weigh the truck loaded once at a CAT scale (~$14) and know your real number.\n\nTongue weight is the second rating to check. The factory setup is rated around 750 lbs tongue weight with the tow package. An aftermarket bumper with an integrated receiver carries its own tongue rating, and the cheaper units are sometimes rated lower than factory \u2014 a problem if you tow a trailer with real tongue load. The rating should be stamped on the hitch or stated in the documentation; if the manufacturer does not publish one, that silence is the answer.\n\nSteel bumpers rust from the mounting hardware outward. During install: put anti-seize on every mounting bolt (future-you removing them after five winters will be grateful), touch up any powder coat scratched during installation immediately with cold galvanizing spray before topcoat, and if you live where roads are salted, spray the bumper's internal cavities with a cavity wax (Fluid Film, Krown) once a year. The difference between a bumper that looks right at year eight and one that streaks rust down the paint at year three is thirty minutes of prevention. Recovery points deserve a periodic look too \u2014 check shackle tab welds for cracking after any hard recovery, and pair the bumper with proper gear from the JT recovery essentials guide rather than trusting a strap over a bare hitch ball.\n\nRough Country: ~$349. Entry-level adequate protection.\n\nPoison Spyder Brawler Pro: ~$549. Better construction, same departure angle improvement. The honest value in this category.\n\nWarn Ascent: ~$799. Premium hardware and finish, correct for a daily-driven trail JT.\n\nFab Fours Premium: ~$999. Full-width protection, looks substantial.\n\nARB Rear Bar: ~$1,199. The benchmark. Long-term value for a JT that will be driven hard consistently."},{"id":"jt-rocker-sliders","title":"Rocker Sliders for the Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"armor","type":"kit","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":399,"high":1200},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set (metric)","drill with 3/8\" bit (if drilling required)","torque wrench","angle grinder (for trimming if needed)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","drilling into rocker panel area"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["armor_sliders","armor","rocker-panel","sliders","protection"],"parts":[{"name":"Poison Spyder Rocker Knockers \u2014 JT Gladiator","vendor":"Poison Spyder","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"GenRight Off-Road Rock Sliders \u2014 JT Gladiator","vendor":"GenRight","price_estimate_usd":899},{"name":"Rock-Slide Engineering Step-Sliders \u2014 JT Gladiator","vendor":"Rock-Slide Engineering","price_estimate_usd":1099},{"name":"Smittybilt Rock Sliders \u2014 JT Gladiator","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":399},{"name":"Fab Fours Premium Rocker Guard \u2014 JT Gladiator","vendor":"Fab Fours","price_estimate_usd":799}],"citations":[{"title":"Poison Spyder Rocker Knockers JT Product Page","url":"https://www.poisonspyder.com"},{"title":"Rock-Slide Engineering JT Step-Slider Overview","url":"https://www.rock-slideengineering.com"},{"title":"GenRight JT Rocker Sliders Fitment Guide","url":"https://www.genright.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-front-bumper","jt-rear-bumper"],"summary":"Rocker panels are the lowest structural part of the JT's body. They're the first thing to contact rocks, stumps, and ledge edges in technical terrain. Sliders give you a replaceable, reinforced surface at that contact point \u2014 you slide over the obstacle instead of bending sheet metal and unibody structure.","description":"The JT Gladiator uses a body-on-frame architecture, which changes how rocker sliders mount compared to unibody vehicles. On the JT, quality sliders mount to the frame rail directly, not to the rocker panel sheet metal. This is the critical distinction between a slider that protects you and a slider that tears off and takes the rocker panel with it.\n\n**What to look for in a JT slider**\n\nFrame-mount construction (mandatory): The slider bolts to the frame rail, not to body sheet metal. On the JT, frame mounting points exist along the rocker section of the frame. Any slider that says \"no-drill\" and attaches only to body bolts or the rocker panel itself is a liability \u2014 it won't hold under side impact loads.\n\nWall thickness: 3/16\" DOM or 1/4\" steel tube is the industry minimum for serious rock contact. Laser-cut, CNC-bent steel is stronger than welded square tube for the same wall thickness.\n\nLength: The JT's wheelbase (137.3\") means the rocker panel extends further than on a JL Wrangler. Confirm that the slider covers the full door opening \u2014 sliders that are too short leave the rear section of the rocker exposed.\n\n**Options and trade-offs**\n\n**Smittybilt ($399):** The entry-level option. Steel construction, basic frame mounting. Gets the job done for mild trail use and tight budget. Powder coat quality is average \u2014 plan to touch up paint where it chips after the first trail contact.\n\n**Poison Spyder Rocker Knockers ($699):** The benchmark mid-tier option. Frame-mounted, 3/16\" steel, available in bare steel for welding or with powder coat. Well-fitted to the JT with a clean installation process. Correct for moderate to serious trail use.\n\n**GenRight Rock Sliders ($899):** Heavier construction with a step feature that doubles as a boarding step. GenRight's tube construction is excellent \u2014 round DOM tube that actually slides over rocks rather than grabbing. The step feature is functional for tall door openings on a lifted JT.\n\n**Rock-Slide Engineering Step-Sliders ($1,099):** The most versatile option \u2014 combines serious rock protection with a functional step that extends and retracts. The retraction mechanism is relevant for the JT's wide stance: when the step is extended, it protrudes past the body line and can catch obstacles. When retracted, the profile is much cleaner. Correct for dual-use builds that spend time both on-trail and in parking structures.\n\n**Fab Fours Premium ($799):** Clean fitment, good powder coat, strong frame mounting. Competitive with GenRight at a slightly lower price. Less depth to the step feature.\n\nInstallation varies by slider design, but the general sequence for frame-mounted JT sliders:\n\n1. Jack the vehicle and support on stands at a secure frame point aft of the rocker area.\n2. Remove the factory step bars if equipped (Rubicon running boards unclip from factory mounting brackets).\n3. Identify the frame mounting holes along the inner rocker area. Most JT sliders use existing holes in the frame rail \u2014 some require drilling. If drilling is required, use a center punch to mark the location precisely before drilling.\n4. Thread the slider mounting brackets to the frame rail (hand-tight first).\n5. Position the slider body against the rocker area. Check alignment \u2014 the slider should sit flush to or slightly below the rocker panel sheet metal.\n6. Torque all mounting bolts to spec per the manufacturer's instructions: typically 35\u201355 ft-lbs depending on bolt size.\n7. Check for any clearance issues with the door sweep or rocker panel trim piece. Trim if needed.\n\n- **Rocker panel trim**: Most JT sliders require removing a plastic rocker cover or trim piece. Some require trimming the plastic \u2014 don't cut more than necessary.\n- **Electrical and brake line routing**: On the JT, wiring runs along the frame rail in the rocker area. Verify that slider brackets don't pinch wiring harnesses. Route any displaced wiring with zip ties to the inner frame before final torque.\n- **Ground clearance impact**: A slider that hangs below the frame rail creates a new contact point. Measure the finished height of the slider bottom vs. frame bottom before ordering \u2014 a slider that sits lower than the frame reduces effective ground clearance.\n\nSmittybilt entry-level: ~$399. Adequate protection, budget install. Skip if the JT will see serious rocky terrain.\n\nPoison Spyder Rocker Knockers: ~$699. The honest value for moderate to hard trail use.\n\nGenRight or Fab Fours: ~$799\u2013$899. Step up in build quality and hardware. Recommended for JTs that see frequent rocky terrain.\n\nRock-Slide Engineering Step-Sliders: ~$1,099. Best overall for a dual-use JT that prioritizes both trail protection and usable boarding steps."},{"id":"jt-skid-plate","title":"Skid Plate Upgrade \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"armor","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2.5},"cost_usd":{"low":199,"high":899},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set (metric)","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","working under a lifted vehicle"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["armor_skid","armor","skid-plate","underbody-protection"],"parts":[{"name":"Rough Country Heavy Duty Skid Plate System \u2014 JT Gladiator","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":299},{"name":"Teraflex JT Transfer Case and Transmission Skid Plate","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":419},{"name":"Synergy Manufacturing JT Full Underbody Skid Package","vendor":"Synergy Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":799},{"name":"RCI Metalworks JT Engine and Transmission Skid","vendor":"RCI Metalworks","price_estimate_usd":549},{"name":"Mopar OEM Bash Plates \u2014 JT Gladiator (Rubicon)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":399}],"citations":[{"title":"Teraflex JT Skid Plate Product Page","url":"https://www.teraflex.com"},{"title":"RCI Metalworks JT Underbody Skid System","url":"https://www.rcimetalworks.com"},{"title":"Synergy Manufacturing JT Skid Plate Overview","url":"https://www.synergymfg.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-front-bumper","jt-rocker-sliders"],"summary":"The JT Gladiator's factory skid plate coverage depends on trim \u2014 the Rubicon gets bash plates on the front axle and transfer case; Sport and Willys trims leave the oil pan, transmission, and fuel tank unprotected. A skid plate system closes the gap between the lowest vehicle component and the rock.","description":"The JT's long wheelbase creates a longer skid plate challenge than on the JL Wrangler \u2014 there's more underbody to protect between the front and rear axles. Budget accordingly: a complete underbody skid system for the JT runs significantly more than a basic skid plate set.\n\n**What's exposed on a stock JT**\n\nOn a Sport or Willys trim:\n- **Oil pan**: Exposed and vulnerable. The 3.6L's aluminum oil pan cracks on a direct rock strike.\n- **Transmission pan**: Exposed. Factory has minimal protection.\n- **Transfer case**: Exposed (Rubicon has a bash plate; Sport and Willys do not).\n- **Fuel tank**: The JT's fuel tank sits forward of the rear axle, partially inside the frame rails. The EVAP canister is exposed below the tank on the passenger side.\n\nOn the Rubicon:\n- Front axle and transfer case have factory bash plates\n- Oil pan and transmission remain unprotected from the factory\n\n**Skid plate priority order for trail use**\n\n1. **Oil pan skid** \u2014 critical. An oil pan hit that punctures the pan means engine damage. This is the first skid to install.\n2. **Transmission skid** \u2014 important for rocky terrain. The 8HP75 transmission pan is aluminum.\n3. **Transfer case skid** \u2014 already on the Rubicon; essential addition for Sport/Willys.\n4. **Fuel tank skid** \u2014 matters for terrain where the rear undercarriage contacts rocks.\n\n**Material: steel vs. aluminum**\n\nMost skid plates are 3/16\" or 1/4\" mild steel or 1/4\"\u20133/8\" aluminum. Steel is denser and absorbs impact energy better for a given thickness; aluminum is significantly lighter. For rock crawling where repeated sliding contact is expected, steel is the preference. For overland/trail use where incidental contact is the scenario, aluminum skids of sufficient thickness are adequate and keep weight down.\n\n**Skid options for the JT**\n\n**Rough Country 3-piece skid package ($299):** Entry-level steel skid system covering the oil pan, transmission, and transfer case. Powder-coated, straightforward install. Correct for a budget-conscious Sport or Willys JT going on moderate trails.\n\n**Teraflex transmission/transfer case skid ($419):** 1/4\" steel, focused on the mid-vehicle components. Quality hardware and a proper mount that keeps the skid from rattling.\n\n**RCI Metalworks engine + transmission skid ($549):** 3/16\" steel, laser-cut, with proper frame-mount hardware. RCI's fit quality on the JT is excellent \u2014 the skid sits flush to the frame rails rather than hanging below them, preserving ground clearance.\n\n**Synergy Manufacturing full system ($799):** Comprehensive underbody coverage \u2014 engine, transmission, transfer case, and optional fuel tank skid. The correct package for a JT built for serious rock crawling where belly scraping is expected rather than incidental.\n\n**Mopar OEM Rubicon bash plates ($399):** If you're on a Rubicon and want OEM fitment quality for the factory-style coverage, the Mopar kit is a clean option. Does not cover the oil pan.\n\n1. Lift and support the vehicle on jack stands with adequate clearance for skid work.\n2. Identify the mounting points for each skid. Most JT skids use existing frame holes with supplied Grade 8 hardware \u2014 no drilling.\n3. Install from front to rear. The engine/oil pan skid typically installs first and may interface with the transmission skid mounting. Install sequence matters.\n4. With a helper, position each skid plate at the frame mounting points. Thread bolts hand-tight before torquing.\n5. Torque to spec: typically 40\u201355 ft-lbs for 3/8\" frame hardware.\n6. Verify that each skid plate doesn't contact drivetrain components at full suspension flex. Pull the vehicle to maximum droop with a jack and confirm clearance.\n\n- **Ground clearance**: A skid plate that hangs below the frame rail becomes a contact point itself. Measure before and after \u2014 the skid should not be lower than the frame rails in the protected area.\n- **Oil and drain access**: Confirm that the skid plate allows oil drain access with a removable panel or sufficient clearance for a wrench. The Rough Country design requires full skid removal for oil changes on some JT years \u2014 a significant nuisance on a truck you're servicing every 5,000 miles.\n- **Fitment with other armor**: If installing bumpers and sliders simultaneously, check that skid plate ends don't conflict with bumper mounting brackets at the forward and rear transitions.\n\nRough Country 3-piece set: ~$299. Correct entry-level coverage for moderate trail use.\n\nTeraflex or RCI individual skids: ~$400\u2013550. Better build quality and fit, suitable for harder use.\n\nSynergy full system: ~$799. The correct investment for a dedicated trail or rock-crawling JT.\n\nShop install for skid plate system: ~$150\u2013250 in labor. The job itself is accessible \u2014 the difficulty is working under the vehicle and managing the weight of steel skids in position. DIY is realistic if you have jack stands and a helper."},{"id":"jt-skid-plates","title":"Skid Plate Kit for the Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"armor","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":399,"high":799},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","under-vehicle access"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["armor","skid-plate","protection","underbody"],"parts":[{"name":"Teraflex JT Skid Plate Kit","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Rock Hard 4x4 JT Gladiator Skid Plate System","vendor":"Rock Hard 4x4","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"Rubicon Express JT Skid Plate Kit","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":449}],"citations":[{"title":"Teraflex Gladiator JT Skid Plate Install","url":"https://www.teraflex.com/products/gladiator-skid-plates"},{"title":"Rock Hard 4x4 JT Skid System Overview","url":"https://www.rockhard4x4.com/jeep-gladiator-jt-skid-plates"}],"alternatives":["jt-sliders","jt-front-bumper"],"summary":"The Gladiator JT's longer 137\" wheelbase means more underbody exposure per trail obstacle \u2014 skid plates that fit the JL won't cover the full length of the JT, and buying a JL set is money wasted.","description":"Stock JT underbody protection is minimal outside of a factory transfer case skid on Rubicon trims. The oil pan, transmission, fuel tank, and the stretch of unprotected frame between those components are all exposed to rock strikes on any trail with ledges or embedded rock. For a truck with the JT's capability envelope \u2014 and the market it attracts, which skews toward owners who actually use the truck \u2014 running without skids is a one-trail mistake.\n\nThe coverage priority on the JT is: (1) engine/oil pan, (2) transmission, (3) transfer case, (4) fuel tank. A kit that addresses all four gives you full protection from bumper to bumper on the underside. The JT's longer wheelbase means the fuel tank sits further back than on the JL, so JT-specific skids extend coverage rearward in a way JL skids can't. Teraflex's JT kit ($499) covers the first three in one purchase; the Rock Hard 4x4 system ($699) extends full coverage to the fuel tank and adds heavier plate gauge (3/16\" vs. 1/8\") for a rock-crawling-focused build. Rubicon Express's $449 kit is the value option and covers the essential surfaces without the upgrade-gauge material.\n\nPlate gauge matters when you're shopping. 1/8\" (11 gauge) steel is adequate for trail skimming and ledge glances; 3/16\" is the right call for sustained rock contact in a crawling context. Aluminum skid plates exist in this price category too \u2014 they're significantly lighter but dent rather than deflect on hard hits, which can transfer impact energy to the component they're protecting. Steel is the correct choice for JTs going on rock.\n\nFull-length skid coverage changes your maintenance routine, and it's better to plan for it than discover it at the next oil change. Most JT skid systems put a removable panel over the oil pan and drain plug \u2014 confirm yours does before you buy, and note which bolts come out for an oil change (usually four to six). Bag and label the hardware the first time so re-install goes quickly.\n\nThe transmission and transfer case fill/drain plugs sit behind a fixed panel on some kits; if you service your own drivetrain fluids, choose a system with access ports or removable sections at those points. Rock Hard's system and Teraflex's kit both leave the common service points reachable \u2014 verify against your specific fluid-change routine.\n\nBury the mounting hardware flush or recessed. Bolt heads that protrude below the plate catch on rock and shear off \u2014 a skid held by half its bolts flexes into the component it protects. Use the countersunk hardware the kit supplies.\n\nSteel plates and an aluminum crossmember create a galvanic-corrosion couple in wet or salted conditions. In a rust-belt climate, use anti-seize on the fasteners and check the mounting points annually. Desert JTs don't face this, but the plates still trap trail mud against the frame \u2014 rinse the underbody after muddy runs so packed clay doesn't hold moisture against bare steel.\n\nSkids are the underbody half of a protection package; [rock sliders](/db/gladiator/jt-sliders/) cover the rockers and [a front bumper](/db/gladiator/jt-front-bumper/) protects the nose. If you run [larger tires](/db/gladiator/jt-35-inch-tire-fitment/) and wheel aggressively, prioritize the heavier 3/16\" plate gauge discussed above.","steps":["Raise the vehicle and support on jack stands under the frame at all four corners \u2014 you need clearance to work under the full length of the truck.","Locate the factory mounting points on the frame per the kit instructions. Most JT skid systems use existing threaded frame holes; some require supplied nut plates slid into the frame channel.","Start with the forward engine/transmission skid: position the plate, insert all bolts hand-tight, then torque in a cross pattern.","Install the transfer case skid next if supplied separately \u2014 overlap the rearmost edge of the transmission skid for continuous coverage.","Install the fuel tank skid last \u2014 verify it doesn't contact the fuel tank directly at any point before tightening.","Inspect all mounting bolts after first trail use and re-torque as needed."],"pros":["Protects the most vulnerable underbody components from rock strikes, ledges, and road debris","JT-specific fitment covers the full wheelbase length \u2014 no coverage gaps","Steel construction deflects impacts rather than transmitting them to the component","Bolt-on install accessible to any home mechanic with basic tools"],"cons":["JL skid plates will not cover the JT's full length \u2014 verify JT fitment before ordering","Adds 30\u201370 lbs of underbody weight depending on gauge and coverage","Access to oil drain plug and transmission service points may require removing a skid panel \u2014 note this before your first oil change","Quality steel plates at this price tier have basic powder coat that chips; touch up annually"]},{"id":"jt-sliders","title":"Rock Sliders for the Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":449,"high":799},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","drill","wire brush"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","drilling into body/frame"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["armor","sliders","protection","rock-rail"],"parts":[{"name":"Poison Spyder Rock Sliders for Gladiator JT","vendor":"Poison Spyder Customs","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"Rock Hard 4x4 JT Gladiator Rock Rails","vendor":"Rock Hard 4x4","price_estimate_usd":449},{"name":"LOD Destroyer JT Rock Sliders","vendor":"LOD Offroad","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"Smittybilt XRC Rock Sliders for JT","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":499}],"citations":[{"title":"Poison Spyder JT Rock Slider Install Guide","url":"https://www.poisonspyder.com/gladiator-jt-sliders"},{"title":"Rock Hard 4x4 JT Gladiator Rock Rail Product Page","url":"https://www.rockhard4x4.com/jeep-gladiator-jt"}],"alternatives":["jt-front-bumper","jt-skid-plates"],"summary":"Rock sliders protect the JT's rocker panels when the trail gets narrow and you're trading paint with the terrain \u2014 and the Gladiator's longer wheelbase means those rockers are exposed across more of the truck's length than on a Wrangler.","description":"The JT Gladiator is 19\" longer than the JL Unlimited, and that extra length shows up in the rocker panel exposure on the trail. A JL slider won't cover a JT's rockers \u2014 this is not a cross-compatible part. When you're sourcing sliders for the JT, verify the listing says JT explicitly and check the coverage dimensions. Quality JT sliders should run the full length of the cab door openings, with enough tube diameter to take side contact without deforming into the rocker.\n\nThe core decision is weld-on vs. bolt-on. Weld-on sliders (like the Poison Spyder JT at $699 and the LOD Destroyer at $699) mount directly to the frame with stronger, lower-profile attachment \u2014 they don't depend on bolt holes in factory pinch-weld flanges. They're the right choice for a dedicated trail truck. Bolt-on sliders (Rock Hard 4x4 at $449, Smittybilt XRC at $499) use existing frame holes or require drilling into the body/frame at specific points. The install is accessible without a welder, but bolt-on designs have more flex under hard lateral contact. That doesn't mean they fail \u2014 the Rock Hard 4x4 units are well-regarded bolt-on sliders that have taken real abuse \u2014 but weld-on is structurally superior when you're doing legitimate rock work.\n\nSmittybilt's XRC JT sliders at $499 are the budget-to-quality inflection point: bolt-on install, step functionality, and enough steel tube to handle moderate trail abuse. If you're building an overland-focused JT that occasionally sees rocky trails rather than a dedicated rock crawler, the Smittybilt units are honest value. Poison Spyder and LOD are the right call for a wheeling-first build where the sliders will get used hard.\n\nThe most common JT slider install mistake is mounting too low in pursuit of a beefier step. A slider that hangs below the frame rail becomes the truck's lowest point and turns every driveway and ledge into a hang-up. Mount the slider so its bottom edge sits at or slightly above the frame rail, not below it.\n\nOn bolt-on kits, the factory pinch-weld flange is not a structural mounting surface \u2014 it's sheet metal. Kits that clamp only to the pinch weld will crush it under a hard side hit and transfer the load into the rocker you were trying to protect. Verify the kit mounts to the frame rail itself (through-bolted or with frame-clamp brackets), not only the body seam.\n\nFor weld-on installs, grind the frame to bare metal at every weld location, tack in three or four spots, then hang your full weight on the slider before laying the final beads \u2014 a slider that shifts after welding is scrap. Keep a fire extinguisher and a wet rag ready: the fuel and brake lines run near the frame on the JT, and a stray spark or heat soak on a line is a real hazard. If you're not confident MIG-welding structural steel, this is a job for a fab shop.\n\nAfter the first hard trail day, re-check every fastener. Bolt-on sliders back their hardware out under repeated impact \u2014 a slider held by two of four bolts is a liability.\n\nPair sliders with a full [skid plate kit](/db/gladiator/jt-skid-plates/) for continuous underbody-to-rocker protection, and a [front bumper](/db/gladiator/jt-front-bumper/) if you're staging a complete armor package. Owners running [larger tires](/db/gladiator/jt-35-inch-tire-fitment/) load the sliders hardest during flex \u2014 mount to the frame accordingly.","steps":["Clean and degrease the frame rail area along the rocker panels where sliders will mount.","For bolt-on kits: locate the factory mounting points per kit instructions; drill any additional holes required. Use a center punch to prevent drill drift.","For weld-on kits: position sliders using clamps and verify fitment before any welding. Tack-weld first, check alignment, then complete the weld.","Apply the supplied hardware (bolt-on kits): hand-thread all bolts before torquing any, then torque to spec in a cross pattern.","Inspect the slider length against the door sill \u2014 sliders should extend from the front door hinge area to at least the rear door hinge on the driver and passenger sides.","Coat any raw or bare metal areas with rattle-can flat black or bedliner to prevent rust."],"pros":["Protects rocker panels from the most common trail damage source: rock contact on side slopes","Doubles as a step for cab entry on lifted JTs","JT-specific fitment covers the full longer wheelbase cab","Weld-on options are among the strongest armor dollar-per-dollar available"],"cons":["JL sliders will not fit \u2014 verify JT fitment before ordering","Weld-on install requires a welder or a fabrication shop; adds $150\u2013$300 to install cost","Adds 30\u201360 lbs per side \u2014 minor but worth noting on a truck you're weight-managing","Powder coat chips on rocks; touch-up paint or bedliner recoat is ongoing maintenance"]},{"id":"jt-dana44-differential-fluid-change","title":"Dana 44 Differential Fluid Change \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"axles","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":80},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","3/8\" drive ratchet","drain pan","funnel with flexible hose","rags","wire brush","RTV silicone or replacement gasket"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","safe vehicle support"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["axles","maintenance","differential","dana44","fluid"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar Gear & Axle Lubricant 75W-140 (1 qt)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Red Line 75W-140 NS GL-5 Gear Oil (1 qt)","vendor":"Red Line","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"Amsoil Severe Gear 75W-140 (1 qt)","vendor":"Amsoil","price_estimate_usd":20},{"name":"Loctite 598 Ultra Grey RTV Silicone","vendor":"Loctite","price_estimate_usd":12}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator JT Owner's Manual \u2014 Fluid Specifications","url":"https://www.jeep.com/content/dam/fca-brands/na/jeep/en_us/2022/owner-manuals/2022-jeep-gladiator-owners-manual.pdf"},{"title":"Dana 44 Service Manual \u2014 Differential Lubrication","url":"https://www.dana.com/en-us/segments/off-highway/"}],"alternatives":["jt-dana44-rear-locker","jt-rear-locker-trail-use"],"summary":"The Gladiator JT runs Dana 44 axles front and rear from the factory \u2014 the same robust architecture Jeep has used in Wranglers for decades, with the same fluid change intervals that most owners run past due.","description":"Differential fluid breaks down from heat, water intrusion, and metal wear particles suspended in the oil. On a trail rig that's done creek crossings or driven through standing water, that interval shortens significantly because water can enter through axle tube vents. Factory spec calls for a fluid change at 15,000 miles or when the vehicle has been submerged past the axle housings \u2014 whichever comes first. Most experienced Gladiator owners drain both diffs at 10,000 miles or annually, especially if the rig sees regular mud or water.\n\nThe JT's front axle also houses the front locking differential on Rubicon trims. The fluid change procedure is identical \u2014 Eaton's factory locker is a helical-gear design that runs in gear oil and doesn't require special handling during the drain/fill.\n\n**What fluid:** The factory spec is 75W-140 synthetic gear oil with a limited-slip additive. The Rubicon's Tru-Lok lockers and Eaton helical unit are compatible with this spec. Using straight 75W-90 without the LS additive can cause the limited-slip functionality to chatter. If you see \"GL-5\" on the label and the spec says 75W-140, you're in the right range \u2014 verify it explicitly includes friction modifier or add it separately.\n\n- **Stripped fill plugs**: The fill plug threads into cast iron and can corrode or cross-thread. If it turns but won't tighten, the threads are gone \u2014 the cover needs replacement or a helicoil insert.\n- **Water contamination**: Milky or grey fluid means water has been in the diff. One fluid change may not clean it fully. A second change at 500 miles after the first is good practice when water contamination is found.\n- **Over-RTV**: A thick bead of RTV can squeeze inward and form debris in the diff oil. Thin bead, even application.\n- **Rear locker vent**: Make sure the axle vent line is clear and unobstructed after the change. A clogged vent is how water gets in during creek crossings.\n\nMopar factory fluid (75W-140): ~$16\u201320/qt. Two quarts covers both axles with some margin. This is the spec-correct choice if you want to avoid any compatibility guesswork.\n\nRed Line or Amsoil 75W-140: ~$18\u201324/qt. Equivalent quality, better availability at most auto parts stores.\n\nRTV silicone (Loctite 598 or Permatex Ultra Grey): ~$8\u201312. One tube does several gasket applications.\n\nShop rate for a differential fluid change is typically $80\u2013150 per axle, plus fluid markup. Both axles DIY comes in well under $60 with quality fluid.","steps":["Park on a level surface. Let the axle cool for at least 30 minutes after driving \u2014 drain temperature should be warm, not scalding.","Support the vehicle on jack stands. You don't need to get the axle high, enough to work under it safely. Never rely on a floor jack alone.","Place your drain pan under the front Dana 44. Locate the fill plug (passenger side of the differential housing, roughly 4 o'clock position) and loosen it first \u2014 this prevents the situation where you drain the fluid and then can't break the fill plug. If you can't loosen the fill plug, stop and figure it out before draining.","Remove the drain plug or, on axles without a dedicated drain, remove the diff cover. The JT front Dana 44 does not have a threaded drain plug \u2014 you'll remove the cover using the 10 bolts around its perimeter (10mm or 3/8\" drive socket).","Allow the old fluid to drain completely. The outgoing fluid tells you something: dark metallic flakes suggest accelerated wear; milky grey oil means water intrusion happened; dark brown is normal for used gear oil. Document it with your phone camera for reference.","Inspect the cover gasket. The factory uses an RTV gasket on most JT diff covers. Clean both mating surfaces with a wire brush and solvent \u2014 no old gasket material, no debris. Apply a thin, even bead of Ultra Grey RTV (about 1/8\" diameter) to the cleaned cover face.","Reinstall the cover and torque the bolts to 30 ft-lbs in a cross pattern. Don't overtighten \u2014 the cover is thin aluminum on some trims and the threads strip at torques above 35 ft-lbs.","Let the RTV cure for 15\u201320 minutes before filling.","Fill the differential through the fill plug hole with 75W-140 synthetic. The correct fill level is \"at or slightly below the fill hole\" \u2014 when you insert a finger and feel fluid, you're at the right level. The JT front Dana 44 holds approximately 2.2 pints. The rear Dana 44 holds approximately 3.5 pints.","Reinstall the fill plug. Torque to 25 ft-lbs. Wipe the housing clean.","Repeat on the rear axle \u2014 same procedure, same fluid spec, same torque values.","Drive for 15\u201320 minutes, then park and inspect both covers for leaks."]},{"id":"jt-dana44-rear-locker","title":"Rear Locker Options for the Gladiator JT Dana 44","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"axles","type":"upgrade","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":499,"high":1800},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","ring gear spreader","bearing press","dial indicator","torque wrench","socket set (metric and SAE)","axle seal driver","RTV sealant"],"skills_required":["differential work","bearing press operation","torque-to-spec discipline"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["locker","axles","traction","dana44","differential"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB Air Locker for Jeep Gladiator JT Rear Dana 44","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":849},{"name":"Eaton ELocker for JT Rear Dana 44","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":749},{"name":"Detroit Locker for Dana 44 (compatible)","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Yukon Gear Zip Locker for Dana 44","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":599}],"citations":[{"title":"ARB Air Locker JT Gladiator Fitment Guide","url":"https://www.arbusa.com"},{"title":"Eaton ELocker Gladiator Application Guide","url":"https://www.eaton.com/locker"},{"title":"Dana 44 Differential Service Guide \u2014 Allpar","url":"https://www.allpar.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-dana44-differential-fluid-change","jt-locker-rear","jt-regear","jt-differential-fluid-change"],"summary":"A rear locker transforms how the JT handles technical terrain. The Rubicon already has this solved from the factory \u2014 Tru-Lok electronic lockers front and rear. This guide is for everyone else: Sport and Willys trims get open differentials (or the optional Trac-Lok anti-spin, which helps in gravel and gives up in rocks), and the Mojave gets a rear Trac-Lok. If your JT spins one tire while the other sits still, a locker fixes that.","description":"The Gladiator JT's rear axle is badged Dana 44 on all trims and shares its architecture with the JL Wrangler rear, which means aftermarket locker support is solid \u2014 as long as you order for the right axle (see the compatibility warning below). What differentiates the JT is the rear leaf spring suspension and the longer wheelbase, which changes the effective traction demand on the rear axle during articulation. When the JT's rear axle droops significantly on uneven terrain, an open diff sends power to the wheel in the air. A locker sends it equally to both \u2014 the wheel with grip does the work.\n\nAn **automatic locker** (Detroit Locker, True-Trac variant) locks mechanically under torque and unlocks during cornering. It's always active, requires no driver input, and costs the least. The trade-off: on-road behavior is altered. The rear end \"bumps\" slightly in tight turns as the locker unlocks and relocks. Fine for rigs that primarily trail-drive; noticeable in street daily drivers.\n\nA **selectable locker** (ARB Air Locker, Eaton ELocker, Yukon Zip Locker) locks on demand via a driver-controlled switch. In unlocked mode, the differential behaves normally \u2014 open (Sport/Willys) or limited-slip (Rubicon). Engage it when you need it. This is the right choice for a JT that divides time between street driving and trail use.\n\n**ARB Air Locker** ($849): The industry benchmark for selectable lockers. Pneumatically actuated via an air compressor (the ARB twin-motor compressor, ~$350 separately). Rock-solid engagement, well-documented install procedure, broad JT fitment support. Requires routing an air line from the compressor to the axle \u2014 adds installation complexity but the reliability record is exceptional. If you're installing a front locker too, one ARB compressor handles both.\n\n**Eaton ELocker** ($749): Electrically actuated \u2014 a 12V solenoid engages the locker on demand, no compressor required. Wiring is more straightforward than ARB pneumatic plumbing. The engagement is slightly slower than the ARB (takes a half-second to lock fully), which is irrelevant in real use. A strong choice for JT owners who want selectable without dealing with air line routing.\n\n**Automatic lockers (Detroit-style)** (~$500 where available): Always engaged under power, no electronics, lowest cost, highest traction output. Two caveats for the JT: on-road manners suffer in a daily driver, and most legacy \"Dana 44\" automatic lockers are catalogued for the old 216mm axle, not the M220 \u2014 confirm a current JL/JT M220 application with the manufacturer before ordering. The right call only for a dedicated trail rig.\n\nDifferential locker installation involves removing the rear axle from the vehicle, disassembling the differential carrier, pressing out the old carrier bearings, installing the locker assembly into the carrier, and reassembling with careful attention to ring gear backlash and bearing preload. This is a precision job \u2014 bearing preload measured in inch-lbs and backlash measured in thousandths of an inch. The cost of getting it wrong is a destroyed ring-and-pinion.\n\nFor most JT owners, the right call is finding a drivetrain shop familiar with Dana 44 work and paying the labor. The install itself takes an experienced mechanic 4\u20135 hours. A first-timer without a ring gear spreader and dial indicator adds significant risk and time.\n\n**If you're doing it yourself:**\n1. Drain differential fluid and remove the cover.\n2. Mark the caps before removal \u2014 they're machined as a matched set to the housing.\n3. Use a ring gear spreader to spread the housing enough to remove the carrier assembly. Forcing it without a spreader damages the housing.\n4. Press out the carrier side bearings using a bearing press \u2014 not a C-clamp, not hammer and drift.\n5. Install the locker assembly per the manufacturer's instructions. ARB and Eaton both include detailed installation guides.\n6. Set ring gear backlash (spec for Dana 44: 0.005\"\u20130.008\"). Use shims to adjust.\n7. Set carrier bearing preload \u2014 typically 25\u201330 in-lbs rotating torque over the rolling friction.\n8. Reinstall caps to original orientation and torque to spec (60\u201380 ft-lbs depending on year).\n9. Reinstall the axle, refill with differential fluid (75W-140 synthetic for a locker, not 80W-90), and test engagement in a safe area before hitting the trail.\n\nThe JT's rear \"Dana 44\" is the AdvanTEK M220 \u2014 220mm ring gear, its own carrier dimensions, bearings, and spline count. A locker, carrier, or ring-and-pinion set catalogued for a TJ/JK-era Dana 44 (216mm) will not go into this axle. When ordering, the listing must say Jeep Gladiator JT, JL Wrangler rear, or M220 explicitly \u2014 \"fits Dana 44\" alone is how people end up with an expensive paperweight and a restocking fee. ARB and Eaton both catalog JT-specific rear applications; verify the part number against your build year before paying. Ratio matters too: if you are re-gearing for 35s or 37s (see the JT re-gear guide), do the locker in the same teardown \u2014 the labor overlaps almost completely, and paying for the diff setup twice is the most expensive way to build an axle.\n\n- **Engage stopped or crawling, never spinning.** Lockers are designed to engage with near-zero wheel speed difference. Flipping the switch while one wheel spins slams the engagement dogs together \u2014 that is how teeth chip.\n- **Unlock for pavement.** A locked rear axle on asphalt binds in every turn, scrubs the rear tires, and loads the axle shafts for no benefit.\n- **Air system care (ARB):** Run the compressor monthly even in winter \u2014 the seals live longer exercised. A slow leak-down when locked usually means the seal housing O-rings, a known consumable (~$30 kit) after years of service.\n- **Fluid:** After the first 500 miles post-install, change the gear oil to flush assembly wear-in metal, then follow the normal interval in the JT differential fluid guide. Selectable lockers run standard 75W-140 synthetic with no friction modifier \u2014 the modifier is for clutch-type limited slips, and adding it to a locker axle does nothing useful.\n\nAutomatic locker (no electronics): ~$500 parts-only where a current M220 application exists \u2014 confirm before ordering; add shop labor if not self-installing.\n\nEaton ELocker: ~$749. Add wiring harness and switch (~$50 separately) and a few hours of labor for the wiring run.\n\nARB Air Locker: ~$849 for the locker + ~$350 for the twin motor compressor. All-in cost including install labor runs $1,400\u20131,800 at a reputable shop.\n\nShop install labor (any locker type): $400\u2013600. Differential work is the one area where cutting corners costs more in the long run."},{"id":"jt-rear-locker-trail-use","title":"Trail-using the Rubicon's rear locker only","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"axles","subcategory":"lockers","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.0,"high":0.0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":["4LO transfer case position","rear locker switch (center dash)","traction/ESC off button"],"skills_required":["trail driving awareness","locker operation"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2020-present"],"tags":["locker","rear locker","front locker","rubicon","trail driving","AdaptTrac","electronic locker"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator Rubicon owner's manual \u2014 locker operation","url":"https://www.jeep.com/gladiator"}],"alternatives":["jt-dana44-rear-locker","jt-locker-rear"],"summary":"Engaging only the rear locker is often the right call on trail \u2014 it provides the traction benefit with more steering ability than running front and rear locked together. Knowing when to use what is more valuable than knowing how to engage everything at once.","description":"The Rubicon runs electronic lockers in both the front Dana 44 and the rear Dana 44. These are selectable differential locks \u2014 when engaged, both wheels on that axle are driven at the same speed regardless of traction conditions. The lockers can be engaged independently via the instrument cluster controls.\n\nThe system operates in 4L (low range) by default for full locker engagement. The rear locker can be engaged in 4H on some terrain conditions.\n\n**Moderate trail with steering demands:** When you need traction but also need to steer precisely \u2014 navigating around obstacles, threading a line through loose rock \u2014 rear-only lock provides drive traction through the rear axle while leaving the front axle free to respond to steering inputs. Locking the front as well reduces steering feel and creates understeer on tight turns.\n\n**Mud and loose terrain:** In deep mud or loose dirt, the rear locker keeps the rear wheels driving together without spinning down. The front axle in open-diff mode can still find traction and contribute, while the rear pushes. This is often enough to get through without front lock.\n\n**Highway-adjacent situations:** If you find yourself in a difficult low-speed situation on or near pavement (a steep gravel driveway, a muddy turnaround), rear-only lock in 4H can provide the traction you need without forcing the front diff to fight the drivetrain binding that full lock creates on hard surfaces.\n\nAdd the front locker when rear-only isn't enough \u2014 typically:\n\n- **Full-on rock crawling:** Both wheels on both axles need to drive together when you're on slick rock or technical terrain where one wheel can easily lift\n- **Deep water crossings:** Maximum drive traction ensures you push through without getting stuck mid-crossing\n- **Severely uneven terrain:** When you're at the limit of articulation and one front wheel is off the ground\n\nWith both lockers engaged, the steering fights back. The truck wants to go straight; turning requires more input and creates axle binding on tight turns. On slick rock this binding is managed by terrain flex. On hard-pack or pavement, binding is uncomfortable and puts stress on the drivetrain.\n\nDrive full-locked at low speed, on terrain that justifies it, and unlock before returning to any hard surface.\n\nFor reference on trail approach:\n\n| Axle Setup | Description | Trail Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Open differential | Torque goes to the wheel with least resistance \u2014 worst case, one spinning wheel | Fine for mild trail |\n| Limited-slip (LSD/Trac-Lok) | Biases torque toward the wheel with more traction \u2014 better than open | Good for moderate terrain |\n| Locked differential | Both wheels driven at equal speed regardless of traction | Maximum traction, reduced steering |\n\nThe Rubicon's electronic lockers are true lockers \u2014 not limited-slip. Engage them for terrain that demands it, not as a default trail setting.\n\nBefore you reach for a locker, understand what the Gladiator already does automatically. Every JT runs brake-lock differential (BLD) traction control \u2014 the ABS system pulses the brake on a spinning wheel to send torque across an open diff to the wheel with grip. On mild trail, BLD alone handles a lot: a single lifted tire on a rutted climb often gets sorted by the electronics before you'd ever think to lock.\n\nWhere BLD runs out is sustained, high-load slip. It's reactive and it works through the brakes, so it heats up and fades on a long, slow, one-wheel-spinning crawl. A mechanical or electronic locker doesn't fade \u2014 it physically ties the axle shafts together and holds. That's the dividing line: BLD for intermittent traction loss, a locker for terrain where you'll be putting steady torque through a lifted or low-grip wheel for more than a few seconds.\n\nOne more interaction worth knowing: engaging the rear locker on the Rubicon automatically relaxes some of the stability-control intervention, because the truck no longer needs to brake a \"spinning\" rear wheel that's now locked to its partner. That's by design \u2014 let the locker do the mechanical work and stop fighting the brakes. If you're adding a locker to a non-Rubicon trim, see the full install and hardware options in [Rear Locker for the Gladiator JT \u2014 Dana 44 Options](/db/gladiator/jt-locker-rear/).\n\n- **Unlock before pavement, every time.** Driving a locked axle onto hard pavement forces both tires to turn at the same speed through a corner when they physically need to travel different distances. The result is tire scrub, driveline wind-up, and \u2014 repeated enough \u2014 accelerated wear on axle shafts and the ring-and-pinion. Make unlocking part of your \"leaving the trail\" checklist alongside airing up.\n- **Throttle discipline on a locked rear.** The JT's factory leaf-sprung rear is prone to axle wrap under hard throttle, and a locked rear multiplies the twisting load because both tires bite at once. Roll into the throttle rather than stabbing it. If you feel a hop or a shudder on a locked launch, that's wrap \u2014 see [Factory leaf spring axle wrap on the JT](/db/gladiator/factory-leaf-spring-axle-wrap/).\n- **4L for full engagement.** The front locker engages only in 4L on the Rubicon. If you try to lock the front in 4H and nothing happens, that's expected behavior, not a fault.\n- **Confirm the indicator, not the switch.** Locker engagement isn't instantaneous \u2014 the dogs sometimes need a few inches of wheel rotation to line up and drop in. Wait for the solid indicator light before assuming you're locked; a blinking light means it's still trying to engage.","steps":["Select 4L for technical terrain (ensures proper engagement range)","Engage rear locker via the instrument cluster control \u2014 wait for the indicator light to confirm lock","Drive the section \u2014 assess whether rear-only is sufficient","If more traction is needed, engage front locker as well","After clearing the difficult section, disengage front locker first, then rear if appropriate","Return to 4H or 2H before returning to any hard surface or higher-speed driving"],"pros":["Rear-only lock is a useful middle-ground state \u2014 more traction than open, better steering than full-locked","Electronic engagement is fast and reliable","Independent front/rear control lets you match the locker setup to the terrain demand"],"cons":["Driving locked on pavement or hard surfaces creates drivetrain binding and wear \u2014 unlock before returning to road","Rear locker on high-traction surfaces with hard acceleration can cause axle wrap \u2014 be smooth with the throttle","Some owners lock everything by default and miss the value of rear-only for moderate terrain"]},{"id":"frame-bend-towing-recall","title":"Gladiator frame bend recall and tongue weight limits","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"body","subcategory":"frame integrity","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.0,"high":2.0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":["flashlight","straightedge or level (for visual inspection)"],"skills_required":["visual inspection"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2022"],"tags":["frame","recall","towing","tongue weight","NHTSA","stellantis","structural"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"NHTSA Recall 21V-127 \u2014 Gladiator Frame","url":"https://www.nhtsa.gov/recalls"},{"title":"Stellantis Service Action \u2014 Gladiator Frame Investigation","url":"https://www.mopar.com/en-us/care/recalls.html"}],"alternatives":["payload-towing-reality","jt-steering-shaft-recall"],"summary":"Early Gladiators (primarily 2020\u20132021 model years) experienced frame bends and cracks linked to towing with excessive tongue weight. Stellantis investigated and issued a recall covering affected vehicles. If you own an early JT and tow, verify the recall status and practice tongue weight discipline \u2014 the tow rating is real, but the tongue weight limit is where people get into trouble.","description":"Stellantis received reports of Gladiator frames showing deformation \u2014 bending and cracking \u2014 primarily at the rear frame rails behind the cab, near the hitch receiver mounting area. The failures were associated with towing scenarios where tongue weight exceeded the recommended limit.\n\nThe Gladiator frame is a pickup-truck ladder frame, but the JT's wheelbase is shorter than most truck platforms. The shorter rear overhang concentrates tongue weight stress at a tighter section of the frame. When tongue weight runs high \u2014 especially with heavier trailers or loads positioned too far back in the trailer \u2014 that stress can exceed what the frame tolerates.\n\nThe NHTSA investigation (NHTSA ID PE21-002) covered early production Gladiators. Stellantis conducted an Engineering Analysis and issued a service action. Check the NHTSA recall database with your VIN to confirm whether your truck is affected and whether the service action has been completed.\n\nTotal tow rating gets the headlines. Tongue weight is where the actual failures happened.\n\nThe Gladiator's tongue weight limit is 10% of trailer weight, with a max that varies by configuration. On a 5,000 lb trailer, that means 500 lb maximum tongue weight. On a 7,650 lb trailer (Sport with Max Tow), that's 765 lb.\n\n**The common mistake:** People load trailers and then add gear at the rear of the trailer to \"balance\" it. This pushes weight toward the tongue and over the limit. The trailer should be loaded with 60% of its weight ahead of the axle \u2014 this is how tongue weight stays in the 10\u201312% range.\n\nA tongue weight scale is a $30\u2013$60 tool that attaches to the ball mount and lets you read tongue weight directly before you put the truck under load. If you tow regularly, this is worth having.\n\nIf you can't weigh it, use the 60/40 rule: place the heavier items in the front half of the trailer (ahead of the axle), lighter items in the rear. Run the numbers: trailer weight \u00d7 0.10 = maximum tongue weight in lb.\n\nOn early Gladiators, inspect the rear frame rails behind the cab:\n\n- Look for cracking or deformation along the top and bottom flanges of the frame rail\n- Check the area around the receiver hitch mounting bolts \u2014 look for elongated bolt holes or cracking at the attachment points\n- Look at the crossmember that ties the rear frame rails together \u2014 deformation here is a flag\n- Any visible bend in the frame rail is serious and warrants a shop visit before towing again\n\nA frame that has bent does not bend back. If you find deformation, stop towing until the truck is inspected by a qualified shop and the frame is evaluated.\n\nFor trailers near the top of the JT's rating, a weight-distributing (WD) hitch changes how tongue load is carried. Instead of dumping the full tongue weight onto the rear hitch and the short rear overhang, a WD hitch uses spring bars to transfer part of that load forward to the front axle and back to the trailer axle. On the JT specifically \u2014 where the concern is stress concentrated at the rear frame section \u2014 a properly set WD hitch reduces rear-frame loading and restores front-axle steering and braking authority that a heavy tongue would otherwise lift away.\n\nA WD hitch does not raise your tongue weight limit or your tow rating. It manages how the rated load is distributed. Match the spring bar rating to your actual measured tongue weight \u2014 bars rated far above your load over-correct and ride harshly; bars rated below won't distribute properly. If you're towing anything past ~3,500 lb regularly, price a WD hitch alongside the trailer. The [payload and towing reality guide](/db/gladiator/payload-towing-reality/) works through how quickly passengers, gear, and tongue weight eat into the JT's real-world payload before you ever hit the tow number.\n\nIf you towed over spec \u2014 even once, even briefly \u2014 inspect before you assume the frame is fine. Overload damage is cumulative and doesn't always show as a dramatic bend. Look for paint cracking or flaking along the frame rail flanges behind the cab, elongated or egg-shaped holes at the hitch mounting bolts, and any daylight or gap change at the rear crossmember-to-rail joints. A frame that has yielded will often show a subtle waviness in the rail that a straightedge laid along the flange will reveal. Any of these findings means a qualified frame shop, not a wait-and-see. A compromised frame also changes how the truck behaves under recovery loads \u2014 review [recovery gear essentials](/db/gladiator/jt-recovery-gear-essentials/) before you rig to a truck whose structure you're unsure of.","steps":["Go to nhtsa.gov/recalls and enter your VIN","Check for any open recalls or service actions on the Gladiator frame","If your truck is covered by the recall and the service action hasn't been completed, contact a Jeep/Stellantis dealer \u2014 this is warranty-covered work","Inspect the rear frame rails with a flashlight","If you currently tow, calculate your tongue weight (trailer weight \u00d7 0.10) and compare it to your actual tongue weight","Adjust trailer loading if tongue weight is above 10%"],"pros":["The recall and investigation resulted in a clear service path for affected trucks","Most early trucks have had the service action completed through dealer network","Practicing tongue weight discipline prevents the failure mode entirely"],"cons":["Frame damage from over-tongue-weight towing is not covered under recall if the vehicle was loaded beyond spec","A bent frame is a serious structural issue \u2014 it's not a cosmetic repair","Used-market JTs may not have recall work completed \u2014 always verify VIN before purchase"]},{"id":"jt-bed-liner-options","title":"Bed Liner Options for the Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"body","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":600},"tools_required":["masking tape","razor blade","degreaser","paint roller and brush (for roll-on)","respirator (for spray-on)"],"skills_required":["basic prep work"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["body","bed","protection","liner","cargo"],"parts":[{"name":"Durabak 18 Truck Bed Liner \u2014 1 Gallon Roll-On","vendor":"Durabak","price_estimate_usd":89},{"name":"Herculiner HCL0B8 Bed Liner Kit","vendor":"Herculiner","price_estimate_usd":69},{"name":"Rough Country RCRB100 Hard Tri-Fold Tonneau Cover","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"BedRug IMJ20SBS Bed Mat for JT","vendor":"BedRug","price_estimate_usd":249}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator JT Bed Dimensions \u2014 Jeep Official","url":"https://www.jeep.com/gladiator/features/cargo.html"},{"title":"Rhino Linings Spray-In Application Guide","url":"https://www.rhinolinings.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-bed-rack-options","jt-cargo-bed-management"],"summary":"The Gladiator JT's 5-foot bed is one of the truck's biggest selling points \u2014 a real cargo space on a real off-road platform. Protecting it correctly depends on how you use it.","description":"The factory JT bed is stamped steel with a basic painted finish. Haul lumber, gear bags, recovery equipment, and firewood over a few seasons without protection and the bed floor starts rusting through paint chips at scratch points. The right liner depends on whether you're protecting paint, improving grip, dampening road noise, or all three.\n\n**Spray-in liner (professional):** A pro spray-in (Line-X, Rhino Lining, or Sherwin-Williams truck bed coating) is the most durable option. The shop scuffs the surface, masks the bed rails, and sprays a textured polyurethane coating that bonds chemically to the steel. Thickness is typically 1/4\" and the texture provides excellent grip. Cost: $450\u2013600 at most shops. Lifetime coverage. The downside: once it's on, it's permanent \u2014 and it adds weight (typically 15\u201320 lbs for a JT-size bed).\n\n**DIY roll-on liner:** Herculiner and Durabak produce roll-on versions of the same polyurethane chemistry at a fraction of the cost. Prep work is the limiting factor \u2014 the bed surface must be clean, dry, degreased, and scuffed with 80-grit sandpaper for the coating to bond correctly. A properly prepared DIY roll-on holds up well; a rushed prep job peels within a year. Durabak 18 is thicker and more resilient than Herculiner and is the preferred choice for builds that see real trail use.\n\n**Drop-in liner:** Plastic drop-in liners (Westin, Husky, Access) protect paint but trap water and debris underneath, which accelerates rust over time. They're also prone to shifting under load. On a truck used primarily for hauling or as a daily, a drop-in is acceptable. On a rig that lives in wet environments or sees seasons of trail use, avoid them.\n\n**Bed mat:** BedRug and DualLiner mats are a compromise \u2014 removable, won't trap water as aggressively as a drop-in, and provide surface protection without the permanence of a spray-in. The BedRug IMJ20SBS is custom-fit to the JT bed including the tailgate and works well for overlanders who regularly rearrange the bed contents and need something they can pull out to clean.\n\n**The JT bed dimensions:** 60\" long x 53.5\" wide between the wheel wells x 20.3\" deep. The Gladiator Sport and Sport S run the same 5-foot bed as all JT trims \u2014 bed size doesn't change by trim level.\n\n1. Remove any accessories from the bed \u2014 tie-down cleats, cargo rails, drain plugs.\n2. Wash the bed thoroughly with soap and water, rinse, and let dry completely. Any surface moisture causes adhesion failure.\n3. Sand the entire bed surface with 80-grit sandpaper to scuff the paint. Don't skip wheel wells or corners. The goal is a uniform matte surface with no gloss remaining.\n4. Wipe down with degreaser (acetone or wax-and-grease remover). Let flash dry for 15 minutes.\n5. Mask the top bed rails, tailgate weatherstripping, and any area you don't want coated. Use automotive masking tape \u2014 standard painter's tape won't hold the overspray.\n6. Apply the first coat with a roller in long, even strokes. Brush into corners and wheel well curves where the roller can't reach. Coverage should be even \u2014 avoid thick puddles.\n7. Let the first coat flash for 2\u20134 hours (per product instructions) before applying the second coat. Two coats are minimum; three coats in high-wear areas (tailgate, behind the wheel wells) is better.\n8. Remove masking tape while the final coat is still slightly tacky. Pulling tape after full cure can lift the liner edge.\n9. Allow 48\u201372 hours full cure before loading heavy gear. The liner will off-gas during cure \u2014 don't park in an enclosed garage during this period.\n\n- **Rust already present**: If there's existing surface rust on the bed floor, treat it with a rust converter before applying liner. Coating over rust traps moisture and accelerates the corrosion underneath.\n- **Drain plugs**: The JT has two drain plugs in the bed floor. Remove them during application and reinstall after cure \u2014 coating over them blocks drainage.\n- **Touch-up**: Spray-in liners can be touched up in spot areas if damaged. Roll-on liners can be re-coated over existing coats as long as the surface is clean and lightly scuffed.\n\nThe right liner is decided by how the bed earns its keep, not by which product is most durable in the abstract. Three questions sort it: do you slide heavy gear in and out, do you sleep or kneel in the bed, and does the truck live somewhere wet?\n\nIf you routinely load firewood, recovery gear, and steel tools that slide and drop, prioritize impact resistance and grip \u2014 a thick spray-in or a Durabak roll-on takes the hits and keeps cargo from skating on braking. If you use the bed as a sleeping platform or a work surface, a BedRug-style carpeted mat is warmer, quieter, and kinder to knees and gear than bare polyurethane, and it pulls out to shake clean. If the bed mostly carries a fridge, drawers, and a [/db/gladiator/jt-bed-rack-options/](/db/gladiator/jt-bed-rack-options/) or drawer system, protection matters less than a flat, gripped surface that won't let a 40-lb drawer slide \u2014 a roll-on plus a rubber mat under the drawers is enough.\n\nThe one combination to avoid is a slick drop-in under heavy point loads: a plastic tub concentrates a dropped jack or rock into a single flex point and cracks, then traps water in the crack. Think in terms of load pattern, then pick the surface, then organize the cargo \u2014 the storage side of that is covered in [/db/gladiator/jt-cargo-bed-management/](/db/gladiator/jt-cargo-bed-management/).\n\nWhatever surface you choose, the failure mode that actually ends a bed is corrosion starting underneath the liner where you can't see it. Water gets in through a chip, a poorly sealed edge, or a drop-in's trapped puddle, and it works outward for a season before it shows.\n\nTreat the metal before you cover it. If there's existing surface rust on the bed floor, wire-wheel it back to sound metal, treat it with a rust converter (Ospho or a phosphoric-acid product), and let it cure fully before any liner goes down. Coating over live rust seals moisture in and accelerates the very failure you're trying to prevent. On a spray-in, the shop's prep should include this \u2014 confirm it, because a rushed franchise job that sprays over flash rust looks perfect on day one and bubbles at year two.\n\nKeep the two factory bed drain plugs clear. Remove them during any roll-on or spray-in application and reinstall after cure so the bed can shed water instead of pooling it against fresh coating. For trucks in salt or coastal environments, a light spray of cavity wax (Fluid Film or Woolwax) into the bed-side seams and the tailgate hinge area once a year does more for long-term bed life than any liner upgrade. The same corrosion logic applies to the cab floor if you run water crossings with the doors off \u2014 the [/db/gladiator/jt-floor-liners/](/db/gladiator/jt-floor-liners/) writeup covers keeping standing water off the interior sheet metal.\n\nProfessional spray-in (Line-X, Rhino Linings): $450\u2013600. Longest-lasting option with a transferable warranty at most franchise shops.\n\nHerculiner DIY kit (1 gallon, covers a full JT bed): ~$65\u201375. Adequate for light-duty protection, but prep work determines the outcome.\n\nDurabak 18 (1 gallon): ~$85\u201395. Thicker formulation than Herculiner, better suited for a truck that hauls trail gear.\n\nBedRug Custom Mat for JT: ~$240\u2013260. Removable, carpet-like surface with good drainage \u2014 the overland-friendly alternative to a permanent coating."},{"id":"jt-bed-rack-options","title":"Bed Rack and Overland Platform Options \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"body","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":299,"high":1800},"tools_required":["socket set (10mm, 12mm)","drill (if required for your rack)","torque wrench","level"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["body","bed-rack","overland","cargo","storage"],"parts":[{"name":"Yakima OverHaul HD Truck Bed Rack \u2014 JT Gladiator","vendor":"Yakima","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"Thule TracRac TR4 Truck Rack \u2014 JT Compatible","vendor":"Thule","price_estimate_usd":849},{"name":"Go Rhino XRS Cargo Rack \u2014 Gladiator JT","vendor":"Go Rhino","price_estimate_usd":399},{"name":"Leitner Designs ACS Classic Bed System \u2014 JT","vendor":"Leitner Designs","price_estimate_usd":1399},{"name":"Sherpa Equipment Co. Colorado Bed Rack \u2014 JT 5ft bed","vendor":"Sherpa Equipment Co.","price_estimate_usd":649}],"citations":[{"title":"Yakima OverHaul HD JT Gladiator Fitment","url":"https://www.yakima.com"},{"title":"Leitner ACS JT Gladiator Product Page","url":"https://www.leitnerdesigns.com"},{"title":"Sherpa Equipment JT Bed Rack Overview","url":"https://www.sherpaequipment.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-bed-liner-options","jt-cargo-bed-management"],"summary":"The Gladiator JT's 5-foot bed is the feature that separates it from every Wrangler variant \u2014 it turns a capable trail rig into a capable trail rig that can actually carry gear. A bed rack expands that capacity vertically and gives you mounting points for overland accessories.","description":"The JT's bed measures 60\" long and 44.5\" wide between the wheel wells \u2014 the standard 5-foot pickup measurement. This fits most bed rack systems designed for mid-size trucks, but the JT's specific stake pocket spacing (the tie-down slots along the top rail) is important to verify before ordering. Stake pocket depth and spacing vary by year, and not all universal racks fit the JT without adapter brackets.\n\nMost JT bed rack systems clamp to the bed rail via stake pocket inserts \u2014 no drilling required. A small number of rack systems require drilling into the bed floor or using factory cargo track anchors. Know which type you're buying before you commit.\n\n**Universal crossbar racks** (Yakima OverHaul HD, Thule TracRac): Two crossbars spanning the bed width, adjustable fore/aft position. Load rating typically 300\u2013500 lbs. Mount roof boxes, kayaks, lumber, or a roof top tent (RTT) on a crossbar system. The JT's 5-foot bed needs careful RTT positioning \u2014 the tent must deploy rearward or to the side without conflicting with the cab.\n\n**Full bed platform systems** (Leitner ACS, Sherpa Colorado): A full-coverage aluminum extrusion system that spans the entire bed floor and rails. Creates a platform above the bed floor for gear organization, drawer mounting, and a sleeping or tent surface. Higher cost ($1,200\u20131,600+) but more versatile \u2014 you can run an RTT on top, drawers underneath, and still have access to the bed below. The Leitner ACS is the benchmark for JT overland platform systems.\n\n**Stake pocket crossbars** (Go Rhino XRS, Smittybilt): Ladder-style racks with two or three crossbars that drop into stake pockets. Lower profile, lower cost ($300\u2013450). Load ratings run 400\u2013600 lbs. Less useful for complex overland setups but adequate for equipment transport (bikes, roof boxes, auxiliary fuel cans).\n\nThe JT's cab height and bed rail position create a rear deck height of approximately 30\u201332\" loaded (wheel arch height). An RTT mounted on a bed rack sits 6\u201312\" above the bed rail when folded. This stacks to 38\u201344\" \u2014 manageable for a garage with an 8' door, tight for lower clearances.\n\nFor RTT mounting: confirm the rack's dynamic load rating (what it can carry while driving, not parked). Most RTTs weigh 100\u2013180 lbs. Most bed racks rate dynamic loads at 150\u2013300 lbs. Match accordingly, and check that the RTT width (most are 56\"\u201365\") fits within the JT bed width without overhanging the wheel wells excessively.\n\n**Stake pocket systems (no-drill):**\n1. Identify the stake pocket positions along the JT bed rail (typically 6 pockets, 3 per side).\n2. Drop the stake pocket inserts from the rack kit into the pockets. Most kits use expanding inserts tightened from above.\n3. Set the crossbars or frame across the inserts and thread the mounting bolts.\n4. Level and square the rack before final torque.\n5. Torque to spec per the rack manufacturer \u2014 typically 15\u201325 ft-lbs on stake pocket inserts.\n\n**Platform systems (Leitner ACS):**\n1. More involved \u2014 follows a kit-specific sequence. Typically: install cargo track base to the bed floor (anchors to factory cargo anchor points), then assemble the extrusion frame to the cargo track, then mount the top deck.\n2. Allow 2\u20133 hours for first install.\n\nThe JT's real limitation is not rack strength \u2014 it's payload. A Rubicon's payload runs roughly 1,100\u20131,200 lbs, a Sport around 1,600 lbs. Everything you add counts against that number: the rack, the RTT, gear, water, fuel, passengers, and the weight of a built truck's armor.\n\nA worked example on a Rubicon: rack (80 lbs) + RTT (140 lbs) + two adults in the tent (360 lbs) + bedding and gear (100 lbs) = 680 lbs before you have loaded the cab, filled a fridge, or added recovery gear. On a truck already carrying bumpers and a winch, you can exceed rated payload before leaving the driveway. Weigh the built truck at a CAT scale and do the arithmetic before committing to a heavy platform. The [payload and towing reality](/db/gladiator/payload-towing-reality/) guide covers the axle and GVWR numbers in full.\n\nA rack that carries fuel cans, firewood, and recovery boards will drop debris into the bed. Pair a platform with a [spray-in or drop-in bed liner](/db/gladiator/jt-bed-liner-options/) so the rack's cargo does not chew up the factory bed finish, and use a [bed drawer or cargo management system](/db/gladiator/jt-cargo-bed-management/) underneath a full platform to keep weight low and organized.\n\n- **Dynamic vs. static load rating:** The number that matters while driving is the dynamic rating, always lower than the static (parked) rating. Size the rack to the RTT plus occupants against the dynamic figure.\n- **Stake pocket corrosion:** Inserts that live in the pockets trap water. Pull and inspect them annually; a seized insert can crack the pocket casting.\n- **Cab clearance on RTT deploy:** The 5-foot bed is short. Confirm the tent opens rearward or sideways without hitting the cab or a high-mount third brake light.\n- **Wind noise and MPG:** A loaded crossbar rack adds meaningful drag. Remove or lower it for long highway stretches when the gear is not needed.\n\nGo Rhino or basic stake pocket rack: ~$299\u2013399. Adequate for occasional gear transport.\n\nYakima OverHaul HD or Thule TracRac: ~$699\u2013849. The right choice for frequent RTT or overhead gear use.\n\nSherpa Colorado or equivalent mid-tier: ~$649. Good balance of build quality and modularity for JT overland builds.\n\nLeitner ACS or similar premium platform: ~$1,399\u20131,600. The correct investment if the JT is a dedicated overland build \u2014 replaces both a bed liner and a rack with a modular, organized system.\n\nInstallation is DIY-friendly for all stake pocket and crossbar systems. The Leitner ACS is more involved but well-documented \u2014 allow an afternoon and follow the instructions carefully."},{"id":"jt-brake-pad-rotor-replacement","title":"Brake Pad and Rotor Replacement \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"brakes","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":120,"high":380},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","breaker bar","socket set (metric)","caliper piston tool or large C-clamp","torque wrench","bungee cord or wire to hang caliper"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","safe vehicle support"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["brakes","safety","maintenance","rotors","pads"],"parts":[{"name":"Power Stop Z23 Evolution Sport Brake Pads & Rotors \u2014 Front JT","vendor":"Power Stop","price_estimate_usd":189},{"name":"Hawk HPS Performance Brake Pads \u2014 Front JT","vendor":"Hawk Performance","price_estimate_usd":89},{"name":"StopTech Sport Slotted Rotors \u2014 JT Front","vendor":"StopTech","price_estimate_usd":129},{"name":"ACDelco OE Replacement Brake Pads \u2014 JT","vendor":"ACDelco","price_estimate_usd":48}],"citations":[{"title":"Gladiator JT Service Manual \u2014 Brake System","url":"https://www.stellantis.com"},{"title":"Power Stop JT Brake Kit Product Page","url":"https://www.powerstop.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-brake-fluid-flush","jt-tire-rotation-balancing"],"summary":"The JT Gladiator uses the same front brake assembly as the JL Wrangler on most trims \u2014 which means the aftermarket selection is broad and pad wear patterns from Wrangler owners translate directly.","description":"The Gladiator's brakes work harder than a standard passenger truck's because the JT is heavier than it looks \u2014 the Rubicon trim with full armor can push 5,100 lbs \u2014 and towing pushes the braking system further. Pads that are marginal on a lighter truck can fade on a Gladiator with a loaded trailer on a mountain descent. Budget accordingly.\n\nFront pads wear faster than rears. On a Rubicon used for moderate off-road and daily driving, expect front pad replacement around 35,000\u201345,000 miles. Towing and trail use shortens that. Check pad thickness whenever you rotate tires.\n\n**Safety note:** Never work under a vehicle supported only by a jack. Use rated jack stands at all four corners before getting under the JT for any brake work.\n\n- **Caliper slides**: If the old pads wore unevenly (outer much thinner than inner, or vice versa), the caliper slides are likely corroded and not moving freely. Clean and re-lube them. Ignoring this causes the new pads to wear the same way.\n- **Brake fluid level**: When you compress the caliper piston, brake fluid backs up into the reservoir. Check the level before starting \u2014 if it's near max, siphon a little out before compressing the piston to avoid overflow.\n- **Rust ring on rotors**: If the rotor has a significant rust lip at the outer edge from pad contact area, the rotor is at or near minimum thickness. Measure with a micrometer before reusing.\n- **Rubicon hubs**: The Rubicon front lockers add complexity to rotor removal. Take photos before disassembly.\n\nPad friction material is the decision that actually changes how the JT stops, and there's no single \"best\" \u2014 each compound trades something.\n\n- **OE-style ceramic (ACDelco OE, Mopar, Akebono):** quiet, low dust, gentle on rotors, and fully adequate for a stock-weight truck doing street and light trail. Their weakness is fade resistance under sustained heat \u2014 a loaded descent or repeated hard stops will heat-soak them faster than a performance compound.\n- **Performance ceramic / street-sport (Power Stop Z23, similar):** higher initial bite and better heat tolerance than OE while staying street-friendly on noise and dust. This is the common upgrade for a JT that tows or carries armor weight.\n- **Semi-metallic / high-performance (Hawk HPS and up):** the highest friction and fade resistance, the right call for heavy towing and mountain grades. The tradeoff is more brake dust, more noise, and faster rotor wear. They also need heat to work best, so they can feel grabby cold.\n\nMatch the compound to your heaviest regular use, not your lightest. A Gladiator that spends most days empty but tows a trailer over a pass twice a month should be specced for the pass, because that's where marginal pads fail.\n\n**Safety note:** Never mix compounds side to side on the same axle, and always replace pads in axle pairs. Uneven friction across an axle pulls the truck under braking \u2014 a real hazard in an emergency stop.\n\nLarger, heavier tires are the single most overlooked brake factor on a built JT, and the physics are not in your favor.\n\nA 37-inch tire is both heavier (more rotating mass to stop) and taller (a longer lever arm against the same brake torque) than the factory 32\u201333-inch tire. The factory brakes were sized for the factory tire. Move to 35s or 37s and the same pedal input produces less deceleration, stopping distances lengthen, and the brakes run hotter doing the same work \u2014 which accelerates pad fade and wear.\n\nWhat to do about it:\n\n- **Re-gear when you go big.** Correcting axle ratio for larger tires restores drivetrain leverage and lets engine braking help again, taking load off the service brakes on descents. See [/db/gladiator/jt-regear/](/db/gladiator/jt-regear/).\n- **Step up pad compound and consider slotted rotors** to handle the added heat, rather than staying on OE-style pads sized for stock weight.\n- **Keep the fluid fresh.** Bigger tires mean more heat into the caliper, and heat is what boils moisture-laden brake fluid into a soft pedal. Stay current on the flush interval \u2014 [/db/gladiator/jt-brake-fluid-flush/](/db/gladiator/jt-brake-fluid-flush/).\n- **Re-check torque and wear at every rotation.** Fold the brake inspection into your [/db/gladiator/jt-tire-rotation-balancing/](/db/gladiator/jt-tire-rotation-balancing/) routine so heavier-tire wear never sneaks up on you.\n\nThere is no full brake-upgrade big-brake kit that's a bolt-on necessity for a mildly built JT, but if you run 37s and tow, budget for the best pad and rotor tier before you need it \u2014 brakes are the one system where \"good enough later\" is the wrong call.\n\nFactory-spec OEM pads (ACDelco or Mopar OE): ~$40\u201360 per axle. Adequate for stock-weight street use and light off-road.\n\nPower Stop Z23 kit (pads and rotors, one axle): ~$150\u2013200. The most common upgrade choice for JT owners \u2014 drilled and slotted rotors handle heat better under load.\n\nHawk HPS pads alone: ~$80\u201390 per axle. Higher friction coefficient than stock, better fade resistance for towing. Works with factory or aftermarket rotors.\n\nStopTech Sport Slotted: ~$120\u2013140 per rotor. Slot pattern sheds gas and debris off the pad face \u2014 meaningful on a trail rig where dust gets into the rotor/pad interface.\n\nAll brake work is DIY-friendly at this level. A shop charges $150\u2013250 in labor for a front axle pad and rotor replacement on a JT \u2014 the parts markup is usually where they make money. Do it yourself and save the labor.","steps":["Break lug nuts loose (turn counterclockwise) before jacking. The JT's 17-inch factory wheels use a 6\u00d75.5-inch bolt pattern with 7/8-inch lug nuts \u2014 standard socket fitment.","Jack the vehicle at the frame or designated jack point, set jack stands, and remove the wheels.","Inspect current pad thickness through the caliper window. Under 3mm of friction material means replacement now. At 3\u20134mm you have some runway but plan the work.","Remove the two caliper slide bolts (typically 13mm on the JT front). Slide the caliper off the rotor \u2014 do not let it hang by the brake hose. Bungee it to the coil spring or a suspension component to relieve stress.","Remove the old pads from the caliper bracket. Note the orientation and clip positions before pulling them out.","If replacing rotors: remove the two caliper bracket bolts (18mm). The rotor slides off the hub \u2014 on a Rubicon with a locking hub assembly, this may require backing out the hub lock ring first.","Install the new rotor and torque the caliper bracket bolts to spec: 77 ft-lbs front, 51 ft-lbs rear (factory spec \u2014 verify against your torque chart).","Compress the caliper piston using a caliper piston tool or large C-clamp with an old pad as a backing plate. Open the brake fluid reservoir cap first to prevent pressure buildup.","Install new pads. Apply a thin coat of brake lubricant (not grease \u2014 use brake-rated ceramic lube) to the pad ears where they contact the caliper bracket. No lubricant on the friction surface.","Reinstall the caliper and torque the slide bolts: 21 ft-lbs (factory spec).","Reinstall wheels. Torque lug nuts to 130 ft-lbs in a star pattern.","Before driving: pump the brake pedal 10\u201315 times until it feels firm. The pedal will be soft on the first pumps as the piston pushes out against the new pads. Do not skip this step.","Bed the new pads: 3\u20134 moderate stops from 40 mph, then 3\u20134 hard stops from 60 mph. Allow 5 minutes between each hard stop for brake cooling. Avoid highway driving for the first 200 miles."]},{"id":"jt-brake-fluid-flush","title":"Brake Fluid Flush \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"brakes","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.75,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":20,"high":50},"tools_required":["turkey baster or fluid transfer pump","8mm or 10mm wrench (bleeder screw)","clear vinyl tubing","catch bottle","torque wrench","nitrile gloves"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["brakes","safety","maintenance","fluid","brake-fluid"],"parts":[{"name":"Valvoline DOT 3/4 Brake Fluid (32 oz)","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":10},{"name":"Motul RBF600 Racing Brake Fluid (DOT 4)","vendor":"Motul","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"Prestone AS800 Brake Fluid (DOT 3)","vendor":"Prestone","price_estimate_usd":8}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator JT Owner's Manual \u2014 Brake Fluid Specification","url":"https://www.mopar.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-brake-pad-rotor-replacement","jt-35-inch-tire-fitment"],"summary":"Brake fluid absorbs moisture over time, lowering its boiling point. On a JT used for towing or trail descents where brakes run hot, degraded fluid can boil before you think it will. Flush it every two years or any time the fluid looks dark.","description":"The Gladiator JT specifies DOT 3 fluid from the factory, but DOT 4 is backward-compatible and offers a higher dry boiling point (446\u00b0F vs 401\u00b0F). If you do any serious towing or downhill trail driving, running DOT 4 is the right call \u2014 the extra margin costs $10 more per flush. Motul RBF600 is the go-to for track or hard-use applications; for most JT owners, any quality DOT 4 is sufficient.\n\nBrake fluid is hygroscopic \u2014 it pulls moisture from the air through micro-permeation of the brake lines and reservoir cap seals. Even in a sealed system, fluid absorbs enough water in two years to drop the boiling point measurably. The visual test is straightforward: fresh fluid is clear to light yellow; fluid with absorbed moisture goes dark brown. If yours looks like weak coffee, flush it now regardless of calendar date.\n\n**Safety note:** DOT 3/4 brake fluid is a paint stripper. It will damage your JT's paint if spilled on bodywork. Work carefully near fenders and the firewall. Nitrile gloves protect your skin.\n\n- **Spongy pedal after bleeding**: Air in the system. Bleed the affected corner again. If it persists, check for a weeping bleeder nipple thread \u2014 they corrode and can introduce air through the threads even when \"closed.\"\n- **Reservoir going dry during procedure**: This is the main risk. Station the fluid bottle right next to the reservoir and check level after every 2\u20133 pumps per wheel.\n- **Rounded bleeder screws**: If the nipple is corroded and rounds off under the wrench, use a bleeder screw extractor set before it gets worse. Penetrating oil the night before helps.\n- **Single-person bleeding**: A vacuum bleeder (Motive Products or similar) lets you flush without a helper. Attach it to the bleeder nipple and use the hand pump to draw fluid through \u2014 no pedal pumping required. More expensive upfront, but worth having in the tool kit.\n\nThe letter on the bottle is a boiling-point and chemistry spec, and getting it wrong on a JT ranges from a wasted flush to a ruined brake system. DOT 3, DOT 4, and DOT 5.1 are all glycol-based and mutually compatible \u2014 you can top a DOT 3 system with DOT 4 without harm. DOT 5 is silicone-based, does not mix with glycol fluid, and must never go into the JT's ABS-equipped system; it foams under ABS pump cycling and its incompatibility with the existing fluid can wreck seals. If a bottle says DOT 5 (not 5.1), leave it on the shelf.\n\nTwo numbers matter: dry boiling point (fresh fluid) and wet boiling point (fluid that has absorbed 3.7% water, roughly two years of service). DOT 3 runs about 401\u00b0F dry / 284\u00b0F wet, DOT 4 about 446\u00b0F / 311\u00b0F, and DOT 5.1 about 500\u00b0F / 356\u00b0F. The wet number is what protects you on a long grade with a loaded trailer, because your fluid is rarely fresh when you need it most. For a JT that tows or runs long trail descents, DOT 4 is the sensible default and DOT 5.1 is worth it if you regularly cook the brakes; for a stock street truck, factory DOT 3 flushed on schedule is adequate.\n\nA straight fluid flush that never opens the hydraulic system below the calipers can be done with the pedal-and-bleeder method above. The moment you crack a brake line, replace a caliper, or let the master cylinder run dry, the JT's ABS module can trap air that manual bleeding won't clear \u2014 the fluid has to be cycled through the ABS unit, which on many Gladiators requires a scan tool with an ABS bleed function (or a shop visit) to actuate the pump and valves. If you get a firm pedal at the wheels but a long or sinking pedal on the road after opening the system, suspect trapped air in the ABS module rather than bleeding the corners a fourth time.\n\nBigger tires change the thermal math even though they don't touch the hydraulics. Stepping a JT to 37s adds rotating mass and a longer lever arm, so the factory brakes work harder and run hotter to stop the same truck \u2014 which pulls more heat into the fluid and makes fresh, high-boiling-point fluid matter more, not less. If you've gone big on tires, treat the two-year flush interval as a ceiling, not a target, and consider DOT 4 minimum. The tire-fitment tradeoffs that drive this are covered in [/db/gladiator/jt-35-inch-tire-fitment/](/db/gladiator/jt-35-inch-tire-fitment/), and pad and rotor choices that pair with a heavier setup are in [/db/gladiator/jt-brake-pad-rotor-replacement/](/db/gladiator/jt-brake-pad-rotor-replacement/).\n\nDOT 3 fluid (factory spec): $8\u201312 per 32 oz bottle. One bottle handles a full flush with fluid left over.\n\nDOT 4 upgrade: $12\u201322 per bottle depending on brand. Motul RBF600 at the top end is overkill for most street/trail JTs; Prestone DOT 4 at $12 is fine.\n\nVacuum bleeder kit: $25\u201340 (Motive Products, OTC). A one-time purchase that eliminates the need for a helper and makes fluid flushes routine. Recommend it for anyone doing their own brake maintenance long-term.\n\nShop charge for a brake fluid flush: $80\u2013120. This is a 45-minute job for a shop with a power bleeder. The DIY savings are real, but the main reason to do it yourself is knowing it was done and done correctly.","steps":["Locate the brake fluid reservoir under the hood on the driver's side of the firewall. Remove the cap and use a turkey baster or fluid transfer pump to remove as much old fluid as possible from the reservoir.","Refill the reservoir with fresh fluid (DOT 4 if upgrading) to the MAX line. Do not let the reservoir run dry during the procedure \u2014 an air bubble introduced at the master cylinder requires bench bleeding to fix.","Jack the vehicle and set jack stands. Start with the wheel farthest from the master cylinder (typically the passenger rear on the JT) and work toward the driver's front: RR \u2192 LR \u2192 RF \u2192 LF.","Locate the bleeder screw on the caliper (rear) or caliper bracket (front). Attach a short length of clear vinyl tubing to the bleeder nipple and run the other end into a catch bottle.","Have an assistant slowly depress the brake pedal while you open the bleeder screw (turn counterclockwise \u2014 usually 8mm or 10mm). Watch the fluid flowing into the catch bottle. Old fluid is dark; fresh fluid is clear or very light yellow.","Close the bleeder screw before the assistant releases the pedal \u2014 this prevents air from being sucked back in. Torque the bleeder screw lightly when closing: 10 in-lbs. Over-tightening cracks the nipple.","Repeat the pump-open-close cycle at each wheel until only clean fluid flows from the bleeder. Expect 4\u20138 pumps per corner.","After each corner, top up the reservoir. Check it frequently \u2014 running it dry introduces air into the system.","After all four corners: top off the reservoir to MAX, reinstall the cap, reinstall wheels, torque lug nuts to 130 ft-lbs.","Pump the brake pedal 10\u201315 times with the vehicle still on stands. Confirm a firm, consistent pedal. If it's spongy, you have air in the system \u2014 bleed again starting from the farthest wheel."]},{"id":"jt-trim-buyers-guide","title":"Gladiator trim guide: Sport vs Willys vs Rubicon vs Mojave","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"build_recipe","subcategory":"trim comparison","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":["VIN decoder / window sticker","Jeep build-and-price configurator"],"skills_required":["research","budget planning"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-present"],"tags":["trim","buyers guide","rubicon","mojave","willys","sport","comparison"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator Specs \u2014 FCA Media","url":"https://www.media.stellantis.com/us-en/jeep/press-releases"},{"title":"Gladiator Mojave Desert-Rated information","url":"https://www.jeep.com/gladiator"}],"alternatives":["payload-towing-reality","gladiator-vs-jl-mods-compatibility"],"summary":"The Rubicon is the trail rig. The Mojave is the desert runner. The Sport and Willys are clean starting points for a custom build. Pick based on what the truck will actually do, not the badge.","description":"The Gladiator lineup splits along two fundamentally different axes: rock-crawling capability (Rubicon) and high-speed desert performance (Mojave). The Sport and Willys sit below both \u2014 they're capable trucks with the same solid-axle bones, but without the factory lockers, sway-bar disconnect, and upgraded suspension you'd need to buy anyway if you go that direction.\n\nBefore comparing trims, answer this: do you want to build the truck yourself, or do you want factory capability out of the gate? If you're building, start with Sport or Willys and save the price difference. If you want to drive it off the lot and onto technical terrain the same weekend, the Rubicon makes the math work.\n\nThe entry point. Solid Dana 44 axles front and rear \u2014 this is meaningful. The Gladiator doesn't have a weak axle option; the base truck runs the same 44s as the Rubicon, without lockers or the electronic disconnect. The Sport runs 32-inch tires (LT255/75R17) and a 3.73 axle ratio. The suspension is the base coil-and-leaf setup \u2014 adequate for mild trail and daily driving, not competitive on technical terrain.\n\n**Sport S** adds convenience and comfort features (8.4-inch Uconnect, power windows, keyless entry). No mechanical upgrades. If you're building from the bottom up, Sport S is the trim to start with \u2014 it has enough creature comforts to live with daily without paying for Willys or Overland features you'll discard.\n\nThe value tier for people who actually trail-drive. The Willys adds rock rails, a limited-slip rear differential (in place of an open diff), 32-inch all-terrain tires, and a few suspension upgrades. The LSD is meaningful \u2014 it's not a locker, but it maintains traction better than an open diff under load. The Willys also gets a heavy-duty Dana 44 rear axle with a Trac-Lok LSD, which is a real improvement.\n\nIf you want to start trail-driving immediately without modification and don't want to pay for the Rubicon, the Willys is the honest buy. It's not as capable on rocks \u2014 no front locker, no sway-bar disconnect \u2014 but it's a better truck than the Sport for actual use.\n\nBuilt for technical terrain. The Rubicon gets what matters for rock crawling: electronic front and rear lockers (Dana 44 AdaptTrac), front sway-bar electric disconnect, 33-inch mud-terrain tires (LT285/70R17), and 4.10 axle gears. It also gets rock rails, skid plates (gas tank, transfer case, front axle), and the Rock-Trac NV241 transfer case with a 4:1 low range.\n\nThe sway-bar disconnect is worth understanding: on the trail, disconnecting the front sway bar allows the axles to articulate freely, dramatically increasing wheel travel. The Rubicon can do this at the push of a button. Adding this to a Sport after purchase is expensive and complex.\n\n**The honest catch:** The Rubicon's 1,200 lb payload rating is the lowest in the lineup. A Rubicon with two adults, a full tank, and a bed-mounted camper shell is already close to its limit. If you plan to haul cargo or tow with passengers on board, this matters.\n\nCompletely different use case. The Mojave is the only production Jeep to be rated Desert-Rated (as opposed to Trail-Rated). It's built for high-speed desert running, not rock crawling.\n\nThe Mojave-specific hardware: Fox 2.5-inch Internal Bypass shocks (front and rear), a hydraulic jounce bumper system in the front, 17-inch beadlock-capable wheels, a 2-inch front lift over Sport, and 33-inch Falken Wildpeak A/T tires. The front suspension travel is substantially longer than any other Gladiator trim.\n\n**What the Mojave doesn't have:** a front locker, a front sway-bar disconnect. The desert-running calibration trades technical rock capability for speed and comfort over rough terrain. If you're planning runs in the desert Southwest \u2014 Sonoran Desert, Baja-style terrain \u2014 the Mojave is the right tool. If you're going to Moab or the Rubicon Trail, buy the Rubicon.\n\nThe comfort-forward trim. Heated leather seats, 8.4-inch Uconnect, premium audio, larger fuel tank (24 gallons vs 17.5 on the Sport). Mechanically it sits between the Willys and Rubicon \u2014 it gets the 3.73 ratio and doesn't have lockers or sway-bar disconnect. Overland buyers are typically using the truck as a daily driver with occasional light-trail use. That's a fine use case; don't confuse it with capability.\n\nThe top-trim Rubicon variant, added in the 2023 model year. Upgraded interior, steel bumpers, LED lighting, and additional content. The mechanical package is the same as the standard Rubicon. If the standard Rubicon fits the budget, the X is mostly comfort and appearance upgrades.\n\n- **For rock crawling:** Rubicon. No trail tier comes close for factory capability.\n- **For high-speed desert terrain:** Mojave, no competition.\n- **For building from scratch:** Sport or Willys. Save the trim premium and spend it on the parts that actually matter for your use case.\n- **For daily driving with light trail use:** Overland or Willys Sport.","steps":["Determine your primary terrain (rocks, desert, mixed)","Set a realistic payload budget \u2014 subtract passengers, gear, and any camper weight from your trim's rating","Decide how much you want the factory to have done versus doing it yourself","Price out the gap between a lower trim + your target upgrades vs. buying the Rubicon/Mojave outright"],"pros":["Every Gladiator gets solid Dana 44 axles \u2014 the platform is strong regardless of trim","The Rubicon is genuinely capable from the factory; it doesn't need $5,000 in mods to get on trail","The Mojave's Fox 2.5 internal bypass shocks are a legitimate performance item; they can't be replicated cheaply"],"cons":["The Rubicon's 1,200 lb payload is tight for a work truck or camper setup","The Mojave has no front locker \u2014 it cannot keep up with a Rubicon on technical terrain","The price gap between Sport and Rubicon is significant; do the math before assuming the Rubicon is always the answer"]},{"id":"payload-towing-reality","title":"Gladiator payload and towing: the honest numbers","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"build_recipe","subcategory":"payload and towing","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":["door-jamb payload sticker","CAT scale or trailer tongue-weight scale"],"skills_required":["research"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-present"],"tags":["payload","towing","max tow","rubicon","overland","weight"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator Owner's Manual \u2014 Payload and Towing sections","url":"https://www.jeep.com/gladiator"}],"alternatives":["jt-trim-buyers-guide","factory-leaf-spring-axle-wrap"],"summary":"The Gladiator's payload and tow ratings vary significantly by trim. A Rubicon's 1,200 lb payload disappears fast. The 7,650 lb tow rating is real, but it requires the Max Tow Package on a Sport-level trim \u2014 not the Rubicon.","description":"| Trim | Payload (approx.) | Max Tow (approx.) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Sport with Max Tow | 1,710 lb | 7,650 lb |\n| Sport S | 1,600 lb | 4,000 lb |\n| Willys | 1,540 lb | 4,000 lb |\n| Overland | 1,490 lb | 6,000 lb |\n| Rubicon | 1,200 lb | 5,000 lb |\n| Mojave | 1,350 lb | 5,000 lb |\n\nThese are the factory maximums \u2014 the number stamped on the door jamb sticker for your specific build. The sticker number is the number that matters. It accounts for your exact trim, options, and factory-installed equipment. The published spec sheet gives a range; your truck's sticker gives the actual number.\n\nThe Rubicon is heavier than the base trims. Lockers, skid plates, rock rails, beefier axle hardware, the NV241 Rock-Trac transfer case, the sway-bar disconnect actuator \u2014 all of it adds curb weight. The factory sets payload capacity as GVWR minus curb weight, so a heavier truck has less available payload even if the GVWR is the same. The Rubicon's additional equipment is what cuts its payload rating to 1,200 lb.\n\nThe 7,650 lb tow rating is achievable only on the Sport and Sport S with the Max Tow Package. The package includes a heavy-duty alternator, trailer brake controller, upgraded cooling, Class IV trailer hitch, four-pin and seven-pin electrical connectors, and a 3.73 axle ratio (held to that ratio; if you spec a different ratio, you lose the rating).\n\nThe Rubicon cannot reach 7,650 lb. Its platform is Trail-Rated, not Max Tow rated, and its heavier curb weight works against towing capacity.\n\nA Rubicon towing a loaded camp trailer with two adults in the cab:\n\n- Two adults: ~340 lb\n- Full 17.5-gallon fuel tank: ~115 lb\n- Loaded bed (recovery gear, camp gear): ~300 lb\n- That's 755 lb used before the trailer hitch is connected.\n\nWith 1,200 lb total capacity, that leaves 445 lb for tongue weight and anything else in the bed. A medium-sized camp trailer like a small teardrop or a pop-up can easily put 400\u2013500 lb on the tongue. You're at the limit before the trailer's loaded.\n\nOverland buyers in camp trailers face this calculation constantly. The Gladiator is a shorter-wheelbase platform than a full-size truck. It was designed for trail access, not towing heavy loads. The platform is honest about that.\n\nRegardless of tow rating, the Gladiator has a specific tongue weight limit: 10% of trailer weight, up to a maximum. The frame bend issues documented in early production Gladiators were specifically linked to tongue weight exceeding limits \u2014 not overall tow weight. See the separate frame bend recall guide for details.\n\nThe takeaway: even if you're well within tow rating, managing tongue weight to stay at 10\u201312% of trailer weight is important on the JT. The platform's pickup-truck framing is shorter than a full-size and sees different stress concentrations when tongue weight is high.\n\nThe tow and payload headlines hide four limits, and you hit whichever comes first \u2014 not whichever is highest:\n\n- **GVWR** (Gross Vehicle Weight Rating): the most the loaded truck itself can weigh \u2014 everything in the cab and bed, plus trailer tongue weight, plus the truck. This is the one payload buyers blow through.\n- **GCWR** (Gross Combined Weight Rating): the most the truck and trailer can weigh together. Max-tow numbers assume a nearly empty truck; load the cab and bed and your usable trailer weight drops below the sticker.\n- **GAWR front / rear** (Gross Axle Weight Rating): the most each axle can carry. Heavy tongue weight loads the rear axle specifically \u2014 you can be under GVWR overall and still overload the rear GAWR.\n- **Max tongue weight**: capped as a hard number regardless of trailer size.\n\nThe honest way to check is a CAT scale: weigh the loaded truck and trailer, then the truck alone, and compare each axle and the combined figure against the door-jamb and manual numbers. Guessing from the spec sheet is how people end up over rear GAWR while feeling \"under tow rating.\"\n\nFor any trailer putting meaningful tongue weight on a Gladiator, a weight-distribution (WD) hitch earns its cost. Spring bars transfer some of the tongue load off the JT's rear axle and back onto the front axle and the trailer's own axles, which restores steering feel, levels the truck, and reduces the rear-axle and frame stress that matters on this platform.\n\nThis is not optional caution on the JT specifically. The early-production frame-bend cases were tied to concentrated rear-frame loading, and a WD hitch is one of the direct mitigations \u2014 see [Frame bend and the towing recall on the JT](/db/gladiator/frame-bend-towing-recall/). Pair the WD setup with an anti-sway component for anything tall or long; the JT's short wheelbase makes it more sway-prone at highway speed than a full-size truck. If your load-out is pushing the rear axle down, also revisit rear suspension behavior under load in [Factory leaf spring axle wrap on the JT](/db/gladiator/factory-leaf-spring-axle-wrap/).","steps":["Find your truck's door jamb sticker \u2014 use the actual payload number, not the spec sheet","Weigh your real-world cargo: passengers, fuel, gear, bed items","Subtract from your payload rating \u2014 what's left is available for tongue weight","If that number is under 400 lb, your current load-out isn't compatible with a trailer","Use a tongue weight scale (or weigh at a truck stop) before committing to a trailer purchase"],"pros":["The Sport with Max Tow is a genuinely capable tow vehicle for mid-size trailers","All trims can tow something useful \u2014 even the Rubicon can pull 5,000 lb","The solid-axle rear means no IFS flex under tongue weight load"],"cons":["The Rubicon's 1,200 lb payload is straightforward to exceed in real-world camp and trail use","The best tow rating requires a non-Rubicon trim \u2014 trail buyers and tow buyers are often looking at different trucks","The JT's shorter wheelbase compared to full-size trucks affects stability at highway tow speeds \u2014 drive accordingly"]},{"id":"jt-coolant-flush","title":"Coolant Flush \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"cooling","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":60},"tools_required":["drain pan","funnel","pliers","socket set","flush kit (optional)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["cooling","maintenance","coolant","radiator","overheating"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar Antifreeze/Coolant 5-Year \u2014 1 Gallon","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"Prestone AF3100 Extended Life Antifreeze (1 Gallon)","vendor":"Prestone","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Zerex G-05 Antifreeze/Coolant (1 Gallon)","vendor":"Zerex","price_estimate_usd":20}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator JT Owner's Manual \u2014 Cooling System Specifications","url":"https://www.jeep.com/content/dam/fca-brands/na/jeep/en_us/2022/owner-manuals/2022-jeep-gladiator-owners-manual.pdf"},{"title":"FCA Coolant Specifications for 3.6L Pentastar","url":"https://www.mopar.com"}],"alternatives":["pentastar-3-6-oil-cooler-leak","jt-oil-change-pentastar"],"summary":"The JT Gladiator's 3.6L Pentastar V6 calls for OAT (Organic Acid Technology) coolant, and using the wrong type \u2014 or letting it go past service life \u2014 is how you get corrosion deposits building up inside an aluminum cylinder head.","description":"Jeep specs the Gladiator's 3.6L for HOAT or OAT coolant, specifically a silicate-free formula compatible with the aluminum coolant passages throughout the Pentastar engine. Mopar's factory fill is a 5-year/150,000-mile OAT formulation. The factory interval holds up under normal use \u2014 if the Gladiator sees regular off-road water crossings, dusty desert runs, or extended periods of high coolant temp, inspect it at 60,000 miles rather than waiting for the 5-year mark.\n\nThe telltale sign that coolant needs replacement: it should be bright orange-yellow (Mopar OAT) or green (some aftermarket fills). Dark brown or rust-tinted coolant is carrying iron oxide and aluminum corrosion \u2014 the system needs a flush, not a drain-and-fill.\n\n**Do not mix coolant types.** Mixing OAT with IAT (green silicate coolant) produces a gel that clogs radiator passages. If you're unsure what's already in the system, flush the entire system before filling with fresh coolant.\n\nThe label confusion around Pentastar coolant causes more wrong fills than any other part of this job. Here's the plain version. The factory fill is Mopar's orange-yellow OAT (Organic Acid Technology) coolant, marketed as the 10-year/150,000-mile formula on newer bottles (5-year on the earliest JT bottles \u2014 both are the same OAT chemistry). What matters is that it's silicate-free and phosphate-free, because both silicates and phosphates attack the aluminum and the water-pump seal over time.\n\nAcceptable substitutes are coolants specifically labeled compatible with Chrysler MS-90032 or the OAT/HOAT spec: Zerex G-05 and Prestone's yellow OAT formulation are the two most commonly confirmed. Where owners go wrong is grabbing a green \"universal\" IAT coolant or a generic \"works with all makes\" jug that's actually a hybrid \u2014 mixing chemistries drops the corrosion package out of suspension and forms the gel that clogs the radiator and heater core. If you can't confirm what a bottle is, don't use it. And always cut concentrate 50/50 with distilled water, never tap; the minerals in tap water are exactly what the aluminum passages don't tolerate.\n\nIf the truck spends real time in Phoenix-grade summer heat or tows near the 7,650-lb rating, the coolant's condition matters more than the calendar. Pair a coolant inspection with your regular [service-interval checks](/db/gladiator/jt-service-intervals/), and if the engine has a history of running warm, rule out the [Pentastar oil-cooler/coolant crossover leak](/db/gladiator/pentastar-3-6-oil-cooler-leak/) before assuming the flush fixed everything.\n\nA JT that overheats after a flush is almost always trapping air, not short on coolant. The Pentastar's coolant passages sit high relative to the fill point, and air trapped at the thermostat or in the heater core reads as a phantom overheat or a heater that blows cold.\n\nBleed it deliberately: with the reservoir cap off and the front of the truck slightly nose-up (park on a gentle incline or ramps), idle the engine with the heater set to full hot and the fan on low. As the thermostat opens you'll see the level drop and bubbles rise \u2014 top off as it settles. Squeeze the upper radiator hose firmly several times to push trapped air toward the reservoir. Bring it fully to temp, let it cool completely, then recheck the cold level at the MAX line and repeat the warm-up once. Two heat-cool cycles clears nearly every air pocket. If the heater still blows lukewarm after that, there's a stubborn pocket in the heater core \u2014 the incline-and-idle cycle usually clears it on the third try.\n\n- **Air pockets**: The JT cooling system, like most modern vehicles with a sealed reservoir, can trap air in the heater core. If the heater blows lukewarm air after a coolant flush, there's an air pocket. Squeeze the upper radiator hose several times with the cap off to help burp air out.\n- **Petcock seal**: The plastic petcock on the radiator can weep after being disturbed. Inspect it closely after the first warm-up cycle. If it seeps, tighten it gently or replace it ($8\u201312 at any parts store).\n- **Overfilling**: Coolant expands significantly when hot. Fill to MAX when cold \u2014 if you fill above MAX when cold, the system pushes excess through the overflow when it heats up.\n\nMopar OAT antifreeze (1 gallon): ~$20\u201324. Spec-correct and the cleanest choice \u2014 no compatibility guesswork.\n\nZerex G-05 antifreeze (1 gallon): ~$18\u201322. OAT-compatible, widely available at auto parts stores, confirmed safe for Pentastar aluminum. This is a common substitute.\n\nPrestone Extended Life: ~$16\u201320. Verify it's the OAT or HOAT orange/yellow formulation \u2014 Prestone sells multiple types and the labels look similar.\n\nCoolant flush is a 60\u201390 minute job. Shop rate for a cooling system flush typically runs $80\u2013150. The DIY cost is under $30 if you already have a drain pan and funnel.","steps":["Let the engine cool completely \u2014 at least two hours after the last run. The cooling system is pressurized when hot; opening the cap on a warm engine causes dangerous steam and coolant spray.","Locate the radiator drain petcock on the lower passenger-side corner of the radiator. It's a plastic valve \u2014 turn counterclockwise to open. Position your drain pan under it. The JT cooling system holds approximately 11\u201312 quarts.","Open the coolant reservoir cap to allow the system to drain freely. The cap is on the translucent overflow reservoir on the driver's side of the engine bay \u2014 not the radiator directly.","If the outgoing fluid looks rusty or has debris, do a flush: close the petcock, fill the system with distilled water, run the engine to operating temp with the heater on full (to flush the heater core), let it cool, and drain again. Repeat until the outgoing water runs clear.","Close the petcock and refill with a 50/50 mix of Mopar OAT coolant (or Zerex G-05 compatible) and distilled water. Use distilled water \u2014 not tap water. Tap water contains minerals that accelerate scale buildup inside aluminum passages.","Fill to the MAX line on the overflow reservoir. The system will draw coolant from the reservoir as it comes to temperature.","With the reservoir cap off, start the engine and let it idle until the thermostat opens (coolant temp gauge reaches normal operating range). You'll see the coolant level drop as hot coolant circulates and air purges from the system. Add coolant to maintain the level.","Once the thermostat opens and the upper radiator hose feels hot to the touch, reinstall the reservoir cap.","Check for leaks at the petcock, hose connections, and reservoir cap. Let the engine cool and verify the reservoir level is between MIN and MAX."]},{"id":"jt-transfer-case-fluid","title":"Transfer Case Fluid Service \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"drivetrain","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":60},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","1/2\" drive ratchet or breaker bar","fluid pump (for fill)","drain pan","nitrile gloves","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","working under a lifted vehicle"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["drivetrain","transfer-case","maintenance","fluid","mp3022","mp3024"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar Transfer Case Lubricant (NV-226, 1 qt)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"Pennzoil Gear+ ATF for Transfer Case (compatible)","vendor":"Pennzoil","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Valvoline ATF+4 Automatic Transmission Fluid (1 qt)","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":10}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator JT Owner's Manual \u2014 Drivetrain Fluids","url":"https://www.mopar.com"},{"title":"Quadratec Gladiator JT Transfer Case Fluid Guide","url":"https://www.quadratec.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-locker-rear","jt-regear"],"summary":"The JT Gladiator runs an MP3022 (Sport/Willys) or MP3024 (Rubicon) transfer case depending on trim. Both use ATF (automatic transmission fluid), not gear oil \u2014 a mistake that ruins the unit quickly.","description":"Transfer case service is one of the most skipped fluid changes on the JT \u2014 most owners do engine oil, some do differential fluid, fewer do the transfer case. It's a mistake. The fluid inside the transfer case shears over time, especially under the heat and load generated during four-wheel-drive engagement in technical terrain. Fresh fluid restores the clutch pack feel and keeps the electronic shift system operating correctly. FCA recommends service at 30,000 miles or every 3 years for off-road use \u2014 street-only use gets a longer interval, but most JT owners aren't street-only.\n\n**Critical fluid specification:** The JT transfer case uses ATF+4 or equivalent DEXRON-VI. Do NOT use gear oil (75W-90, 75W-140) \u2014 the clutch packs inside the transfer case require ATF's friction modifiers to function correctly. Gear oil destroys them. Use Mopar ATF+4 (part number 68218057AC) or a compatible brand that meets the specification. Valvoline ATF+4 and Pennzoil Multi-Vehicle ATF are both acceptable alternatives.\n\nThe JT's transfer case sits between the transmission and the front and rear driveshafts, roughly centered under the cab. On a lifted JT with factory skidplates, the skidplate must be removed first \u2014 four mounting bolts. The drain plug is at the bottom of the transfer case; the fill plug is on the side, slightly higher. Both are typically 1/2\" drive square plugs (no socket required, use a 1/2\" ratchet directly). On some JT configurations, one or both may be hex bolt plugs \u2014 check before committing to a drain.\n\n- **Overfilling**: The weep method (fill until fluid seeps out the fill port) is accurate for the transfer case. Overfilling pressurizes the case and can push fluid past seals.\n- **Metal shavings on the drain plug**: A small amount of fine metallic paste on the drain plug is normal. Chunks or significant magnetic debris indicate internal wear \u2014 inspect more closely and consider having the case checked by a drivetrain shop.\n- **Grinding or delayed engagement in 4WD**: If the transfer case engages sluggishly, grinds when shifting between 2H/4H/4L, or refuses to transfer smoothly, a fluid change sometimes resolves it \u2014 degraded fluid loses the friction modifiers needed for smooth clutch pack engagement. If grinding continues after fresh fluid, the clutch packs may be worn.\n- **Skidplate alignment**: The factory skidplate bolts into specific locations. Reinstall before checking for leaks \u2014 the plate and transfer case share some mounting brackets on certain JT configurations.\n\nThe two cases take the same ATF+4 but differ in gearing and access:\n\n- **MP3022 (Command-Trac, Sport/Willys/Overland):** Part-time 4WD, 2.72:1 low range. Standard drain-and-fill.\n- **MP3024 (Rock-Trac, Rubicon/Mojave):** 4:1 low range for serious crawling, heavier-duty internals. Same fluid, same procedure, but the Rubicon's fuller skid package means more to remove for access.\n\nBoth hold roughly a pint. Both use ATF+4. Neither takes gear oil. If you [regear the axles](/db/gladiator/jt-regear/) or add a [rear locker](/db/gladiator/jt-locker-rear/), the transfer case fluid interval matters more \u2014 a locked, regeared truck puts more heat and shock load through the case.\n\n| Item | Spec |\n|---|---|\n| Drain plug torque | 20 ft-lbs |\n| Fill plug torque | 20 ft-lbs |\n| Fluid | ATF+4 (Mopar 68218057AC) |\n| Capacity | ~1 pt (0.5 L) |\n| Fill method | Weep \u2014 fill until fluid seeps from port |\n| Service interval (trail) | 30,000 mi / 3 yr |\n\nFold this into your broader [service interval schedule](/db/gladiator/jt-service-intervals/) so the transfer case does not become the fluid everyone forgets.\n\nMopar ATF+4 (OEM specification): ~$11\u201315 per quart at the dealer parts counter. One quart covers the JT transfer case plus a small margin.\n\nValvoline or Pennzoil ATF+4 alternative: ~$8\u201312 per quart. Same spec, lower cost, appropriate for this application.\n\nFluid pump kit (manual, one-time purchase): ~$10\u201315. Makes transfer case and differential fills manageable solo \u2014 don't try pouring from a quart bottle into a side port on an overhead angle.\n\nShop service for transfer case fluid: ~$80\u2013120 including parts. The DIY savings here are worth capturing \u2014 this is a 30-minute job once you've done it once.","steps":["Warm up the vehicle \u2014 drive 10\u201315 minutes so the fluid is warm and flows freely. Warm fluid drains more completely than cold.","Lift the vehicle on jack stands to working height. Access to the transfer case requires working under the vehicle.","If equipped with factory skidplates: remove the four mounting bolts and lower the plate. Secure it out of the way.","Position the drain pan under the transfer case drain plug.","Remove the FILL plug first \u2014 confirm you can break it loose before draining. If the fill plug is seized, don't drain \u2014 a drained transfer case with a stripped fill plug is a serious problem.","Remove the drain plug. Allow to drain completely (5\u20137 minutes for warm fluid).","Reinstall the drain plug. Torque to 20 ft-lbs. Do not overtighten the aluminum housing threads.","Using a fluid pump (hand pump with a hose works well), push ATF+4 into the fill port until fluid begins to weep out the fill hole. This indicates the case is full. The JT transfer case holds approximately 1 pint (0.5 L) \u2014 have at least 1 quart on hand.","Reinstall the fill plug. Torque to 20 ft-lbs.","Reinstall the skidplate. Lower the vehicle and check for leaks at both plugs during the first drive."]},{"id":"jt-locker-rear","title":"Rear Locker for the Gladiator JT \u2014 Dana 44 Options","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"drivetrain","type":"part","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":549,"high":999},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","bearing press or shop press","axle seal driver","dial indicator for backlash","RTV sealant"],"skills_required":["axle work","differential service","precision torquing","bearing and seal installation"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["locker","dana-44","traction","drivetrain"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB Air Locker for Dana 44 Rear (JT compatible)","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":849},{"name":"Eaton ELocker for Dana 44 Rear","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":649},{"name":"Detroit Truetrac Limited Slip for Dana 44","vendor":"Detroit Locker / Eaton","price_estimate_usd":549}],"citations":[{"title":"ARB Air Locker Dana 44 Install Guide","url":"https://www.arb.com.au/lockers/dana-44"},{"title":"Eaton ELocker Dana 44 Product Page","url":"https://www.eatondriveline.com/elocker"}],"alternatives":["jt-regear","jt-transfer-case-fluid"],"summary":"The Gladiator Rubicon already comes with Tru-Lok front and rear lockers \u2014 this guide is for Sport, Willys, Overland, and Mojave owners who want locking traction without stepping into Rubicon pricing.","description":"The rear axle on all non-Rubicon JT trims is a Dana 44 with an open differential. An open diff is fine for pavement and light gravel, but on uneven terrain \u2014 a side slope where one rear tire lifts, or a muddy wash where traction is patchy \u2014 an open diff sends power to the spinning tire instead of the tire with grip. A locker eliminates that. It mechanically (or electronically) connects both rear axle shafts so they turn together regardless of traction difference between the tires.\n\nThe ARB Air Locker ($849) is the performance choice: a pneumatic locker that engages and disengages via air pressure, giving you a true open differential on pavement and a locked rear when you activate it on the trail. ARB Air Lockers require a 12V air compressor \u2014 ARB sells a compatible twin-cylinder compressor kit ($369) that mounts under the hood. The combined cost puts the full ARB setup at roughly $1,200, but the on-demand engagement is the only design that genuinely delivers both behaviors without compromise.\n\nThe Eaton ELocker ($649) uses an electric solenoid to lock the differential on demand. It's cleaner to install than the ARB (no air lines), engages nearly as quickly, and costs less. The primary downside is that solenoid failure (which is uncommon but not unheard of) leaves you with a locked rear until you diagnose and repair it \u2014 the ARB at least fails open. The Detroit Truetrac ($549) is a helical gear limited-slip, not a true locker \u2014 it won't mechanically lock both shafts, but it biases torque to the gripping tire more aggressively than an open diff. It's the choice for owners who want improved traction without any driver activation required and don't need a fully locked differential.\n\n- **Backlash and preload are pass/fail, not close-enough.** The two numbers that make or break this job are pinion bearing preload and ring-and-pinion backlash (.006\"\u2013.010\" on the Dana 44). Too tight and the gears run hot and howl; too loose and they clunk and wear fast. A dial indicator is not optional here \u2014 if you don't have one and can't read a contact pattern with marking compound, this is the moment to hand the diff to a shop rather than guess.\n- **Mark the bearing caps before they come off.** The caps are machined in place and are side- and orientation-specific. Swapping or flipping them changes the bore and ruins your preload. Center-punch or paint-mark them before removal.\n- **Don't reuse the crush sleeve or old seals.** If your teardown disturbs the pinion, use the new collapsible spacer and axle seals from the install kit. Reused seals weep, and a reused crush sleeve won't hold preload.\n- **ARB fails open, ELocker fails locked.** Weigh that into the choice: an ARB compressor or air-line failure leaves you with a normal open diff and a drivable truck. An ELocker solenoid stuck engaged leaves you with a locked rear until you can address it \u2014 safe to limp home slowly, but plan your exit.\n\nParts land at $549 (Truetrac) to roughly $1,200 (ARB with compressor). Shop labor to install a locker in the rear Dana 44 runs 4\u20136 hours, typically $400\u2013700 depending on rates. The single biggest way to save money: **do the locker and a regear at the same time.** Both jobs require the exact same teardown \u2014 pulling the carrier, resetting backlash and preload \u2014 so combining them means paying that labor once instead of twice. If bigger tires are anywhere in your plan, read [Re-gearing the Gladiator JT](/db/gladiator/jt-regear/) before you commit to a locker-only install. For a factory-locked alternative and the electronic-locker comparison, see [Trail-using the Rubicon's rear locker only](/db/gladiator/jt-rear-locker-trail-use/) and [Dana 44 rear locker options](/db/gladiator/jt-dana44-rear-locker/).","steps":["Drain the rear differential fluid; loosen the cover bolts and pull the cover, catching the remaining fluid in a drain pan.","Remove the rear axle shafts: unbolt the backing plate retainers and slide the shafts out.","Remove the differential carrier: mark the bearing caps before removing (caps are side-specific), unbolt the caps, and carefully remove the carrier from the housing.","Remove the ring gear from the old carrier and install it on the new locker differential. Torque ring gear bolts to spec in a cross pattern \u2014 ring gear bolts are typically 70\u201390 ft-lbs.","Install the new carrier into the housing, checking bearing preload and ring/pinion backlash with a dial indicator. Backlash on Dana 44 should be .006\"\u2013.010\".","Reinstall bearing caps \u2014 torque to spec, position-specific.","For ARB: drill and tap the air fitting port in the diff housing per ARB's instructions. Route the air line through the axle housing to the solenoid.","Reinstall axle shafts and backing plates. Install new axle seals with a seal driver.","Clean the diff cover mating surface, apply RTV, and reinstall cover. Fill with gear oil (75W-140 synthetic recommended; approximately 1.5\u20132 quarts for Dana 44).","For ARB: mount and wire the compressor, run the air line to the locker, and test engagement before trail use."],"pros":["True locking traction on the trail eliminates the one-tire-spinning problem completely","ARB Air Locker delivers transparent on-road behavior \u2014 you'll forget it's there until you lock it","ELocker is a clean install without air lines and costs less than ARB","Significant traction upgrade for any JT trim without Rubicon's Tru-Lok system"],"cons":["Differential work requires precision \u2014 backlash and bearing preload errors cause gear noise and premature wear","ARB setup adds compressor cost and complexity; total cost exceeds $1,200 with compressor","Full locker in the rear changes on-road handling during parking maneuvers \u2014 turning radius increases when locked","Detroit Truetrac is not a full locker; it won't match the traction of ARB or ELocker in extreme situations"]},{"id":"jt-regear","title":"Re-Gearing the Gladiator JT \u2014 4.56 and 4.88 Ratios","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"drivetrain","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":12},"cost_usd":{"low":900,"high":2500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","bearing press","dial indicator","inch-pound torque wrench","gear marking compound","pinion depth tool set"],"skills_required":["differential rebuild","precision measurement","gear pattern reading","bearing and seal installation"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["regear","drivetrain","dana-44","gears"],"parts":[{"name":"Yukon Gear 4.88 Ring & Pinion for Dana 44 Front (JT)","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":249},{"name":"Yukon Gear 4.88 Ring & Pinion for Dana 44 Rear (JT)","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":249},{"name":"Yukon Master Install Kit for Dana 44 (bearings, seals, shims)","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":269},{"name":"Motive Gear 4.56 Ring & Pinion for Dana 44","vendor":"Motive Gear","price_estimate_usd":199}],"citations":[{"title":"Yukon Gear Dana 44 Ring and Pinion Install Guide","url":"https://www.yukongear.com/tech-info/install-instructions"},{"title":"Gear Ratio Selection Guide for Lifted Jeeps","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/c/journal/jeep-gear-ratio-guide"}],"alternatives":["jt-locker-rear","jt-transfer-case-fluid"],"summary":"Stock JT gearing (3.73) was calibrated for factory tires \u2014 run 37s without regearing and you're dragging a heavy truck through every gear while your fuel economy craters and your transmission runs hot.","description":"The Gladiator JT's stock gear ratio is 3.73 on most trims. That ratio was engineered around the factory 32\"\u201333\" tire diameter. When you step up to 35s, the effective final drive ratio gets numerically lower \u2014 the engine has to pull a larger tire through each rotation, and it doesn't have the multiplication it needs to do that efficiently. At 37s, the problem is pronounced: slow acceleration, labored highway cruising, poor low-end pulling power, and a transmission that works harder in every gear.\n\nThe JT's longer 137\" wheelbase also means more rotational mass in the driveshafts, and carrying a truck bed and its payload adds total system weight. The impact of running tall tires without a regear is more pronounced on the JT than on the JL because of that extra mass \u2014 you feel it earlier and more consistently. For a JT running 35s, 4.56 gears restore the drivetrain math. For a JT on 37s, especially in rock-crawling or regular trail use with gear in the bed, 4.88 is the correct answer.\n\nThe Dana 44 front (non-Rubicon trims have Dana 30 fronts \u2014 this guide covers the Dana 44 found on Rubicon and Mojave) and Dana 44 rear both need to be re-geared simultaneously. Mismatched front and rear ratios will cause binding in 4WD. Yukon Gear's 4.88 ring and pinion for the Dana 44 ($249 each axle) is well-proven and widely installed on JT/JL builds; the Yukon master install kit ($269) bundles all the bearings, seals, and shims needed so you're not sourcing pieces separately. Budget for professional installation unless you have prior differential rebuild experience \u2014 this is not a beginner job, and a poorly shimmed pinion will destroy a new gear set in short order.\n\nThe goal of a regear is to put your crawl ratio and highway RPM back where the factory intended for stock tires. Rough matches for the JT:\n\n| Tire size | Recommended ratio | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| 33\" (stock-ish) | 3.73 (factory) | No change needed |\n| 35\" | 4.56 | Restores acceleration and highway cruise near stock |\n| 37\" | 4.88 | Correct for trail/crawl use with gear in the bed |\n| 37\"+ heavy overland | 5.13 (specialty) | For very heavy, tall-tire builds \u2014 rare on the JT |\n\nGoing one step milder (staying 4.56 on 37s) leaves the truck feeling doughy under load; going aggressive (4.88 on 35s) buzzes at highway speed and costs a little fuel economy. Match the ratio to the tire you're actually running, not the tire you might buy someday. Confirm your fitment first in [37-inch tire fitment on the JT](/db/gladiator/jt-tire-fitment-37s/) or [35-inch fitment](/db/gladiator/jt-35-inch-tire-fitment/).\n\n- **Pinion depth is the one that ruins gear sets.** Backlash you can adjust after the fact; pinion depth you set once with the shim stack, and getting it wrong prints a bad contact pattern that whines and eventually fails. Read the gear pattern with marking compound and don't final-assemble until it's centered \u2014 this is why professional installation is worth it for a first-timer.\n- **Gear the axles as a matched pair.** Front and rear must run the same ratio or you'll get driveline binding in 4WD. This is also why a regear costs roughly double a single-axle job \u2014 you're paying for two complete teardowns.\n- **Respect the break-in.** New gears need a gentle first 500 miles with no heavy towing or hard trail load, then a fluid change to flush the metal from initial wear-in. Skipping the fluid change traps that grit in the diff.\n- **Do it with the locker if one's coming.** The teardown for a regear is identical to a locker install. If a locker is anywhere in your plan, combine them and pay the labor once \u2014 see [Rear Locker for the Gladiator JT](/db/gladiator/jt-locker-rear/). Add the correct friction modifier if you're running a limited-slip, and top off with 75W-140 synthetic per [transfer case and driveline fluid service](/db/gladiator/jt-transfer-case-fluid/).","steps":["Drain both differentials completely.","Front differential: remove front driveshaft, disconnect axle shafts, and remove differential cover.","Remove and mark pinion flange nut (count and record the rotational torque before removal for reference on reassembly bearing preload).","Remove the pinion shaft, bearing races, and carrier assembly. Clean the housing.","Install the new ring gear on the carrier; torque ring gear bolts to spec.","Set pinion depth using the new shim stack and a depth tool \u2014 this is the most critical measurement. Use gear marking compound to verify contact pattern before final assembly.","Install new pinion bearings, set pinion preload to spec using an inch-pound torque wrench on the pinion flange nut.","Install the carrier, set backlash (.006\"\u2013.010\" for Dana 44), verify contact pattern again.","Reinstall bearing caps and torque to spec. Install new axle seals.","Repeat for the rear differential.","Fill both axles with 75W-140 synthetic gear oil. Add ARB/locker additive if running a limited-slip unit.","Drive gently for the first 500 miles during gear break-in; re-inspect fluid at 500 miles and replace."],"pros":["Restores factory powertrain feel after tire upsizing \u2014 acceleration, highway cruising, and fuel economy all improve","4.88 gears dramatically improve crawl ratio on rock trails with 37s","JT's longer wheelbase and payload capacity make regearing more impactful vs. JL \u2014 the math favors it","Gear swap pairs naturally with a locker install (same labor, same differential teardown)"],"cons":["Professional installation strongly recommended \u2014 incorrect pinion depth and preload ruin new gear sets","Both axles must be geared simultaneously to avoid 4WD binding \u2014 costs double vs. a single axle swap","Total cost including labor typically runs $1,500\u2013$2,500 for parts and shop time on both axles","Non-Rubicon JT Sport/Willys/Overland have a Dana 30 front \u2014 regearing a Dana 30 is a different (and more limited) gear selection than the Dana 44"]},{"id":"jt-dual-battery-setup","title":"Dual Battery System for the Gladiator JT \u2014 Overland and Camp Power","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"electrical","type":"upgrade","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":900},"tools_required":["socket set","wire crimper and heat gun","multimeter","fuse holders","cable cutters","eye protection","wrench set"],"skills_required":["basic electrical","wire sizing and fusing","battery safety"],"build_tiers":["overland","moderate_trail","mild_trail"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["electrical","dual-battery","overland","aux-power","camp-power","battery"],"parts":[{"name":"Odyssey PC2150 AGM Battery (aux, 1150 CCA)","vendor":"Odyssey","price_estimate_usd":289},{"name":"REDARC BCDC1225D DC-DC Battery to Battery Charger (25A)","vendor":"REDARC","price_estimate_usd":239},{"name":"Ctek D250SE DC-DC Charger / Solar Combiner","vendor":"Ctek","price_estimate_usd":189},{"name":"Blue Sea Systems ML-ACR Automatic Charging Relay","vendor":"Blue Sea","price_estimate_usd":55},{"name":"4WD Products JT Bed Battery Tray","vendor":"4WD Products","price_estimate_usd":95}],"citations":[{"title":"REDARC BCDC1225D Installation Manual","url":"https://www.redarc.com.au"},{"title":"Blue Sea Systems ML-ACR Wiring Guide","url":"https://www.bluesea.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-alternator-battery-upgrade","jt-led-lighting-upgrade"],"summary":"A dual battery system on the JT pays off fast if you run a fridge, lights, air compressor, or recovery gear from camp \u2014 the stock battery wasn't sized for the accessory loads overlanders put on it.","description":"The Gladiator JT's factory electrical system is a 160A alternator paired with a 650 CCA AGM battery. That's adequate for driving with accessories on, but a camp night with a 12V fridge, lighting, and phone charging running for 8\u201310 hours will deplete a single battery to the point of unreliable starting. The answer is an auxiliary (aux) battery \u2014 a second battery dedicated to accessories, isolated from the starting circuit so the engine always has what it needs to start.\n\n**Two approaches differ fundamentally in how they charge the aux battery:**\n\n**Automatic Charging Relay (ACR):** Connects the two batteries when alternator voltage is detected (engine running), isolates them when voltage drops (engine off). Less expensive (~$55 for the relay), more straightforward to wire, but charges the aux battery at full alternator output \u2014 which can undercharge an AGM or lithium aux if the drive is short.\n\n**DC-DC Charger (REDARC BCDC, Ctek D250):** A smart charger that sits between the alternator and aux battery. It conditions the charge profile for the specific battery chemistry (AGM, lithium), can accept solar input through the same device, and protects against alternator damage from direct lithium charging. More expensive (~$180\u2013240) but the right answer for lithium aux batteries and extended overland use.\n\nFor an AGM aux battery with occasional weekend use: an ACR relay works. For a lithium battery or full-time overland rig: the DC-DC charger is required.\n\n1. Determine aux battery location. The JT truck bed gives options the Wrangler doesn't \u2014 a bed-mounted tray behind the cab is a common install that keeps the battery accessible and doesn't consume interior space. Some owners mount under-hood in the fender well, but the JT engine bay is tight.\n2. Install the battery tray in your chosen location. For bed installations, most trays bolt to the bed crossmember rails.\n3. Run a positive cable from the starter battery terminal, through a fuse holder positioned within 18 inches of the battery terminal, to the DC-DC charger input. Fuse the run at 40A (or per charger specs). Use minimum 8AWG wire for runs under 10 feet; go to 6AWG for longer runs.\n4. Run a negative return from the DC-DC charger back to chassis ground \u2014 a nearby body bolt or the negative terminal of the starter battery.\n5. Run positive and negative cables from the DC-DC charger output to the aux battery terminals. Fuse the positive run within 18 inches of the aux battery at 40A or per manufacturer spec.\n6. Connect the charger's ignition sense wire (if equipped) to an ignition-switched 12V source so the charger activates when the engine runs. Many owners tap the fuse block in the cab.\n7. Ground the aux battery negative to the vehicle chassis \u2014 a dedicated ground point near the battery location. Bond it back to the chassis, not to the body.\n8. Wire accessories to the aux battery through a dedicated fuse block. Size each fuse to the accessory circuit \u2014 a 12V fridge draws 3\u20135A, a compressor draws 15\u201340A. Never skip fusing individual circuits.\n9. Test: start the engine and verify the DC-DC charger's charge LED activates. Measure aux battery voltage with a multimeter \u2014 it should rise toward 14.2\u201314.8V as the charger works. Turn the engine off and verify aux voltage holds while running accessories.\n\n- **Wire sizing is non-negotiable**: Undersized wire generates heat under load \u2014 this is how electrical fires start. Use an automotive wire sizing chart. When in doubt, go one gauge heavier.\n- **Fuse every positive run**: A short circuit in an unfused run can cause a fire before the alternator trips. No positive wire should be unfused \u2014 ever.\n- **JT alternator note**: The 3.6L Pentastar JT has a variable-voltage alternator (also called smart charge). This means alternator output isn't a steady 14.4V \u2014 it modulates. Some older ACR relays interpret the low-voltage economy mode as \"engine off\" and disconnect the batteries prematurely. A DC-DC charger handles variable alternator output correctly; a basic ACR may not on 2020+ vehicles.\n- **Battery chemistry must match charger profile**: Charging an AGM with a charger set for flooded or lithium damages the battery. Confirm your DC-DC charger's profile matches your aux battery chemistry.\n\nBuy the battery to match a real amp-hour budget, not a round number that sounds generous. Add up what runs overnight with the engine off. A 12V compressor fridge averages 3-5A once it's cold-soaked, so 30-50Ah over a 10-hour camp night. LED interior and awning lights pull 1-2A. A vent fan, a heater blower, phone and camera charging - each adds a few amp-hours. A typical JT overland night lands around 40-70Ah of draw.\n\nThen account for usable depth, because rated capacity is not usable capacity. A flooded or AGM battery should not drop below roughly 50% state of charge if you want it to last - so a 100Ah AGM gives you about 50Ah before you're into damaging territory. LiFePO4 (lithium) tolerates 80-100% depth of discharge, so a 100Ah lithium delivers 80-100 usable Ah at roughly one-third the weight. That difference is why serious overlanders pay the lithium premium: a 100Ah lithium replaces a 200Ah AGM bank for the same usable energy and a fraction of the mass on the [payload budget](/db/gladiator/payload-towing-reality/).\n\nMatch the charge source to the same math. Drive two hours between camps and a 25A DC-DC charger replaces 40-50Ah - enough to break even on a moderate night. Camp stationary for days and you need solar input or a larger bank, because no amount of engine-off wiring recharges a battery. Sizing the [alternator and charging system](/db/gladiator/jt-alternator-battery-upgrade/) to the load is the other half of this equation, and [lighting loads](/db/gladiator/jt-led-lighting-upgrade/) are the draw most owners underestimate.\n\nThe Gladiator's bed is the reason a dual-battery build is more flexible here than on a Wrangler, but the location you pick drives the wiring, not the other way around. A bed-mounted tray behind the cab keeps the aux battery accessible, cool, and out of the cramped engine bay - the tradeoff is a longer positive run from the starter battery, which means heavier cable to hold voltage drop in check and a sealed, vibration-resistant box because the bed sees weather and dust. Route the cable through an existing grommet, never over a sharp bed-panel edge, and fuse it within 18 inches of each battery terminal.\n\nUnder-hood mounting in the fender area shortens the cable run and keeps everything in the charging zone, but the JT's 3.6L engine bay is tight and hot - heat shortens AGM life and pushes lithium outside its safe charge window on desert days. Whichever you choose, a bed-mounted battery adds 40-60 lbs high and rearward, which nibbles at the same [payload and tongue-weight budget](/db/gladiator/payload-towing-reality/) your camp gear and [bed storage](/db/gladiator/jt-cargo-bed-management/) already compete for. Weigh the build, not only the parts list.\n\nACR relay path (Blue Sea ML-ACR + Odyssey PC2150 AGM aux): ~$350\u2013400 for parts, plus wire, fuse holders, and tray. The lowest-cost path to a working dual battery system.\n\nDC-DC charger path (REDARC BCDC1225D + Odyssey PC2150): ~$550\u2013600. The right answer for overlanders who may later add solar \u2014 the REDARC has a built-in solar input.\n\nLithium aux (100Ah LiFePO4) + REDARC: ~$750\u2013900 depending on lithium brand. Weight savings and deeper discharge capability worth it for serious builds.\n\nA shop that does off-road electrical work will charge $400\u2013600 in labor for a dual battery install, depending on complexity and routing. With good wiring and a weekend afternoon, this is within reach of an owner comfortable with basic electrical work."},{"id":"jt-alternator-battery-upgrade","title":"Alternator and Battery Upgrade \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"electrical","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2.5},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":450},"tools_required":["10mm socket","13mm socket","ratchet","extension","memory saver (optional)","multimeter"],"skills_required":["basic electrical","basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["electrical","battery","alternator","overlanding","power"],"parts":[{"name":"Odyssey PC1750 12V AGM Battery","vendor":"Odyssey","price_estimate_usd":289},{"name":"Optima YellowTop D35 Deep Cycle AGM Battery","vendor":"Optima","price_estimate_usd":199},{"name":"Interstate MTX-65/H6 AGM Battery","vendor":"Interstate","price_estimate_usd":189},{"name":"Painless Wiring 50102 Dual Battery Kit","vendor":"Painless","price_estimate_usd":89}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator JT Electrical System Overview \u2014 Mopar","url":"https://www.mopar.com"},{"title":"Odyssey Battery Gladiator Fitment Guide","url":"https://www.odysseybattery.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-dual-battery-setup","jt-led-lighting-upgrade"],"summary":"The stock JT electrical system handles factory loads fine, but the moment you add a winch, fridge, light bar, and a dual-zone air compressor, you're asking more than the factory 160-amp alternator was sized for.","description":"The Gladiator JT comes from the factory with a Group 48/H6 lead-acid battery and a 160-amp alternator. For a stock truck doing highway miles, that's adequate. For an overlander running a 12V fridge around the clock, a compressor air locker, LED light bar, and recharging accessories through USB, the math stops working \u2014 especially during slow trail speeds when alternator output drops significantly.\n\nThe first upgrade most JT owners make is swapping to an AGM (absorbed glass mat) battery. AGM batteries offer higher cold cranking amps, better deep-cycle recovery, and longer service life under repeated partial discharging \u2014 which is exactly how an overlanding electrical system uses a battery. The factory lead-acid battery isn't designed to be discharged below 50% repeatedly; an AGM handles it without permanent capacity loss.\n\n**Group size:** The JT uses a Group 48/H6 battery. Do not try to cram a Group 65 \u2014 the battery tray and hold-down won't accommodate it and the battery can shift. The Odyssey PC1750, Optima YellowTop D35, and Interstate MTX-65 all fit the factory tray.\n\n**Alternator:** The stock 160-amp unit is adequate for most single-battery setups. If you're running a winch that pulls 400+ amps and a lithium secondary battery, consider a high-output alternator upgrade (Nations, Mechman, or Balmar offer JT-specific units in the 200\u2013250 amp range at $350\u2013500). For the majority of overlanders, the battery upgrade alone is the right move \u2014 the alternator holds up.\n\n1. Make sure the engine is off and the key is out of the ignition. If your JT has a push-button start, remove the key fob from the vehicle to prevent accidental accessory activation.\n2. If you're concerned about losing radio presets or throttle body adaptations, connect a memory saver to the OBD-II port before disconnecting the battery.\n3. Locate the battery in the engine bay on the driver's side. Remove the negative cable first (black, 10mm bolt). Secure it away from the terminal \u2014 don't let it touch the battery post.\n4. Remove the positive cable (red, 10mm bolt). Then remove the battery hold-down bracket (typically one 13mm bolt).\n5. Lift the old battery out. The H6 weighs about 45 lbs \u2014 get a grip before lifting.\n6. Clean the battery tray with a wire brush if there's corrosion. Apply a thin coat of battery terminal protector spray to the tray and terminal clamps.\n7. Set the new AGM battery in the tray, install the hold-down bracket, and torque it snug \u2014 not so tight that you crack the battery case.\n8. Connect the positive cable first, then the negative. Apply terminal protector spray to both posts.\n9. Start the engine and let it run for 15\u201320 minutes to allow the alternator to put an initial charge on the new battery.\n10. Check voltage with a multimeter at idle: 13.5\u201314.5V is normal charging range. Below 13V with the engine running points to an alternator issue.\n\nIf your overlanding load consistently draws down the battery overnight, a dual battery setup is the answer \u2014 not a bigger single battery. A properly wired dual system uses a battery isolator or DC-DC charger to keep the secondary battery available for accessories while protecting the starting battery.\n\nThe Painless Wiring dual battery kit ($89) provides the isolator and wiring for a basic split-charge setup. A proper DC-DC charger (Redarc, Renogy) runs $120\u2013250 but charges the secondary battery more efficiently, especially at low alternator RPM \u2014 relevant for overland camping where you run accessories at idle.\n\n- **AGM charging voltage**: AGM batteries require a charger or alternator that doesn't exceed 14.7V. Most factory alternators are within spec; if you've modified the electrical system, verify the charging voltage.\n- **Terminal orientation**: Confirm positive and negative terminal positions match the factory orientation before installing. A reversed battery in an H6 housing will require different cable routing.\n- **Memory reset**: After disconnecting and reconnecting the battery, the JT may run slightly rough for the first few miles while the throttle body adaptation resets. This is normal.\n\nBefore spending money, add up what you actually run. Size a JT electrical system by totaling the sustained draw of your accessories and comparing it against what the alternator can spare at trail RPM \u2014 not at highway cruise, where output is highest and least representative of how you camp.\n\nTypical sustained draws: a 12V compressor fridge pulls 3\u20135A while the compressor cycles, an air-locker compressor 30\u201340A in short bursts, a 40W LED pod pair around 6\u20138A, a cab USB and charging load 5\u201310A, and a mounted winch 250\u2013450A during a pull (but only for seconds at a time). The steady-state overnight load that flattens a battery is the fridge plus parasitic draw \u2014 usually 2\u20134A averaged. Over a 12-hour night that's 24\u201348Ah, which is roughly half the usable capacity of a single Group H6 AGM before you're into harmful deep-discharge territory.\n\nThat math is the reason the fix for an overnight-drain problem is a second battery, not a bigger starting battery \u2014 see the wiring approach in [/db/gladiator/jt-dual-battery-setup/](/db/gladiator/jt-dual-battery-setup/). The starting battery should never double as the house battery. Reserve the high-output alternator for the specific case where you run a winch hard and recharge a lithium bank at low RPM, where the factory 160\u2013180A unit can't keep the starter battery topped while also feeding a DC-DC charger. Frequent winching stresses the charging system harder than lights ever will \u2014 plan the electrical budget around a recovery day, not a fair-weather cruise. Winch selection and current draw is covered in [/db/gladiator/jt-winch/](/db/gladiator/jt-winch/).\n\nFor the auxiliary battery, the choice is AGM or lithium iron phosphate (LiFePO4), and the tradeoffs are real in both directions. AGM is cheaper up front ($185\u2013290), tolerates the JT's factory charging voltage without a special profile, and handles cold well. Its downsides are weight (an H6 AGM is ~45 lbs) and usable depth \u2014 plan to pull no more than 50% before recharging, so a 65Ah AGM gives you about 32Ah of real overnight capacity.\n\nLiFePO4 flips those numbers. A 100Ah lithium battery weighs 25\u201330 lbs, delivers 80\u201390Ah of usable capacity, and lasts several times as many cycles. The catches: it costs $500\u2013900, it needs a DC-DC charger (Redarc BCDC1225D or Renogy 20\u201340A, $150\u2013300) because the factory alternator voltage curve won't fully or safely charge lithium, and most LiFePO4 chemistries won't accept a charge below freezing without an internal heater. For a JT that camps in shoulder-season cold or above the snow line, confirm the battery has cold-charge protection or a heating element before buying.\n\nThe honest recommendation for most JT overlanders: start with a quality AGM secondary and a basic isolator, then move to lithium and a DC-DC charger only once you've measured your actual overnight draw and found the AGM's usable capacity is the real limiting factor. Auxiliary lighting is a common companion upgrade \u2014 wire it correctly per [/db/gladiator/jt-led-lighting-upgrade/](/db/gladiator/jt-led-lighting-upgrade/) so the new loads don't outrun the battery you chose.\n\nOdyssey PC1750 (top-tier AGM, direct Group H6 fitment): ~$280\u2013300. The recommended choice for builds with heavy electrical loads.\n\nOptima YellowTop D35: ~$185\u2013220. A solid step up from stock at a more accessible price point.\n\nInterstate MTX-65: ~$175\u2013195 from Interstate dealers. Good warranty, widely available for replacement.\n\nBattery replacement is a 30-minute job at home with no special tools. Shop rate for a battery swap runs $30\u201360 in labor beyond the battery cost \u2014 unnecessary for most owners."},{"id":"jt-led-lighting-upgrade","title":"LED Lighting Upgrade \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"electrical","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":89,"high":450},"tools_required":["panel removal tools","wire stripper","crimping tool or heat gun (for heat shrink)","multimeter","socket set (10mm)"],"skills_required":["basic 12V wiring","panel removal"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["electrical","lighting","led","off-road-lights","auxiliary-lights"],"parts":[{"name":"Baja Designs Squadron Sport LED Pod (pair)","vendor":"Baja Designs","price_estimate_usd":249},{"name":"KC HiLiTES FLEX ERA 3 LED (pair)","vendor":"KC HiLiTES","price_estimate_usd":299},{"name":"Rigid Industries SR-Series Pro LED (pair)","vendor":"Rigid Industries","price_estimate_usd":199},{"name":"Diode Dynamics SS3 Pro LED Pod (pair)","vendor":"Diode Dynamics","price_estimate_usd":299},{"name":"Rugged Ridge Spartacus LED Light Bar (50\")","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":449}],"citations":[{"title":"Baja Designs Squadron Sport Product Page","url":"https://www.bajadesigns.com"},{"title":"Diode Dynamics SS3 JT Gladiator Application","url":"https://www.diodedynamics.com"},{"title":"Rigid Industries JT Mount Compatibility Guide","url":"https://www.rigidindustries.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-alternator-battery-upgrade","jt-dual-battery-setup"],"summary":"Auxiliary LED lighting is one of the first modifications JT owners reach for \u2014 the factory headlights are adequate for highway driving and useless for off-road. Quality pods mounted to a front bumper or A-pillars expand your visible range by 300\u2013500 feet in conditions where factory lights illuminate maybe 150 feet.","description":"The JT Gladiator's electrical system is capable of handling substantial auxiliary lighting loads \u2014 the alternator on the 3.6L produces 180A, which leaves plenty of headroom for LED pods. The limiting factor is wiring gauge and relay quality, not the alternator. A pair of 40W LED pods wired through an undersized switch (no relay) can melt wiring and create a fire risk. Wire it correctly the first time.\n\n**Where to mount lights on the JT**\n\nThe factory steel bumper on Sport and Willys trims includes fog light provisions that can accept 3\" round pods directly. The Rubicon trail-rated bumper is similar. The most popular mounting positions for auxiliary lights are:\n\n- **Front bumper fog light pockets**: Takes 3\" round or small rectangular pods. Diode Dynamics SS3 Pro is designed specifically for these pockets with plug-and-play harness kits that tie into the factory fog light circuit.\n- **A-pillar mounts**: Positions the lights higher for better throw angle, useful for trail use. Adds roof-mounted pod options as well.\n- **Roof rack crossbar**: For a full light bar \u2014 the JT's longer bed makes this less common than on the JL, but it's functional for overland setups.\n- **Aftermarket front bumper**: If replacing the stock bumper, choose one with factory LED pod provisions (RCI Metalworks, Warn, Fab Fours all offer JT-specific options with built-in pod mounts).\n\n**Pod selection: what matters**\n\nThe measurable difference between quality pods and discount pods is beam pattern, thermal management, and longevity under vibration. A $35 pair of pods from an unnamed brand will appear bright in a parking lot and develop flickering or dead elements within a season of trail use. Baja Designs, Rigid Industries, KC HiLiTES, and Diode Dynamics have all proven durable in trail environments with consistent beam performance.\n\n**Beam pattern matters more than raw lumens**: A spot beam throws light far forward in a narrow cone \u2014 useful for high-speed dirt roads. A flood beam spreads light wide at shorter distance \u2014 better for low-speed obstacle reading in technical terrain. Most JT owners run a combination: spot pods on the outer bumper mounts for distance, flood pods or a light bar for close-in visibility.\n\n1. Run a dedicated wire from the battery positive to a relay (Bosch-style 30/40A automotive relay, ~$8 each). Do not wire high-current lights directly through a switch \u2014 the switch carries only the relay trigger current, not the light current.\n2. Run the relay output wire (14-gauge minimum for 40W pods, 12-gauge preferred for a bar) forward to the light location with a fuse at the battery (inline holder, 30A fuse for a pair of 40W pods).\n3. Run a switched 12V trigger wire from the relay to a switch location in the cab. Most JT owners use the factory switch blank on the dash (center console switch bank) \u2014 a Diode Dynamics or Rough Country rocker switch fits the factory cutout on the JT and connects to the relay trigger.\n4. Ground the lights to the frame or a chassis ground point near the lights. Do not ground to body panels \u2014 the JT's unibody-adjacent construction creates ground loops on some body ground points.\n5. Use waterproof connectors (Deutsch DT connectors or equivalent) at any exterior connection point. The JT sees water crossing and rain-on exposure when doors are off \u2014 automotive butt connectors without heat-shrink will corrode within one season.\n\nDiode Dynamics makes a complete plug-and-play SS3 kit for the JT that uses the factory fog light connector. The kit includes pods, brackets, a wiring harness, and a rocker switch that fits the factory switch bank. Install is under 90 minutes and requires no relay wiring \u2014 the factory fog light circuit already has proper relay protection. This is the correct starting point for anyone who wants quality pods without custom wiring.\n\nAuxiliary lights that are wired to work on the street can put you on the wrong side of the law and blind oncoming drivers, so treat aiming and switching as part of the install rather than an afterthought. In most states, forward-facing off-road pods and light bars must be covered or wired so they cannot operate on public roads \u2014 the common approach is to wire the aux circuit through the high-beam signal or a dedicated switch so the pods only come on with high beams off-pavement, and to run opaque covers on a light bar for road use. Check your state's specific statute; enforcement varies widely.\n\nAim matters as much as legality. Pods thrown on and pointed level will light up the underside of every road sign and reflect glare straight back at you in dust or fog. Aim spot beams slightly down so the hot center lands 100\u2013200 feet ahead on the trail, and aim a flood or the near half of a combo bar closer in. Do the aiming at night against a wall or down an actual trail, not in the driveway at noon. If you added pods to the factory fog pockets, remember they share the aim of a fog light \u2014 low and wide \u2014 which is correct for close terrain but wrong if you wanted distance throw.\n\nMatching beam pattern to terrain is where a lighting setup either transforms night driving or wastes money. The three patterns \u2014 spot (narrow, far), flood or wide (broad, near), and driving or combo (a blend) \u2014 each solve a different problem, and the right build usually mixes them.\n\nFor fast desert and graded dirt roads, run spot pods on the outer positions for distance and a driving pattern in the center so you see the road unfolding at speed. For technical rock crawling, throw is useless \u2014 you want wide flood coverage right in front of the tires and out to the sides to read line and spotter hand signals, so a pair of wide pods low on the bumper beats any long-range bar. For general overland and camp-to-camp trail running, a combo bar or a spot-plus-flood pod pair covers both, which is the setup that makes sense for most JT owners.\n\nColor temperature is the last variable. Cool white (5000\u20136500K) throws farthest and reads cleanest on dry nights; selectable or amber (3000K) cuts through dust, fog, and falling snow far better because the shorter cool wavelengths scatter back at you. Overlanders who run dusty convoys or shoulder-season storms increasingly choose amber or a switchable pod for exactly that reason. Whatever combination you land on, size the wiring and charging system to match \u2014 the load math and when a battery or alternator upgrade becomes necessary is in [/db/gladiator/jt-alternator-battery-upgrade/](/db/gladiator/jt-alternator-battery-upgrade/), and if the lights run at camp off the engine, plan a second battery per [/db/gladiator/jt-dual-battery-setup/](/db/gladiator/jt-dual-battery-setup/).\n\nDiode Dynamics SS3 Sport plug-and-play kit (pair, fog light pockets): ~$289. Best value entry point for quality pods on the JT.\n\nRigid Industries SR-Series Pro pair: ~$199. Compact, waterproof to IP68, suitable for bumper mounts with a standalone relay harness.\n\nBaja Designs Squadron Sport pair: ~$249. Well-regarded for beam pattern quality and vibration resistance. The overland community standard.\n\nKC HiLiTES FLEX Era 3 pair: ~$299. Versatile mounting, adjustable beam pattern via swappable lenses \u2014 useful if use case shifts from trail to highway.\n\nCustom wiring harness (relay, wire, connectors, fuse holder): ~$25\u201340 in parts. Every light install should have a dedicated harness \u2014 this is not where to cut costs."},{"id":"jt-air-intake-upgrade","title":"Cold Air Intake Upgrade \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT 3.6L","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"engine","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":199,"high":450},"tools_required":["socket set (8mm, 10mm)","flathead screwdriver or trim tool","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["engine","intake","performance","3.6L","air-intake"],"parts":[{"name":"K&N 77-Series High Flow Intake \u2014 JT Gladiator 3.6L","vendor":"K&N","price_estimate_usd":399},{"name":"aFe Power Momentum GT Cold Air Intake \u2014 JT 3.6L","vendor":"aFe Power","price_estimate_usd":349},{"name":"Volant Cold Air Intake with Powercore Filter \u2014 JT 3.6L","vendor":"Volant","price_estimate_usd":449},{"name":"Mishimoto Performance Air Intake \u2014 JT 3.6L","vendor":"Mishimoto","price_estimate_usd":279}],"citations":[{"title":"aFe Power Gladiator JT Intake Dyno Data","url":"https://www.afepower.com"},{"title":"K&N 77-Series JT Product Page","url":"https://www.knfilters.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-oil-change-pentastar","pentastar-3-6-oil-cooler-leak"],"summary":"A cold air intake on the JT Gladiator 3.6L will get you 8\u201314 horsepower under the right conditions and a throttle response improvement you'll notice in daily driving. It will not transform the truck, and the honest gains are at the top of the RPM range \u2014 not where most Gladiator owners spend their time.","description":"The JT's 3.6L Pentastar makes 285 hp factory. The intake system is restrictive by design \u2014 FCA optimized it for noise, not airflow. A cold air intake routes the filter box away from underhood heat and uses a larger-diameter intake tube to reduce restriction. The gains are real: third-party dynos from aFe and K&N consistently show 8\u201312 whp increases on the 3.6L. The throttle response improvement \u2014 faster pedal response at light throttle \u2014 is arguably more noticeable than the peak power number in street driving.\n\nWhat a cold air intake won't do: add meaningful low-end torque (the gains are concentrated above 3,500 RPM), improve fuel economy significantly (any gain is offset by the tendency to use the extra power), or change anything about the JT's fundamental character as a truck. The 3.6L Pentastar is an efficient naturally aspirated engine \u2014 the intake is the right first modification, but the returns are modest.\n\n**Emission compliance note:** Confirm the intake kit is CARB-compliant if you're in California or another CARB-state. Most major brands (K&N, aFe, Mishimoto) offer CARB-certified versions. An aftermarket intake on a JT that isn't CARB-certified in an emissions-tested state can trigger a check engine light or emissions failure.\n\n1. Disconnect the negative battery terminal. This prevents the ECU from throwing intake MAF codes during the install and resets learned fuel trims so the ECU can adapt to the new airflow characteristics.\n2. Remove the factory airbox lid. On the JT 3.6L, the lid is held by two 8mm bolts at the rear and two friction clips at the front. Lift it straight up after loosening.\n3. Disconnect the mass airflow sensor (MAF) connector from the factory intake tube. Squeeze the release tab and pull straight back \u2014 these connectors are plastic and break if levered sideways.\n4. Loosen the hose clamp securing the factory intake tube to the throttle body. Slide the tube off the throttle body elbow.\n5. Remove the factory airbox lower section \u2014 typically two 10mm bolts into the fender well.\n6. Follow the kit-specific instructions for your chosen intake. Most kits route the filter toward the fender or the front of the engine bay with a conical element in place of the factory box.\n7. Transfer the MAF sensor from the factory tube to the new tube. Typically two 8mm Torx screws. Handle the MAF element carefully \u2014 the sensing wire is fragile.\n8. Connect all couplers and tighten hose clamps hand-tight, then snug with a screwdriver (avoid over-tightening rubber couplers \u2014 they split).\n9. Reconnect the negative battery terminal. Start the engine and check for intake air leaks (hissing sound at idle, rough idle) before driving.\n\nNo. Unlike a turbocharged engine, the naturally aspirated 3.6L Pentastar does not need a supporting ECU tune to run an aftermarket intake safely. The stock ECU reads actual airflow through the relocated MAF sensor and adjusts fuel delivery through its normal closed-loop trims. As long as the MAF sensor is transferred correctly and mounted at the factory-specified distance from any bends in the new tube, the truck compensates on its own within a few drive cycles.\n\nTwo caveats that get people into trouble:\n\n- **MAF placement matters.** Every quality kit specifies where the MAF bung sits relative to the tube's bends because turbulent air across the sensor element reads inaccurately. Installing the sensor in the wrong bung, or flipping its orientation, produces a lean or rich condition the ECU can only partly trim out. If you get a persistent fuel-trim code (P0171/P0174) after an intake install, suspect MAF placement before anything else.\n- **A tune is for other goals.** People who tune a JT are usually chasing gains from larger tires (re-gearing the speedometer and shift points) or want throttle-response and transmission-behavior changes \u2014 not because the intake demands it. If you eventually re-gear for 37s, handle that as its own project; see the rear-end work in [/db/gladiator/jt-regear/](/db/gladiator/jt-regear/).\n\n- **Hydrolocking risk with open filter intakes**: A filter mounted low in the engine bay can ingest water during a water crossing. If your JT goes through water deeper than door-sill level, a sealed-box intake (Volant with sealed Powercore filter, or a factory airbox if you're going deep) is safer than an open conical filter. Many JT trail owners run an open intake on the street and swap back to the factory box for known water events.\n- **MAF codes after install**: A P0100 or P0102 code shortly after install usually means the MAF connector wasn't fully seated. Recheck the connector first. If the code persists, the ECU needs more drive time to adapt to the new airflow curve.\n- **K&N oiling**: If running a K&N oiled filter, don't over-oil during maintenance cleans. Excess oil migrates onto the MAF sensor and causes lean codes. Use the K&N recharger kit as directed \u2014 thin coats only.\n\nThe maintenance burden depends entirely on which filter type you chose, and this is where trail use changes the math.\n\n**Oiled cotton-gauze filters (K&N, aFe oiled):** clean and re-oil roughly every 30,000 street miles, but far more often in dusty trail conditions \u2014 a JT that runs washboard dirt roads weekly can load a filter in a fraction of that. Cleaning requires the brand's recharger kit: spray cleaner, rinse from the clean side out, dry fully (never with compressed air \u2014 it tears the media), then apply a thin, even coat of oil and let it wick for 20 minutes before reinstalling. The single most common mistake is over-oiling, which migrates onto the MAF sensor and throws lean codes. If you already have MAF-related codes on an oiled filter, clean the MAF element with dedicated MAF cleaner before assuming the sensor failed.\n\n**Dry synthetic filters (aFe Pro Dry S, Volant Powercore):** no oil, no MAF contamination risk. Clean with compressed air from the clean side or a mild wash per the manufacturer, then reinstall dry. For anyone spending real time in dust, a dry filter is the lower-hassle, lower-risk choice, which is part of why the sealed Volant is the pick for water and dust exposure.\n\nWhatever filter you run, inspect it at every oil change \u2014 pair it with the interval in [/db/gladiator/jt-oil-change-pentastar/](/db/gladiator/jt-oil-change-pentastar/) so it becomes a habit rather than an afterthought. A clogged filter costs you the airflow gain you paid for and, worse, an unfiltered gap from a poorly seated element feeds grit straight into the engine.\n\nMishimoto intake: ~$279. Solid OEM+ quality, available in polished aluminum. The value option in this category.\n\naFe Momentum GT: ~$349. Dyno-tested gains, well-fitting for the JT, available with dry or oiled filter elements.\n\nK&N 77-Series: ~$399. The brand recognition premium is real, but so is the support and filter replacement availability.\n\nVolant with Powercore (sealed): ~$449. The correct choice if the JT sees water crossings. The sealed box provides an air source above the water line and the Powercore filter is dry-element (no oiling required).\n\nInstall is consistently under 90 minutes for anyone who's opened an engine bay before. Shop labor for this job is a waste \u2014 the complexity doesn't justify it."},{"id":"jt-oil-change-pentastar","title":"Oil Change \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT 3.6L Pentastar V6","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"engine","type":"procedure","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":35,"high":75},"tools_required":["oil filter wrench","drain pan","funnel","rags","13/16\" socket or oil filter cap (PN: 4884899AC)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["engine","maintenance","oil","filter","pentastar"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar Oil Filter \u2014 PN 4884899AC (fits 3.6L JT)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":14},{"name":"Mobil 1 0W-20 Advanced Full Synthetic (5 qt jug)","vendor":"Mobil 1","price_estimate_usd":32},{"name":"Pennzoil Platinum 0W-20 Full Synthetic (5 qt)","vendor":"Pennzoil","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Castrol Edge 0W-20 Full Synthetic (5 qt)","vendor":"Castrol","price_estimate_usd":29}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator JT Owner's Manual \u2014 3.6L Oil Specifications","url":"https://www.jeep.com/content/dam/fca-brands/na/jeep/en_us/2022/owner-manuals/2022-jeep-gladiator-owners-manual.pdf"},{"title":"FCA 3.6L Pentastar V6 Service Manual \u2014 Engine Lubrication","url":"https://www.stellantis.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-spark-plug-replacement","jt-service-intervals"],"summary":"The Gladiator's 3.6L Pentastar V6 requires full synthetic oil \u2014 0W-20 is the factory spec. Using the correct viscosity matters more than the brand; using conventional oil in this engine is a mistake.","description":"The 3.6L Pentastar powers every JT Gladiator. It's a robust, well-proven engine with one known sensitivity: it requires full synthetic oil at all times. The tight tolerances in the Pentastar's variable valve timing (VVT) system are designed around synthetic oil's viscosity characteristics \u2014 conventional oil doesn't flow fast enough at cold start to properly lubricate the VVT actuators, and degraded synthetic causes sludging in the VVT passages more readily than the spec'd fluid.\n\nJeep's oil life monitor (OLM) uses engine parameters (temperature cycles, idling time, load) to estimate when the oil should be changed. For most drivers, this works out to roughly 7,000\u201310,000 miles between changes. For a Gladiator doing heavy trail work with sustained low-speed high-load operation, pull the interval in to 5,000\u20137,500 miles \u2014 the OLM doesn't fully account for trail-use abuse.\n\n**Oil capacity:** 5.9 quarts with filter change. Have 6 quarts on hand \u2014 drain capacity varies slightly by how long the oil drains.\n\n**Oil filter:** The Pentastar uses a cartridge-style oil filter (not a spin-on canister). The housing is on the front of the engine, passenger side, accessible from above with a 13/16\" socket or the Mopar filter cap tool (PN: 4884899AC). Mopar's OEM filter fits correctly; WIX 57356, Fram Ultra XG10575, and Purolator Boss PBL14621 are compatible aftermarket alternatives.\n\n- **Overtightened drain plug**: Torque to 20 ft-lbs \u2014 that's hand-tight plus about 1/4 turn. Overtightening strips the aluminum pan threads. If the threads feel suspect, use a thread repair kit (Timesert, not Helicoil) before the next change.\n- **Filter housing O-ring**: If you forget to replace the O-ring and the old one is compromised, the housing will weep oil. This is the most common cause of post-oil-change leaks on the Pentastar.\n- **Underfill vs. overfill**: Overfilling by more than 1 quart can cause the crankshaft to whip oil into foam, reducing lubrication. The JT's 5.9-qt capacity is precise \u2014 add, check the stick, add again in small amounts.\n\nThe factory spec for the 3.6L Pentastar in the JT is 0W-20 full synthetic, and that is the viscosity to run in almost every case. It's chosen for cold-start flow into the VVT phasers and for the fuel-economy targets the engine was certified against. The brand matters far less than the specification \u2014 Mobil 1, Pennzoil Platinum, and Castrol Edge in 0W-20 full synthetic are all correct.\n\nWhere owners ask about deviating:\n\n- **Hot climates and heavy towing.** Some Gladiator owners in desert heat or those towing at capacity in summer step up to a 5W-30 full synthetic for a slightly thicker film at operating temperature. This is a defensible choice for a truck that lives under sustained high load, and the Pentastar tolerates it, but understand you're trading a little cold-start flow and a fraction of fuel economy for it. If the truck is under warranty, staying on 0W-20 keeps you aligned with the spec.\n- **Cold climates.** Do not go thicker than 0W-20 in genuine cold. The 0W winter rating is what gets oil to the top end on a sub-freezing start; a 5W or 10W cold rating flows slower when you need it most.\n\nOne rule that isn't negotiable: this engine runs full synthetic only. Conventional or synthetic-blend oil in a Pentastar sludges the VVT passages and contributes to the oil-system problems the platform is known for \u2014 including the housing failure covered in [/db/gladiator/pentastar-3-6-oil-cooler-leak/](/db/gladiator/pentastar-3-6-oil-cooler-leak/).\n\nEvery drain is a free diagnostic if you pay attention. Before the pan drains, look and smell:\n\n- **Milky, coffee-with-cream color** means coolant is entering the oil \u2014 on the Pentastar, suspect the oil filter housing before anything else.\n- **A gritty or metallic sheen** in the stream or on the drain plug magnet points to internal wear worth investigating.\n- **A sharp, gasoline smell** suggests fuel dilution, common on short-trip trucks that never fully warm the oil.\n\nFor a trail truck that works hard, a used-oil analysis (Blackstone or similar, roughly $30\u201335) once or twice is worth it. Pull a sample mid-stream into the lab's bottle, and the report tells you wear-metal levels and whether your interval is right for how you actually drive. It's the honest way to decide whether to hold the factory oil-life-monitor interval or pull it in \u2014 pair the result with the interval guidance in [/db/gladiator/jt-service-intervals/](/db/gladiator/jt-service-intervals/) rather than guessing.\n\nMobil 1 0W-20 (5 qt jug): ~$30\u201335. Industry-standard quality at a reasonable price.\n\nMopar OEM filter: ~$12\u201316 at the dealer or through online Mopar parts suppliers. The correct specification, not worth compromising on.\n\nFull DIY kit (6 qt oil + OEM filter + new drain plug washer): ~$50\u201355. A dealer oil change on a JT typically runs $75\u2013100 depending on location. DIY saves $25\u201350 per interval.","steps":["Warm the engine for 2\u20133 minutes. Warm oil drains faster and carries more suspended particles with it. Don't change oil on a cold engine if you can avoid it.","Position your drain pan under the oil drain plug, located on the passenger-side rear of the oil pan. The drain plug uses a 13mm hex head.","Remove the drain plug and let the oil drain for 5\u201310 minutes. Inspect the old oil \u2014 metallic particles, a milky color (water intrusion), or a burnt smell are worth noting.","Replace the drain plug washer if your replacement plug kit includes one (or on every other change). Reinstall the plug and torque to 20 ft-lbs. Don't overtighten \u2014 the oil pan threads are aluminum.","Move to the oil filter housing on the top-passenger side of the engine. Remove the housing cap with the filter cap tool or 13/16\" socket (counter-clockwise). Lift out the old filter element.","Inspect the O-ring on the housing cap \u2014 replace it if it's cracked or flattened. Most OEM and quality aftermarket filters include a replacement O-ring.","Install the new filter element into the housing cap. Lightly lubricate the O-ring with fresh oil before reinstalling the cap. Torque the cap to 18 ft-lbs.","Remove the oil filler cap on top of the engine (yellow cap on the valve cover). Add 5.5 quarts of 0W-20 full synthetic. Wait 30 seconds for the oil to settle in the pan, then check the dipstick. Add in small amounts to reach the full mark \u2014 don't overfill.","Replace the filler cap. Start the engine and let it run for 30 seconds. Check under the vehicle for leaks at the drain plug. Check around the filter housing for weeping.","Turn off the engine, wait 2 minutes, and verify the oil level on the dipstick. Add if needed to reach the full mark.","Reset the oil life monitor: turn the ignition to Run (don't start the engine), press the gas pedal to the floor three times within 10 seconds. The OLM resets and the maintenance reminder clears."]},{"id":"pentastar-3-6-oil-cooler-leak","title":"Pentastar 3.6L oil cooler / filter housing leak (TSB fix)","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"engine","subcategory":"oil system","type":"how_to","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2.0,"high":4.0},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":180},"tools_required":["3/8\" drive ratchet","10mm socket","13mm socket","torque wrench","drain pan","funnel","coolant catch container"],"skills_required":["basic engine work","coolant system knowledge","torque spec adherence"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-present"],"tags":["oil cooler","filter housing","3.6L pentastar","coolant","oil leak","TSB","dorman"],"parts":[{"name":"Metal oil filter housing assembly (replaces plastic factory unit)","vendor":"Dorman","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Oil filter housing gasket/O-ring kit","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Engine coolant (Mopar OAT or equivalent)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"Engine oil (5W-20 full synthetic, 6 quarts)","vendor":"Mopar / Pennzoil / Mobil 1","price_estimate_usd":42}],"citations":[{"title":"NHTSA TSB database \u2014 Pentastar 3.6L oil cooler housing failures","url":"https://www.nhtsa.gov/vehicle/2020/JEEP/GLADIATOR/PU/AWD"},{"title":"Dorman 926-959 product page","url":"https://www.dormanproducts.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-oil-change-pentastar","jt-coolant-flush"],"summary":"The Pentastar 3.6L's plastic oil filter housing develops cracks around 60,000\u2013100,000 miles. When it fails, you get oil in the coolant, coolant in the oil, or both. The fix is replacing the factory plastic housing with a metal aftermarket unit. This isn't a difficult job \u2014 it's a medium-complexity engine bay task.","description":"The Pentastar 3.6L \u2014 shared across the JK, JL, and JT \u2014 uses a plastic oil filter housing that also integrates the oil cooler. The housing is a single assembly that sits on the front of the engine block. Coolant runs through it to cool the oil; oil runs through it to the filter.\n\nThe plastic material degrades over time from heat cycling. Cracks typically develop around the gasket surfaces, at the coolant ports, and at the threads for the oil filter cap. When a crack appears, you can get oil mixing into the coolant (look for a brownish, milky sheen on the coolant reservoir cap), coolant mixing into the oil (look for a coffee-with-milk color on the dipstick), or external leaks pooling under the truck.\n\n**Catch this early.** Oil-coolant mixing is a fast path to bearing damage and a ruined engine if left unaddressed. If your coolant smells like oil or your dipstick shows discoloration, stop driving and diagnose.\n\n- External oil leak near the front of the engine, below the intake\n- Coolant reservoir showing a brownish tint or emulsified appearance\n- Dipstick oil with a milky or frothy appearance (coolant contamination)\n- Coolant loss without visible external leaks\n- White smoke from exhaust (coolant burning in cylinders \u2014 late-stage)\n\nBefore pulling the housing, confirm the housing is the source. A flashlight and a rag: wipe the area around the housing clean, run the engine to temp, then let it cool slightly and look for fresh seepage. The housing-to-block gasket and the coolant hose connections are the most common leak points.\n\nIf you have oil-coolant mixing confirmed, the housing replacement is mandatory. You'll also need to flush the cooling system and change the oil after the repair \u2014 the contaminated fluids need to come out completely.\n\nThe factory plastic housing can be replaced with an identical plastic unit (the leak may return) or with a metal aftermarket housing. The metal housing is the better repair. Dorman makes a direct-fit metal replacement that covers JK, JL, and JT applications; verify your year and engine application before ordering.\n\nThe Dorman unit (product number in the 926-series for this application) retains the factory oil filter thread spec and coolant port locations. It bolts in where the factory unit came out.\n\nIf the housing failed internally and mixed oil with coolant (not merely an external weep), replacing the housing is only half the job. Contaminated coolant leaves an oily film through the entire cooling system, and oil that emulsified with coolant has to come out of the crankcase completely.\n\n1. **Flush the cooling system twice.** After the new housing is on, fill with distilled water and a cooling-system flush chemical, run to temp, drain, and repeat until the drain runs clear with no oil sheen. Only then fill with the correct Mopar OAT coolant. Full procedure and coolant spec: [/db/gladiator/jt-coolant-flush/](/db/gladiator/jt-coolant-flush/).\n2. **Change the oil, then change it again short-interval.** Do a full oil-and-filter change with the housing repair, drive 500\u20131,000 miles, then change it a second time. Coolant contamination degrades oil additives and a single change rarely clears residual moisture from the crankcase. Correct capacity, filter part number, and torque specs are in [/db/gladiator/jt-oil-change-pentastar/](/db/gladiator/jt-oil-change-pentastar/).\n3. **Inspect the oil after the second change.** Milky residue on the dipstick or filler cap after a clean flush and two changes means coolant is still entering the oil \u2014 recheck the housing seal and, if it persists, have the block and head gaskets evaluated. Emulsified oil that reached the bearings is the failure mode that turns an $150 repair into an engine.\n\nThe plastic-to-metal swap is the prevention \u2014 a metal Dorman housing removes the material failure mode entirely, so once it's on, this specific leak does not come back. Beyond that:\n\n- **Watch the coolant reservoir at every oil change.** A faint brown tint or an oily ring at the cap fill line is the earliest warning, usually months before external dripping. Catching it here means a planned repair instead of a roadside one.\n- **Don't over-torque the housing bolts.** The most common way people re-introduce a leak on the metal unit is crushing the gasket by exceeding the ~18 ft-lb spec. Use a torque wrench, not feel.\n- **Keep coolant fresh.** Degraded, acidic coolant accelerates gasket and seal wear at every joint in the system, including a freshly installed housing. Stay on the coolant interval rather than running the factory fill indefinitely.","steps":["Allow the engine to cool completely \u2014 at least 2 hours after last operation. Do not work on a hot cooling system.","Drain the coolant from the lower radiator drain petcock into a catch pan. Mark the container \u2014 used coolant is toxic and needs proper disposal.","Drain the engine oil \u2014 place a drain pan under the oil drain plug, remove the plug, remove the oil filter cap from the housing to help the oil drain faster.","Disconnect the coolant hoses from the oil cooler housing (two hoses, clamps). Have rags ready \u2014 residual coolant will drain.","Remove the oil filter housing mounting bolts (typically 10mm). Note the number and location.","Lift the housing assembly free of the block. Inspect the block sealing surface \u2014 clean it with a rag if there's residue.","Install the new metal housing with a fresh gasket/O-ring kit. Apply no sealant unless the kit instructions specify it. Torque mounting bolts to spec (check the Dorman instructions \u2014 typically 18 ft-lb for the housing bolts).","Reconnect coolant hoses. Reinstall drain plug and new oil filter.","Fill engine oil (6 quarts 5W-20 full synthetic for the 3.6L).","Refill cooling system with fresh coolant. Burp the system: leave the reservoir cap loose, run the engine to temp, watch for air bubbles, then seal.","Check for leaks after first full heat cycle. Recheck coolant level when cold."],"pros":["Metal housing eliminates the material failure mode that caused the original crack","Direct-fit replacement \u2014 no modifications required","Resolves oil-coolant mixing before it causes downstream engine damage"],"cons":["Must flush and refill both oil and coolant \u2014 adds to total cost","If coolant contamination reached the oil passages significantly, inspect further for bearing damage","Aftermarket housing quality varies \u2014 buy from a reputable supplier and verify fitment before ordering"]},{"id":"jt-fuel-system-maintenance","title":"Fuel System Maintenance \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT 3.6L","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"fuel","type":"reference","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":15,"high":85},"tools_required":["socket set","fuel line disconnect tools (if filter replacement needed)","drain pan","multimeter for fuel pump voltage test"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","fuel system safety awareness"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["fuel","maintenance","fuel-filter","fuel-pump","throttle-body"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar Fuel Filter (in-tank, 2020-2024 JT)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"CRC Throttle Body Cleaner","vendor":"CRC","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"Sea Foam Motor Treatment (fuel system cleaner)","vendor":"Sea Foam","price_estimate_usd":14}],"citations":[{"title":"Gladiator JT Owner's Manual \u2014 Fuel System","url":"https://www.stellantis.com"},{"title":"Mopar JT Fuel Filter OEM Part","url":"https://www.mopar.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-spark-plug-replacement","jt-fuel-filter-service"],"summary":"The 3.6L Pentastar in the Gladiator JT does not have a serviceable inline fuel filter \u2014 the filter is integrated into the in-tank fuel pump module and replaced only when the pump fails or at high mileage.","description":"The JT's fuel system is more straightforward to maintain than it looks. Stellantis doesn't specify a standalone fuel filter service interval because the filter is part of the pump module \u2014 it only comes out with the pump. In practice, this means most JT owners don't touch the fuel filter until 100,000+ miles or when a fuel delivery symptom appears (hard starts, rough idle, hesitation at throttle).\n\nWhat you can do proactively: throttle body cleaning and a fuel system additive. Both are worth doing at 30,000\u201350,000-mile intervals and take under an hour combined.\n\nThe Pentastar's throttle body accumulates carbon deposits from crankcase vapor recirculation (PCV). Enough buildup causes rough idle and sluggish tip-in.\n\n1. Park with the engine cold. Locate the throttle body on the driver's side of the intake \u2014 it's the round aluminum housing where the large air intake hose connects.\n2. Disconnect the intake air hose by loosening the clamp and pulling the hose free. Stuff a clean rag in the hose end to prevent debris ingestion.\n3. With the throttle blade closed, spray a small amount of throttle body cleaner onto a clean shop rag and wipe the inner bore and blade edges. Do not spray cleaner directly into the throttle body on a drive-by-wire engine \u2014 the pressure can force debris past the blade into the intake.\n4. If deposits are heavy: have an assistant hold the accelerator pedal to open the blade (ignition on, engine off), and carefully wipe the backside of the blade and bore with a solvent-soaked rag.\n5. Reconnect the intake hose and clamp. Start the engine \u2014 expect a rough idle for 30\u201360 seconds as residual cleaner burns off.\n\nAdding Sea Foam or a similar concentrated fuel system cleaner to a full tank of fuel once a year cleans injector deposits and keeps the fuel pump pickup screen clear of varnish buildup. Follow the label instructions \u2014 for Sea Foam, typically 1 oz per gallon of fuel capacity (the JT holds 22.5 gallons, so a full can of Sea Foam is appropriate).\n\nYou won't know the in-tank filter is clogged until the pump shows symptoms:\n- Longer than usual crank time before start\n- Hesitation or stumble under heavy load (climbing a grade, trailer towing)\n- Engine cutting out briefly under hard acceleration\n- Low fuel pressure code (P0087, P0090)\n\nA rough check: at key-on (not cranked), listen for the fuel pump prime \u2014 a faint whirr from under the truck for 2\u20133 seconds. If you don't hear it, the pump is suspect. Verify with a fuel pressure gauge at the Schrader valve on the fuel rail \u2014 3.6L Pentastar spec is 55\u201360 PSI at idle.\n\n- **No smoking, no open flames**: Fuel system work near an open fuel tank is fire hazard territory. No exceptions.\n- **Carbon deposits vs. mechanical issues**: If cleaning the throttle body doesn't resolve a rough idle, don't keep cleaning \u2014 investigate further. Misfires, vacuum leaks, and failing coil packs produce similar symptoms.\n- **Ethanol content**: E85 or high-ethanol blends are not compatible with the JT's 3.6L unless it's a Flex Fuel variant (check the fuel door label \u2014 FFV models have a yellow gas cap). Running E85 in a non-FFV model can damage injectors and fuel system seals.\n\nThrottle body cleaning: ~$8\u201312 for a can of CRC or similar. Your time: 30 minutes. Worth doing every 30,000 miles.\n\nSea Foam or fuel system additive: ~$10\u201315 per treatment. Once a year at fill-up.\n\nIn-tank fuel pump/filter module: ~$200\u2013350 for OEM Mopar. Labor at a shop to drop the tank and replace the module is 2\u20133 hours, adding $150\u2013300. A symptom-free pump doesn't need replacement.\n\nWhen the pump finally does go (usually a no-start with no prime whirr, or a P0087/P0230-class code), the filter comes out with it because they're one assembly. This is a tank-drop job, not a roadside fix:\n\n1. Run the tank as low as safely possible before you start \u2014 you'll be lifting it, and 22.5 gallons of fuel is roughly 140 lbs of dead weight you don't want overhead.\n2. Relieve fuel pressure: pull the fuel pump relay and crank the engine for a few seconds until it stalls, then disconnect the battery negative.\n3. Support the tank with a transmission jack or a padded floor jack and a wide board. Disconnect the filler neck hose, vent lines, the fuel feed line (use the correct quick-disconnect tool \u2014 the plastic clips break if you pry), and the electrical connector on top of the module.\n4. Remove the two tank straps and lower the tank enough to reach the lock ring on top. Turn the lock ring counterclockwise (a brass punch and a hammer work; don't use a steel screwdriver near a full fuel tank).\n5. Lift the module out at an angle to clear the float arm without bending it. Install the new module with a fresh lock-ring seal, indexed to the same orientation.\n6. Reconnect everything, add the fuel you drained, and key on three times before cranking to prime and pressurize the rail. Check for leaks at the feed line and lock ring before the first start.\n\nIf any step past pressure relief feels beyond your setup \u2014 no way to safely support the tank, no quick-disconnect tools \u2014 this is a reasonable job to hand to a shop. Fuel over your head is not the place to improvise.\n\nThe throttle-body and additive work above pairs naturally with the rest of the 3.6L's tune-up items. If you're chasing a rough idle or hesitation, rule out ignition before condemning the fuel system \u2014 see [Spark plug replacement on the JT](/db/gladiator/jt-spark-plug-replacement/), and fold this into your broader schedule in [Gladiator JT service intervals](/db/gladiator/jt-service-intervals/)."},{"id":"jt-fuel-filter-service","title":"Fuel Filter Service \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"fuel","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":80},"tools_required":["fuel line disconnect tools","nitrile gloves","catch container","socket set","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","fuel system safety awareness"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["fuel","maintenance","filter","fuel-system"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OEM Fuel Filter for JT (Part #68439719AA)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":38},{"name":"FRAM G12 In-Line Fuel Filter (compatible)","vendor":"FRAM","price_estimate_usd":18}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator JT Service Manual \u2014 Fuel System","url":"https://www.stellantis.com"},{"title":"Mopar OEM Parts \u2014 Gladiator JT Fuel Filter","url":"https://www.mopar.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-fuel-system-maintenance","jt-oil-change-pentastar"],"summary":"The Gladiator JT's 3.6L Pentastar runs a fuel filter that's part of the in-tank fuel pump module \u2014 not a traditional in-line filter you'll find under the hood. Service intervals and access are different from older Jeeps.","description":"Most JT owners come from older Wrangler or Cherokee ownership where a replaceable in-line filter was a routine item. The JT changed this. The 3.6L Pentastar uses a strainer integrated into the fuel pump module that sits inside the tank. Stellantis rates it as \"lifetime\" \u2014 meaning they intend it to go the life of the pump module, not that it never needs attention. In practice, this means you're not doing an annual filter swap; you're watching for symptoms that indicate a clogged strainer or degraded pump.\n\nThe only physical filter most JT owners will service is the fuel pump strainer/sock at module replacement time, or when symptoms indicate restriction: hard starts when hot, sputtering at wide-open throttle, a lean fuel trim code (P0171, P0174), or reduced power under load.\n\n**Fuel safety \u2014 mandatory:** Depressurize the fuel system before any fuel line work. Locate the fuel pump fuse in the power distribution center under the hood, remove it, then crank the engine until it stalls (this purges line pressure). Work in a ventilated space with no open flames or ignition sources within 20 feet.\n\nBefore replacing anything, confirm fuel system pressure is actually low. A fuel pressure gauge (Schrader valve test port access via the fuel rail \u2014 check service manual location for the JT's 3.6L) should read 49\u201351 PSI at idle and hold pressure for 10+ minutes after shutdown. A reading below 45 PSI or pressure drop under load points to the pump/strainer, not a sensor issue.\n\n1. Relieve system pressure via the fuse method above.\n2. Remove the rear seat cushion and fold back the cargo area trim panel to access the fuel pump module access cover (center of the floor, beneath a plastic trim piece held by screws).\n3. Disconnect the negative battery terminal before disconnecting any fuel or electrical connections at the module.\n4. Use a fuel pump lock ring removal tool (or improvised spanner) to turn the lock ring counterclockwise. The JT uses a plastic lock ring \u2014 do not use an impact wrench. Hand effort only.\n5. Lift the module straight up slowly \u2014 the float arm is long and will catch if you tip the module sideways. Drain residual fuel into a catch container.\n6. Inspect the strainer/sock on the bottom of the module assembly. A grey/brown sock with visible debris is clogged. A clean replacement strainer is usually included with any aftermarket pump kit.\n7. Install in reverse. New O-ring required at the module flange \u2014 do not reuse the old one.\n8. Reinstall the lock ring and torque to 40 ft-lbs with a lock ring tool. Reinstall the pump fuse, turn the key to ON (do not crank) three times to prime the system, then start.\n\n\"Lifetime\" strainer is an engineering intent, not a promise. For a JT that only sees clean gas-station fuel and highway miles, the factory position holds \u2014 you may never touch the strainer before the pump itself wears out somewhere past 120,000 miles. The trucks that need attention earlier are the ones living the life the Gladiator is built for.\n\nPull the interval in if any of these apply: you've run fuel from questionable sources (backcountry cans, small-town pumps with old underground tanks), you've stored the truck with less than a quarter tank for long stretches (condensation and tank rust), or you've run ethanol blends above the approved E10/E15 range. Dirt and water reach the strainer through the fill, and a truck that fords water crossings with a marginal fuel-cap seal is a candidate too. The honest read: don't replace the strainer on a schedule, but inspect it any time you're in the tank for a pump, sending-unit, or level-sensor job, and treat the symptoms below as your real service trigger. Fold a fuel-system look into your broader [maintenance-interval planning](/db/gladiator/jt-service-intervals/) so it doesn't get forgotten.\n\nThe symptoms of a clogged strainer, a weak pump, and a bad fuel-pressure sensor overlap \u2014 and the parts range from a $30 sock to a $350 module, so guessing is expensive. Work it in order:\n\n1. **Pull the codes first.** A lean bank code (P0171/P0174) or a fuel-pressure-performance code narrows the field. If you don't have a scanner handy, the [OBD2 diagnostics guide](/db/gladiator/jt-obd2-diagnostics/) covers reading and interpreting them on the JT.\n2. **Measure rail pressure under load, not only at idle.** A tired pump or a partially blocked strainer often holds 49\u201351 PSI at idle and then sags under wide-open-throttle demand. If pressure drops below ~45 PSI on a hard pull or up a grade, the supply side is the problem \u2014 pump or strainer.\n3. **Separate pump from strainer by inspection.** Once the module is out (procedure above), a grey/brown loaded sock with the pump still spinning strong points to a strainer clog you can fix cheaply. A pump that runs weak or intermittent with a clean sock means the module is worn \u2014 replace the assembly, not the sock.\n4. **Rule out the sensor last.** If rail pressure measured mechanically is in spec but the ECM reports low pressure, the fuel-rail pressure sensor or its wiring is lying \u2014 don't condemn a good pump over a $40 sensor.\n\nSkipping straight to a $350 module because the truck stumbles is the common, costly mistake. Confirm low pressure mechanically before you buy anything.\n\n- **Lock ring cracks**: The plastic lock ring on the JT is fragile when corroded. If it's cracking or won't seat cleanly, replace it \u2014 a lock ring failure drops the module into the tank and requires dropping the tank to recover.\n- **Fuel level**: Drop the tank below 1/4 before this job. Pulling the module from a near-full tank creates a significant spill risk.\n- **Ethanol content**: If the JT has run E85 or high-ethanol blends not approved for the fuel system, inspect the strainer more carefully \u2014 high-ethanol fuel degrades rubber components and may require fuel system inspection beyond the filter.\n\nMopar OEM fuel pump strainer (replacement sock only): ~$25\u201335. If the pump is otherwise functional, this is the minimum intervention.\n\nFull fuel pump module replacement (OEM): ~$200\u2013350. The pump and strainer are sold as an assembly \u2014 if the pump is marginal, replace the whole unit rather than the sock.\n\nFull module replacement (aftermarket, Spectra Premium or Delphi): ~$120\u2013200. Both are acceptable OEM-equivalent suppliers for this application.\n\nShop labor for fuel pump module replacement: $250\u2013400. Access through the floor vs. dropping the tank is the JT's time-saver \u2014 it brings this job within reasonable DIY range. A shop will charge for the time savings they don't actually get."},{"id":"jt-cargo-bed-management","title":"Cargo Management and Bed Organization \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"interior","type":"upgrade","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":650},"tools_required":["socket set","drill (for some systems)","measuring tape"],"skills_required":["basic install"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["interior","cargo","bed","overland","organization","truck-bed"],"parts":[{"name":"Decked JT Truck Bed Drawer System","vendor":"Decked","price_estimate_usd":1299},{"name":"Gladiator JT Bed Cargo Net (OEM-style)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":89},{"name":"TruXedo TruXport Soft Roll-Up Tonneau Cover \u2014 JT","vendor":"TruXedo","price_estimate_usd":295},{"name":"Rough Country Bed Cargo Rack (cross bars, JT)","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":189},{"name":"Tyger Auto Tri-Fold Hard Tonneau Cover \u2014 JT","vendor":"Tyger Auto","price_estimate_usd":349}],"citations":[{"title":"Decked JT Drawer System Product Page","url":"https://www.decked.com"},{"title":"TruXedo JT Tonneau Compatibility","url":"https://www.truxedo.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-floor-liners","jt-bed-rack-options"],"summary":"The Gladiator JT's 5-foot bed is shorter than most truck buyers expect \u2014 but it's wider than a Wrangler's cargo area and comes with factory tie-down cleats that make organizing a trail rig or overland setup much more manageable than it sounds.","description":"The JT's bed is 60 inches long (5 feet exactly) and 44 inches wide at the narrowest inside point. That's shorter than a Tacoma's standard 5-foot bed by about 0.5 inches, and shorter than most full-size trucks by a significant margin \u2014 but it's a truck bed that a Wrangler doesn't have. For overlanders used to packing everything into an XJ or Wrangler cargo area, the JT bed feels like an upgrade even at 5 feet.\n\nFactory tie-downs are one of the JT's practical wins. The four factory cleats in the bed corners are rated and useful. Use them before buying additional hardware.\n\n**Most-asked cargo questions:**\n\n**Tonneau cover compatibility**: Most 5-foot tonneau covers fit the JT. Soft roll-up covers (TruXedo, Extang, Gator) are the most trail-friendly because they fold away when you need full bed access for camp gear. Hard tri-fold covers add security but limit what you can haul sticking above the side rails.\n\n**Does a full-size mattress fit?** No. A 5-foot bed is too short for any standard mattress without hanging over the tailgate. A 54\u00d780 full mattress hangs out 20 inches. Overland-specific sleeping platforms with foam cut to 54\u00d758 inches work in the bed with the tailgate down and a tailgate pad extension.\n\n**Bed drawers (Decked):** The most organized solution for overlanders. Decked's JT system installs without drilling, uses the factory stake pockets, and leaves the top surface of the drawers as a full flat floor \u2014 sleeping platform if you add a sleeping pad. Drawers lock and are waterproof. Heavy and expensive ($1,299) but it's the right answer for a dedicated overland rig.\n\n**Cargo nets and straps:** The cheapest and most flexible option. Factory cleats accept standard tie-down straps. A cargo net across the width of the bed (Mopar or aftermarket) keeps loose gear from shifting. Zero permanent modification.\n\n**Bed cargo rack (crossbars):** Rough Country and Thule make crossbar systems for the JT that use the factory stake pockets. A crossbar system adds high-mount points for overlanding bags, recovery gear, or a rooftop tent if you're using the truck without a cab-height rack. Pair with a cargo bag rather than loose items.\n\n**Tonneau cover + tray combo:** A soft tonneau over a shallow plastic bed tray keeps gear organized without the cost of a drawer system. Removable when you need full bed height for a large haul.\n\n- **Soft tonneau covers don't lock**: If security matters (urban camping, leaving gear overnight), use a locking hard cover or a Decked system with locking drawers. A rolled-up soft cover offers no theft resistance.\n- **Tailgate latch weight limits**: The JT tailgate is rated to support the tailgate itself plus around 200 lbs of static load. Standing on the tailgate, loading heavy gear by stepping on it, or using it as a workbench over time can bend the hinges. Keep load centered.\n- **Bed liner protects resale**: JT resale values hold well. A spray-in liner (Line-X, Rhino Liner) or drop-in plastic tray prevents scratches that reduce value. Spray-in is permanent; drop-in is removable.\n- **Rubicon vs. Sport/Overland bed differences**: The Rubicon trim uses the same bed dimensions as all JT trims. Accessories that fit one trim fit all \u2014 there's no bed width or length difference between trim levels.\n\nThe cargo mistake most JT owners make is buying the most-photographed solution instead of the one that fits their use. Sort it by what the truck does most weekends:\n\n**Daily driver that occasionally hauls:** A soft roll-up tonneau plus the factory cleats and a cargo net covers 90% of use. You keep full bed height when you need it and weather protection when you don't. Total spend under $350. Skip the drawer system \u2014 it's dead weight and lost bed volume for a truck that rarely carries organized gear.\n\n**Weekend overlander:** A drawer system (Decked) or a mid-height platform earns its cost here. You gain a lockable, waterproof floor and a flat sleeping surface, and you stop re-packing loose gear at every camp. The tradeoff is ~200 lbs of permanent weight and roughly 8 inches of bed depth consumed \u2014 plan your other storage around that. Pair it with a bed rack (see [bed rack options for the JT](/db/gladiator/jt-bed-rack-options/)) if you're mounting a tent or traction boards up high.\n\n**Rock crawler / technical trail rig:** Keep it minimal. Every pound in the bed rides high relative to a low center of gravity, and a loaded high rack hurts you on off-camber lines. A locking under-tonneau tray plus cinched-down recovery gear beats a tall rack for this use. Keep recovery gear reachable without unloading \u2014 see [recovery gear essentials for the JT](/db/gladiator/jt-recovery-gear-essentials/).\n\nBed organization is also a weight-management decision, and the JT's payload is tighter than the truck's size suggests. Depending on trim and options, JT payload runs roughly 1,100\u20131,700 lbs \u2014 and that number has to cover passengers, fuel, a full tank of accessories, and everything in the bed. A Rubicon with a winch bumper, steel skids, and 37s can be down to 900\u20131,000 lbs of real payload before you load a single cooler.\n\nA loaded drawer system, a rooftop tent on a bed rack, and a week of camp gear add up fast. Two practical rules: weigh your built truck at a CAT scale once so you know your actual remaining payload, and keep heavy items (water, tools, recovery gear) low and forward in the bed rather than high on a rack. Weight high and rearward hurts both handling and departure angle. For the full payload/towing picture \u2014 including how a bed load interacts with tongue weight \u2014 see [payload and towing reality on the JT](/db/gladiator/payload-towing-reality/).\n\nCargo net (Mopar or aftermarket): $40\u201390. Most straightforward option, gets the job done.\n\nSoft roll-up tonneau (TruXedo TruXport): ~$280\u2013320. The most popular cover for trail-going JTs \u2014 folds up in seconds when you need the full bed.\n\nHard tri-fold (Tyger Auto or similar): ~$300\u2013400. More security, better for daily use, slower to remove for large hauls.\n\nCargo crossbars (Rough Country stake-pocket): ~$150\u2013200. Bolt-on, no permanent modification.\n\nDecked drawer system: ~$1,299. The premium organized solution \u2014 do it once and stop fighting with loose gear."},{"id":"jt-floor-liners","title":"Floor Liner Selection for the Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"interior","type":"upgrade","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":0.5},"cost_usd":{"low":89,"high":249},"tools_required":["none \u2014 tool-free installation"],"skills_required":[],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["interior","floor-liners","protection","weather"],"parts":[{"name":"WeatherTech DigitalFit Floor Liners \u2014 Gladiator JT Front","vendor":"WeatherTech","price_estimate_usd":109},{"name":"Husky WeatherBeater Floor Liners \u2014 JT Front & Rear Set","vendor":"Husky Liners","price_estimate_usd":99},{"name":"MAXLINER A1/B1 Floor Mats \u2014 JT Complete Set","vendor":"MAXLINER","price_estimate_usd":89},{"name":"MOPAR All-Weather Floor Mat Kit \u2014 JT","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":149}],"citations":[{"title":"WeatherTech Jeep Gladiator JT Floor Liners Product Page","url":"https://www.weathertech.com"},{"title":"Husky Liners JT Gladiator Fit Guide","url":"https://www.huskyliners.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-cargo-bed-management","jt-bed-liner-options"],"summary":"The Gladiator JT's interior takes abuse that most truck cabs never see \u2014 trail mud, standing water from doorsoff driving, and gear shuffled in and out for camp. Factory mats don't survive this. Custom-fit liners do.","description":"The JT's cab footwell geometry changed slightly from the JL Wrangler, so don't assume Wrangler-fit liners will transfer over \u2014 they won't, and the misfit gaps defeat the purpose. Custom-molded liners for the JT have been available since 2020 from WeatherTech, Husky Liners, Maxliner, and Mopar. Each has trade-offs worth understanding before ordering.\n\n**WeatherTech DigitalFit** ($109 front, ~$169 full set) is the benchmark for fit precision. Laser-scanned molds produce liners that cover every corner of the JT footwell with no gaps. The lip height is taller than stock mats, which is the whole point \u2014 mud and water pool in the liner rather than soaking into the carpet. Downside: the material is slightly stiffer, which some owners find less comfortable under the heel in an all-day drive. For anything that involves water or mud \u2014 even occasional \u2014 it's the correct choice.\n\n**Husky WeatherBeater** (~$99 for a front-and-rear set) gives most of the WeatherTech protection at a lower price. Fit is slightly less precise \u2014 there are small gaps at the corners that WeatherTech doesn't have \u2014 but for owners who don't submerge the floors regularly, the Husky set is honest value. Husky's X-Act Contour series (a step up at ~$130 for fronts) closes the gap considerably.\n\n**Maxliner A1/B1 set** (~$89 complete) is the budget option. Fit is passable, material is adequate. Not the choice for serious trail or off-road use, but perfectly acceptable for the owner who wants something better than paper mats on a street-driven JT.\n\n**Mopar OEM liners** ($149): FCA's official option. Identical fit to WeatherTech (same scanning process for the OEM supplier), with a Mopar logo. The premium over WeatherTech is purely branding \u2014 no functional difference. Skip unless you want the logo.\n\nThe JT gets water on the floor \u2014 not trail water, but rain \u2014 every time you remove the doors and the sky opens up. Liners with high sidewall lips handle this. WeatherTech and Husky both have lip heights sufficient to contain rain events. After a downpour, pull the plugs in the JT's floor drain holes (factory-installed in the cab) and tilt the vehicle slightly to drain, or use a shop vac to pull standing water from inside the liner before it seeps under.\n\n1. Remove the factory floor mats (if present).\n2. WeatherTech and Husky liners drop into place with no hardware \u2014 they're held by friction and the factory mat retention posts. Line up the posts with the holes in the liner and press down.\n3. Check that the driver's liner does not interfere with pedal movement or return. This is the only safety check required \u2014 a liner that catches under the brake pedal is dangerous.\n4. Rear liners in 4-door Crew Cab JT models cover the second-row footwell. The Sport and Willys single-cab configuration uses a different rear section \u2014 confirm the correct part number before ordering.\n\nFloor liners protect the cab. They do nothing for the bed, and JT owners routinely conflate the two. The cab footwell needs a molded liner with tall sidewalls to contain water and mud from doors-off driving. The bed needs its own protection strategy \u2014 a spray-in or drop-in bedliner \u2014 because the 5-foot steel bed rusts from the same trail mud and standing water, plus the abrasion of hauling gear, firewood, and recovery equipment. If you're outfitting a JT for trail and camp duty, budget for both; see [bed liner options](/db/gladiator/jt-bed-liner-options/) for the spray-in vs. drop-in decision, and [cargo bed management](/db/gladiator/jt-cargo-bed-management/) for keeping loose gear from sliding into the tailgate on the trail.\n\nThe reason to buy a liner is so you can hose it out \u2014 but that only works if you pull it to do it. Trapped water and grit under a liner will hold moisture against the factory carpet and, over seasons, promote mildew and corrosion at the seat and seatbelt anchor points. After a muddy weekend, remove the liners, rinse both the liner and the carpet beneath, and let both dry fully before reinstalling. Thermoplastic liners (WeatherTech, Husky) tolerate a pressure washer on a wide fan; rubber-blend mats can be scrubbed with mild soap. Avoid petroleum-based dressings on the driver's liner \u2014 they leave a slick film exactly where your foot needs grip on the pedals.\n\nThe single safety item on this install is pedal clearance. A liner that shifts forward and catches under the brake or accelerator is a genuine hazard, not a nuisance. Confirm the driver's liner seats on the factory retention posts and doesn't creep. Re-check it after the first few drives and any time you've pulled it for cleaning. If your JT didn't come with floor retention posts (some base configurations), add aftermarket anchor hooks rather than trusting friction alone.\n\nBudget-tier Maxliner set: ~$89 complete (front + rear). Acceptable for street use.\n\nMid-tier Husky WeatherBeater set: ~$99\u2013130. Best value for trail owners who want reliable containment without WeatherTech pricing.\n\nPremium WeatherTech DigitalFit set (front + rear): ~$160\u2013180. The ceiling for this category. Overkill for a garage-kept JT; exactly right for a rig that sees mud seasonally.\n\nFloor liners are install-it-yourself items with no tools required. The only money saved by going to a shop is time, and there's no time to save \u2014 this is a 10-minute job."},{"id":"jt-differential-fluid-change","title":"Differential Fluid Change \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT Front and Rear Dana 44","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"maintenance","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":2.5},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":95},"tools_required":["socket set (3/8 drive)","drain pan (2 gallon)","3/8 ratchet and extension","fluid pump or squeeze bottle","rags","gasket scraper if reusing cover"],"skills_required":["basic fluid work","safe vehicle support"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["maintenance","differential","dana-44","gear-oil","axle","fluid"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar Gear and Axle Lubricant 75W-140 (Rubicon limited-slip additive included)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Royal Purple Max-Gear 75W-140 Synthetic Gear Oil","vendor":"Royal Purple","price_estimate_usd":16},{"name":"Mopar Friction Modifier for Limited-Slip Differential","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Permatex Ultra Grey RTV Gasket Maker (M210/M220 covers seal with RTV, not a cut gasket)","vendor":"Permatex","price_estimate_usd":10}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator JT Owner's Manual \u2014 Fluid Specifications","url":"https://www.stellantis.com"},{"title":"Dana 44 Service Manual \u2014 Fluid Capacity and Specification","url":"https://www.dana.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-transfer-case-fluid","jt-service-intervals","jt-dana44-rear-locker","jt-regear"],"summary":"The JT Gladiator's front and rear Dana 44 axles hold approximately 1.6 quarts of fluid each. Rubicon trims with the factory locker require a friction modifier additive \u2014 without it, the Tru-Lok locker chatters and shudders during low-speed turns.","description":"Jeep specifies 75W-140 full synthetic gear oil for the JT's Dana 44 axles. Change interval is 30,000 miles under normal use \u2014 or after every water crossing where the axles were submerged. Water contamination in differential fluid turns it milky gray and looks nothing like fresh gear oil. If you've wheeled through a significant water crossing, check the axle fluid within a week. A half-pint of water in a differential will destroy bearings over 5,000\u201310,000 miles.\n\n**Rubicon locker note:** The Tru-Lok locker in the Rubicon front and rear axles requires Mopar friction modifier additive in the gear oil \u2014 or equivalent (Loctite 504821). Without it, the clutch packs chatter during parking-lot turns. This is normal behavior initially after an oil change without additive, but persistent chatter means the clutch pack surfaces are being worn prematurely. Use the additive.\n\nNon-Rubicon JT trims (Sport, Overland, Mojave): standard 75W-140 synthetic, no additive needed.\n\n- **Dana 44 fill capacity**: Front approximately 1.6 quarts, rear approximately 1.75 quarts. Fill to overflow \u2014 don't estimate.\n- **Synthetic vs. conventional**: Use full synthetic 75W-140 only. The Dana 44 in the JT runs at higher loads and temperatures than older axle designs, and conventional gear oil won't keep up over a 30,000-mile interval.\n- **Rubicon front locker vent**: The Rubicon's front axle has an air locker connection \u2014 a small airline fitting on the axle housing. Don't confuse it for a fill plug. It's usually smaller and has a fitting, not a square drive.\n- **Aftermarket locker service (ELocker, air locker)**: If you've added an aftermarket locker, check the manufacturer's fluid specification. Some aftermarket lockers specify a different viscosity or prohibit friction modifiers.\n\nThe axles under a JT are badged Dana 44, but they are the AdvanTEK generation: M210 front, M220 rear. The M220 runs a 220mm ring gear versus the legacy Dana 44's 216mm, with its own carrier, bearings, cover, and bolt pattern. Practical consequence for a fluid change: parts listings that say \"Dana 44\" without specifying JL/JT or M210/M220 are usually for the legacy axle and will not fit. This is the same trap covered in the rear locker guide \u2014 anything internal to these axles must be catalogued for the JL/JT application specifically. Fluid spec is unaffected (75W-140 synthetic either way), but covers, gaskets, and hardware are not interchangeable with a TJ/JK-era 44.\n\nThe drained fluid is a free axle inspection \u2014 look before you dump the pan:\n\n- **Dark amber to brown, slight metallic sheen on the magnet:** normal wear. The cover magnet collecting fine gray paste is expected; that is what it is there for.\n- **Milky, gray, or coffee-with-cream:** water. Change the fluid, drive a week, change it again. If the second drain is milky too, hunt for the entry point \u2014 most often the axle vent hose has cracked or popped off its fitting on the housing. Extend the vents if you cross water regularly.\n- **Glitter, flakes, or chunks on the magnet:** bearing or gear damage. Do not refill and hope. Pull the cover, photograph the ring gear wear pattern, and diagnose before the carrier bearing finishes letting go on a trail.\n- **Burnt smell with black fluid:** overheated \u2014 common after heavy towing or long sand running. Shorten the interval to 15,000 miles if this is your use pattern.\n\n75W-140 synthetic gear oil: ~$16\u201320 per quart. You'll use 3.5 quarts for both axles (front + rear). Two quarts of Mopar or Royal Purple covers it if each axle is slightly under 1.75 quarts.\n\nFriction modifier (Rubicon only): ~$12\u201315 per bottle. One bottle per differential (front and rear each get one bottle).\n\nSealant: one tube of Permatex Ultra Grey or Mopar RTV (~$10) covers both axles with plenty left over. Legacy Dana 44 cut gaskets sold by parts counters do not match the M210/M220 cover bolt pattern \u2014 skip them and use RTV like the factory did.\n\nTotal per axle: ~$35\u201345. Both axles: ~$65\u201390. A shop charges $80\u2013120 per axle for differential fluid service on a JT. Do both at home in an afternoon.","steps":["Warm the differential slightly \u2014 10\u201315 minutes of driving \u2014 to thin the old gear oil for more manageable draining. Hot gear oil is dangerous; warm is the target.","Jack the vehicle and support it on jack stands to level the axle. Differential drain and fill is more manageable on level ground.","Position a 2-gallon drain pan under the front differential. Remove the front differential cover bolts. Start from the bottom and work up \u2014 when you break the cover loose, old fluid will run out.","Allow the old fluid to drain completely. Inspect the drained fluid: dark and slightly metallic is normal. Milky or gray means water ingestion. Metallic chunks mean bearing damage \u2014 stop and investigate before refilling.","Clean the differential cover mating surface with a gasket scraper and brake cleaner. The factory seal is RTV, not a cut gasket \u2014 scrape every trace of the old bead off both the cover and the housing face. Any leftover ridge becomes a leak path.","Inspect the ring and pinion gears and carrier bearings through the open cover. Look for scoring, pitting, or unusual wear on the gear teeth. Everything should look uniform \u2014 no bright patches on only one section of a tooth.","Lay a 3/16\" bead of RTV (Permatex Ultra Grey or Mopar RTV) around the cover flange, circling inside each bolt hole. Install the cover and torque the bolts to spec in a crosshatch pattern. Then wait \u2014 RTV needs about an hour of set time before it will hold gear oil. Filling immediately is the classic cause of the slow weep that shows up a week later.","Locate the fill plug on the side of the differential housing (not the cover \u2014 a separate smaller plug on the housing, typically 3/8-inch square drive). Remove it.","After the RTV has set, use a fluid pump or squeeze bottle to pump fresh 75W-140 gear oil into the fill port until fluid begins to run out the hole \u2014 this is the correct fill level indicator (full when it's running out). For Rubicon: add the friction modifier first, then fill with gear oil to overflow.","Reinstall the fill plug. Torque to 30 ft-lbs.","Repeat for the rear differential.","Lower the vehicle, take a short drive, and re-check both fill plugs for leaks."]},{"id":"jt-service-intervals","title":"Jeep Gladiator JT Service Intervals \u2014 What to Do and When","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"maintenance","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":0.5},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":["owner's manual maintenance schedule","OBD2 scanner (oil-life reset)"],"skills_required":[],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["maintenance","service","intervals","oil","fluids","filter"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator JT Owner's Manual \u2014 Maintenance Schedule","url":"https://www.jeep.com/content/dam/fca-brands/na/jeep/en_us/2022/owner-manuals/2022-jeep-gladiator-owners-manual.pdf"}],"alternatives":["jt-oil-change-pentastar","jt-dana44-differential-fluid-change","jt-coolant-flush"],"summary":"The Gladiator JT's maintenance schedule is conservative for street use and not conservative enough for aggressive trail use \u2014 the right intervals depend on what you're actually doing with the truck.","description":"Jeep publishes one maintenance schedule. Most JT owners who trail the truck weekly should treat it as a baseline, not a ceiling. The intervals below cover the factory schedule and note where to pull them in for builds that see real dirt.\n\n**Factory spec:** Full synthetic 0W-20, change every 10,000 miles or 12 months via the Oil Life Monitor (OLM). The 3.6L Pentastar V6 requires synthetic \u2014 do not use conventional oil.\n\n**Trail use adjustment:** Dusty desert runs and extended idle sessions (running the AC while camping) degrade oil faster than highway miles. If the OLM isn't accounting for your use case, change at 5,000\u20137,500 miles. The oil itself tells you \u2014 pull the dipstick at 7,000 miles on a trail-heavy rig and see where the color sits.\n\n**Factory spec:** 30,000 miles or when dirty.\n\n**Trail use adjustment:** After any Moab-level dust trip, pull and inspect the filter. A visually clogged filter (no light visible through the media when held up to sunlight) is due regardless of mileage. Dusty overland drives can clog a filter in 5,000 miles. K&N or aFe reusable filters reduce replacement frequency but require cleaning every 15,000\u201320,000 miles.\n\n**Factory spec:** 100,000 miles (iridium plugs from factory).\n\n**Trail use adjustment:** Plugs at 100K are typically within spec. No adjustment needed unless the engine shows misfires or poor cold starts. Inspect at 80,000 miles on a high-mileage build to catch early erosion.\n\n**Factory spec:** 15,000 miles for the Dana 44 front and rear differentials. Change sooner after water immersion.\n\n**Trail use adjustment:** Change annually or every 10,000 miles for trucks that see creek crossings or standing water. See the Dana 44 Differential Fluid Change guide for procedure and product specs.\n\n**Factory spec:** 60,000 miles (Rock-Trac NV241OR transfer case in Rubicon; Command-Trac NV241 in Sport/Overland).\n\n**Trail use adjustment:** 30,000 miles for trails with regular water crossings. The Rock-Trac case runs ATF+4 fluid \u2014 verify before filling, it's different from the differential spec.\n\n**Factory spec:** 60,000 miles for the 8-speed automatic; inspect only (no change) for the 6-speed manual per factory schedule.\n\n**Trail use adjustment:** On an automatic JT used for towing at the rated 7,650-lb capacity, change at 40,000 miles. The 8HP75 auto runs ATF+4. Manual transmission fluid (GL-4 75W-85) is worth changing at 40,000 miles if you do extended trail use in low range \u2014 the clutch debris ends up in the fluid.\n\n**Factory spec:** Inspect annually; no specific mileage interval given.\n\n**Trail use adjustment:** Flush every 2 years or 30,000 miles on a trail rig. Brake fluid absorbs moisture over time, which lowers its boiling point. A loaded Gladiator descending a rock slab with hot brakes is not the time to discover degraded fluid. Use DOT 4 (factory spec) \u2014 do not use DOT 3.\n\n**Factory spec:** 5 years/150,000 miles (Mopar OAT, orange-yellow formula).\n\n**Trail use adjustment:** Inspect at 60,000 miles in hot climates or if the truck has seen regular extended high-load runs (towing, Rubicon trail crawling in August). Color change to brown is the indicator \u2014 see the Coolant Flush guide.\n\n| Service | Factory interval | Trail-adjusted interval |\n|---|---|---|\n| Engine oil (synthetic 0W-20) | 10,000 mi / OLM | 5,000\u20137,500 mi if dusty/heavy idle |\n| Engine air filter | 30,000 mi | Inspect after dusty trips |\n| Spark plugs (iridium) | 100,000 mi | Inspect at 80,000 mi |\n| Front/rear differential | 15,000 mi | Annually or 10,000 mi (trail) |\n| Transfer case | 60,000 mi | 30,000 mi (trail/water) |\n| Transmission (auto) | 60,000 mi | 40,000 mi (towing) |\n| Brake fluid | 2 years inspect | Flush every 2 years (trail) |\n| Coolant | 5 yr/150K mi | Inspect at 60,000 mi (hot/heavy) |\n| Tire rotation | 5,000\u20137,500 mi | Every oil change |\n| Wheel bearing inspect | 60,000 mi | Inspect annually (off-road) |\n\nMatching the right fluid to each service prevents the expensive mistakes \u2014 the transfer case and the differentials take completely different products.\n\n| System | Fluid spec | Capacity | Mopar part |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Engine oil | 0W-20 full synthetic | 6.0 qt w/ filter | 68218920AB (filter) |\n| Front differential (Dana 44) | 75W-90 synthetic gear oil | ~1.1 qt | 68382517AA |\n| Rear differential (Dana 44) | 75W-90 (+ friction modifier if Trac-Lok) | ~1.5 qt | 68382517AA |\n| Transfer case | ATF+4 | ~1.0 qt | 68218057AC |\n| Automatic transmission (8HP75) | ATF+4 | ~1.9 qt drain/fill | 68218057AC |\n| Brake system | DOT 4 | as needed | 68232360AA |\n| Coolant | Mopar OAT (orange) | ~11 qt | 68163849AB |\n\nThe differentials and transfer case are the pair people mix up. The [Dana 44 differential](/db/gladiator/jt-dana44-differential-fluid-change/) runs gear oil; the [transfer case](/db/gladiator/jt-transfer-case-fluid/) runs ATF+4. Putting gear oil in the transfer case ruins its clutch packs.\n\nSubmerging the axles or transfer case pulls water past the vent tubes and seals. Water in gear oil turns it milky and destroys the ring and pinion within a few hundred miles. After any crossing where water reached the axle tubes or higher:\n\n1. Check the differential fluid for a milky, coffee-with-cream color. If contaminated, change it before the next trail day.\n2. Check the transfer case fill plug for water intrusion the same way.\n3. Inspect the engine air filter \u2014 a soaked filter means water may have reached the intake, which is a far more serious concern.\n4. Extend your axle vent tubes for future trips if crossings are routine; the factory vents sit low.\n\nThe JT's electronic instrument cluster will surface maintenance reminders via the Uconnect system, but it keys primarily off mileage and time \u2014 not use intensity. A rig that spends weekends crawling at 5 mph in low range won't accumulate highway miles quickly, but the drivetrain is working hard. Track fluids by calendar date and off-road trips, not the OLM."},{"id":"jt-spark-plug-replacement","title":"Spark Plug Replacement \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT 3.6L Pentastar","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"maintenance","type":"procedure","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":35,"high":90},"tools_required":["5/8\" spark plug socket","3/8\" or 1/2\" ratchet with 6\" extension","torque wrench","spark plug gap tool","compressed air (for blowing out wells)","dielectric grease","anti-seize compound"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","torque control"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["maintenance","spark-plugs","tune-up","3.6L","ignition"],"parts":[{"name":"NGK Iridium IX Spark Plugs \u2014 3.6L Pentastar (set of 6)","vendor":"NGK","price_estimate_usd":48},{"name":"Champion Platinum Power Spark Plugs \u2014 3.6L (set of 6)","vendor":"Champion","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"Mopar OEM Spark Plugs \u2014 Gladiator JT 3.6L (set of 6)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"Denso Iridium TT Spark Plugs \u2014 3.6L Pentastar (set of 6)","vendor":"Denso","price_estimate_usd":58}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator JT Scheduled Maintenance \u2014 FCA Owner's Manual","url":"https://www.mopar.com"},{"title":"NGK Spark Plug Application Guide \u2014 3.6L Pentastar","url":"https://www.ngksparkplugs.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-differential-fluid-change","jt-service-intervals"],"summary":"The Gladiator's 3.6L Pentastar has six cylinders arranged in a V configuration \u2014 three on each bank. Stellantis recommends spark plug replacement at 60,000 miles. In practice, iridium plugs often go longer; copper plugs need replacement closer to 30,000 miles if the JT does significant idling or towing.","description":"The 3.6L Pentastar is a well-proven engine but the V6 layout means the rear bank (cylinders 4, 5, 6) is significantly harder to access than the front bank. Budget extra time for the rear plugs \u2014 they require working over the top of the engine near the firewall, and a ratchet with a universal joint or swivel extension makes the job manageable without skinning knuckles. A spark plug socket with a rubber insert (to grip the plug during extraction) is mandatory \u2014 dropping a plug down into the cylinder head on the rear bank is a very bad day.\n\n**Factory plug specification:** The JT 3.6L uses a 14mm thread, 5/8\" socket plug gapped at 0.042\". Confirm with your NGK or Denso application guide before purchasing \u2014 plug length matters and an incorrect plug depth can cause pre-ignition damage.\n\n- **Stripped head threads**: If a plug feels loose when torquing (no resistance increasing), stop immediately. The threads are stripped or the plug is cross-threaded. Do not force it. Thread repair inserts (Heli-Coil) can fix this but require careful work.\n- **Broken plug removal**: On high-mileage JTs with original plugs, the ceramic can separate from the steel body during removal. Soak the plugs with penetrating oil two hours before the job if the vehicle has significant mileage or has been sitting.\n- **Rear bank coil accessibility**: Coils 4\u20136 are partially blocked by the firewall and intake runners. A 1/4\" drive ratchet with a short extension sometimes works better than a 3/8\" on the tightest rear coils.\n\nThe plug metal sets both the replacement interval and how consistently the 3.6L fires, so it's worth understanding rather than grabbing whatever the parts counter hands you. Copper plugs conduct best and light off cleanly, but the electrode is soft and erodes fast \u2014 figure 20,000\u201330,000 miles in the Pentastar, which makes them false economy given the rear-bank labor. Platinum plugs use a hard platinum pad on the center electrode and roughly double that life. Iridium goes further still: a fine iridium tip resists erosion at the highest temperatures and holds its gap toward and past the factory 60,000-mile interval while firing more consistently at idle and under load.\n\nFor the Gladiator's naturally aspirated 3.6L, iridium (NGK Iridium IX or Denso Iridium TT) is the right default \u2014 the small premium over copper pays back in a longer interval and one fewer trip into that awkward rear bank. One warning that overrides brand preference: run the factory heat range and gap (0.042\"). Chasing a colder plug because you added a tune or bigger tires without a real forced-induction reason invites fouling and hard starts. Confirm the exact application against the maintenance schedule in [/db/gladiator/jt-service-intervals/](/db/gladiator/jt-service-intervals/) before buying.\n\nThe 60,000-mile figure is a ceiling for gentle highway miles. A JT that idles for winching and compressor work, tows regularly, or spends time in dust and heat loads the plugs harder and shortens the real interval \u2014 pull and inspect at 40,000\u201345,000 if that's your duty cycle rather than trusting the sticker.\n\nThe plugs and the ignition coils fail as a system, and a spark-plug job is the natural moment to think about both. Each cylinder has its own coil-on-plug unit; when one weakens it throws a cylinder-specific misfire code (P0301 through P0306, where the last digit is the cylinder number) and often a P0300 random-misfire alongside. If you're already pulling coils to reach the plugs on a high-mileage JT and one boot is cracked or the truck has logged a misfire on that cylinder, replacing that coil now is cheap insurance against a repeat teardown of the rear bank.\n\nReading the old plugs as they come out is free diagnostic data \u2014 do it before you toss them. A tan-to-light-gray insulator is a healthy, correctly running cylinder. Dry black soot points to a rich condition or heavy idling; wet, oily black points to oil intrusion and is a reason to investigate before buttoning up, since a worsening oil-consumption problem shows here first. A white or blistered insulator suggests running too hot or too lean. If one plug looks dramatically different from its five siblings, that cylinder is telling you something \u2014 chase it before it becomes a misfire on the trail. For related driveline fluid service you can knock out in the same session under the truck, see [/db/gladiator/jt-transfer-case-fluid/](/db/gladiator/jt-transfer-case-fluid/) and [/db/gladiator/jt-oil-change-pentastar/](/db/gladiator/jt-oil-change-pentastar/).\n\nChampion Platinum set of 6: ~$35. Adequate for normal use, shorter service life than iridium.\n\nNGK Iridium IX set of 6: ~$48. The right choice for most JT owners \u2014 iridium lasts longer, fires more consistently, and the price premium over copper pays back in extended interval.\n\nDenso Iridium TT set of 6: ~$58. Slightly better electrode design than standard iridium \u2014 minimal real-world difference from NGK Iridium on a naturally aspirated street/trail JT.\n\nMopar OEM set of 6: ~$65. Factory-spec iridium, carries a Mopar warranty. No performance benefit over NGK or Denso iridium \u2014 the premium is the brand.\n\nShop labor for spark plug replacement on a V6 (accessible bank + rear bank): ~$200\u2013300. The rear bank access is what shops charge for \u2014 it's legitimate labor time. If you're comfortable with the task, the DIY save is real.","steps":["Allow the engine to cool completely before starting. Hot aluminum threads strip more easily than cold ones, and pulling plugs from a warm engine risks galling the threads.","Remove the engine cover (held by friction or plastic push pins depending on trim/year). Set aside.","**Front bank (cylinders 1, 2, 3):** Access is from the top front of the engine bay. Use a spark plug socket on a 6\" extension to reach straight down into each cylinder well. Blow compressed air into the well before removing the plug \u2014 debris falls into the combustion chamber if you skip this step.","Disconnect the ignition coil connector, then remove the coil bolt (8mm). Lift the coil straight up out of the well. Label each coil if you plan to reinstall the same ones (some builders replace coils at this service interval \u2014 $35\u201350 each for aftermarket, $60\u201375 OEM).","Remove the old plug. Inspect the gap and the electrode condition:","Normal wear: rounded center electrode, slight gap growth","Oil fouling (black, oily): oil consumption issue \u2014 investigate before reinstalling","Carbon fouling (dry, black soot): rich mixture or excessive idling","Excessive gap (over 0.055\"): plug past service life \u2014 replace now","Gap new plugs to 0.042\" before installing. Even pre-gapped plugs can shift during shipping.","Apply a thin coat of anti-seize to the threads (first two threads only \u2014 not the full thread length). Do not get anti-seize on the electrode or ground strap.","Thread each plug in by hand until seated, then torque to 13 ft-lbs. Do not exceed \u2014 over-torquing strips the aluminum head threads, which is a $500+ repair.","Apply dielectric grease to the inside of each ignition coil boot before reinstalling. It prevents the boot from bonding to the ceramic plug insulator and makes future coil removal possible without tearing the boot.","**Rear bank (cylinders 4, 5, 6):** Same procedure, but access requires leaning over the engine from the passenger side, or working from below on a lifted vehicle. A 3/8\" ratchet with a universal joint and 6\" extension is the right combination. Take your time \u2014 rushing the rear bank is where mistakes happen.","Reconnect all coil connectors. Reinstall the engine cover.","Start the engine. It may run rough for the first 30 seconds as the new plugs and fresh gaps adjust. A smooth idle should follow quickly."]},{"id":"jt-recovery-gear-essentials","title":"Recovery Gear Essentials for the Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"recovery","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":0.5},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":600},"tools_required":["work gloves","recovery line damper/blanket","rated shackle or soft shackle"],"skills_required":["recovery basics"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["recovery","winch","traction","safety","overlanding","stuck"],"parts":[{"name":"MAXTRAX MKII Recovery Boards (set of 2)","vendor":"MAXTRAX","price_estimate_usd":299},{"name":"Smittybilt XRC Comp Recovery Traction Boards (set of 2)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":89},{"name":"Warn Epic Kinetic Recovery Rope \u2014 7/8\" x 30'","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":149},{"name":"Factor 55 FlatLink Shackle Mount","vendor":"Factor 55","price_estimate_usd":89},{"name":"ARB Snatch Block 8-ton","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":59}],"citations":[{"title":"Tread Lightly Recovery Best Practices","url":"https://www.treadlightly.org"},{"title":"ARB Recovery Gear Application Guide","url":"https://www.arb.com.au/recovery"}],"alternatives":["jt-winch","jt-tow-hook-upgrade","jt-rear-bumper","jt-front-bumper"],"summary":"The Gladiator JT's factory recovery points are functional for a basic strap pull. What's behind them \u2014 and what you carry in the bed \u2014 determines whether you get out on your own or wait for help.","description":"The JT Gladiator comes with two factory front tow hooks (rated at 4,500 lbs each on the Rubicon; standard Steel Tube bumper hooks on Sport/Overland) and a 2\" rear receiver hitch on trucks equipped with the factory tow package (not every Sport has one \u2014 check under the rear bumper, not the spec sheet). The factory hooks are rated for vehicle recovery and adequate for a D-shackle and kinetic rope pull on firm ground. They are not rated for anchor-point winching \u2014 for that, you need a proper front bumper with rated anchor points.\n\n**The recovery gear every JT owner should carry:**\n\n**Kinetic recovery rope:** The single most important piece of recovery equipment. A kinetic rope (also called a snatch rope) stretches under load and stores energy \u2014 when the recovery vehicle accelerates, the rope snaps back and delivers an additional kinetic impulse that breaks the stuck vehicle loose. Traditional tow straps with no stretch can yank shackles loose or snap under dynamic load. Warn's Epic series or ARB's equivalent in 7/8\" x 30' is sized correctly for the JT's weight range. Budget $120\u2013160 for a quality kinetic rope \u2014 this is not the place to buy cheap.\n\n**Traction boards:** MAXTRAX (Australian-designed, widely used in overlanding) and Smittybilt's X20 boards address the most common stuck scenario: the truck has good traction somewhere on the axles, but one or two tires are spinning in loose material. Slide the boards under the spinning tires and drive out. MAXTRAX boards are rugged and reliably grip tire tread; Smittybilt boards are adequate and come in at one-third the price. Mount them on the front bumper, bed rails, or roof rack so they're accessible without unpacking the bed.\n\n**D-shackles or soft shackles:** Two bow shackles rated at 4.75 tons each are the minimum. Factor 55 FlatLink is a shackle-mount upgrade that reduces snag points on a winch hook. Soft shackles (Dyneema construction, rated at 11+ tons) are lighter and safer than steel \u2014 if a soft shackle breaks under load, it drops rather than whipping. Carry at least one soft shackle.\n\n**Snatch block:** A 4WD.com or ARB 8-ton snatch block runs $45\u201360 and doubles your winch's effective pulling capacity by creating a mechanical advantage redirect. If your winch is pulling a stuck JT with 5,500 lbs of load and your winch is rated for 9,500 lbs, a single-block redirect effectively gives you 19,000 lbs of potential \u2014 while also reducing stress on the winch motor and rope. Carry one.\n\n**Gloves:** High-tensile recovery straps and synthetic winch rope store enough energy under tension to injure hands severely if they fail or slip. Leather or high-dexterity work gloves are required for any recovery operation \u2014 not optional.\n\n**Shovel and traction base plate:** A full-size military folding shovel ($25\u201340) digs out a buried axle or creates a base for the high-lift jack on soft ground. A \u00bc\" steel plate (12\" x 12\") under the jack base prevents it from sinking in sand or mud.\n\nThe factory front tow hooks on Rubicon trims are adequate for strap pulls. If you're running a winch on an aftermarket bumper, that bumper's shackle mounts are your primary forward anchor \u2014 verify they're rated for your winch's line pull.\n\nThe rear hitch receiver is not a recovery point. A proper rear recovery setup on the JT uses either a receiver-mounted D-ring (Warn 8-ton receiver shackle, ~$35) or a dedicated rear bumper with rated recovery tabs.\n\n- **Never use chain for kinetic recovery.** Chain doesn't stretch and can send hardware flying at high velocity if it fails.\n- **Never stand in the bight of the rope or strap** during a kinetic recovery pull. If the strap snaps, the energy release is violent and directional.\n- **Never hook a kinetic rope to a trailer ball.** Trailer balls are not rated for recovery loads and have failed catastrophically in field recoveries.\n- **Don't pull from the front tow hooks at an angle greater than 30\u00b0 off-axis.** Lateral loads exceed the hook rating and can pull the mount off the frame.\n\nRecovery gear is sized against the weight of the stuck vehicle, and a JT is heavier than people think: 4,650-5,050 lbs curb depending on trim, call it 5,500-6,000 lbs loaded for a trip. Two numbers appear on gear: MBS (minimum breaking strength \u2014 where it fails) and WLL (working load limit \u2014 what you may load it to, typically MBS divided by a safety factor of 4-5). The rules of thumb:\n\n- **Kinetic rope:** MBS of 2-3x the stuck vehicle's loaded weight. A 7/8\" kinetic rope carries roughly 28,000-29,000 lbs MBS \u2014 correctly in the window for a loaded JT. Bigger is not better: an oversized rope does not stretch enough at JT weights to build the kinetic effect, which is the entire point.\n- **Soft shackles:** MBS 30,000+ lbs is standard for 7/16\" Dyneema \u2014 comfortably adequate.\n- **Steel bow shackles:** the stamped rating is WLL, not MBS. A \"4.75t\" shackle is rated to 10,500 lbs working load with a built-in safety factor above that. Never buy unstamped hardware-store shackles for recovery \u2014 the stamp is the entire difference.\n- **Winch pairing:** rated line pull should be at least 1.5x loaded weight \u2014 for a JT that means the 10,000-12,000 lb class, covered in the JT winch guide.\n\nThe bed is the JT's recovery advantage and its trap: gear loose in a bed becomes a projectile on a hard trail hit, and a kinetic rope baking under a black tonneau in summer sun degrades faster than one stored dry and dark. What works: a lidded crossbed box or milk-crate-and-tie-down setup against the front bulkhead for the rope, shackles, gloves, and snatch block (soft gear stored out of UV); traction boards mounted vertically on bed-rail brackets (~$60-90) where they are reachable with the truck nose-down in a hole; the shovel along a bed rail. Keep one soft shackle and gloves in the cab door pocket \u2014 the recovery where you cannot walk to the bed (deep water, off-camber against a tree) is exactly the one where that matters.\n\nMAXTRAX boards (set of 2): ~$290\u2013310. Worth it if you overland solo in loose terrain. Survivable quality.\n\nSmittybilt X20 boards (set of 2): ~$85\u2013100. The budget choice \u2014 adequate for occasional use, not as durable in high-frequency deployment.\n\nWarn Epic kinetic rope 7/8\" x 30': ~$145\u2013165. Correct sizing for JT weight class. Buy one quality rope rather than two cheap ones.\n\nARB snatch block 8-ton: ~$55\u201365. Required for effective winch use in technical terrain.\n\nComplete kit (kinetic rope + 2 shackles + snatch block + traction boards + gloves): $350\u2013550 depending on board choice. Reasonable baseline for a trail-capable JT."},{"id":"jt-tow-hook-upgrade","title":"Tow Hook and Recovery Point Upgrade \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"recovery","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":59,"high":250},"tools_required":["socket set (metric)","torque wrench","floor jack (if removing skidplate)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["recovery","recovery_points","tow-hooks","D-ring","shackle"],"parts":[{"name":"Smittybilt 3/4\" D-Ring Shackles (pair) \u2014 4.75 ton rated","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":25},{"name":"Factor 55 Flatlink Shackle Mount (fits factory hook opening)","vendor":"Factor 55","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"ARB Recovery Points for JT Front Bumper","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":129},{"name":"Warn Epic Recovery Point Kit \u2014 JT","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":89}],"citations":[{"title":"Factor 55 Flatlink Recovery Point Guide","url":"https://www.factor55.com"},{"title":"ARB JT Gladiator Accessories Fitment","url":"https://www.arbusa.com"},{"title":"Warn JT Epic Recovery Point Product Page","url":"https://www.warn.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-recovery-gear-essentials","jt-winch"],"summary":"The Gladiator JT's factory tow hooks are frame-mounted and rated for recovery \u2014 but the attachment point is a hook, not a shackle-ready loop. Upgrading to proper recovery points gives you a clean, rated connection point for straps, shackles, and snatch blocks.","description":"The JT comes with front tow hooks (standard on all trims) that bolt to the frame rails behind the front bumper. They're functional for light-duty recovery \u2014 a friend pulling you off a soft shoulder \u2014 but the hook geometry makes it difficult to attach a soft shackle cleanly, and there's no industry-standard rating stamped on them. For serious recovery work, you want rated recovery points with clear WLL (working load limit) figures and a shackle mount that won't roll off under load.\n\n**Factory hook situation by trim:**\n- Sport and Willys: front tow hooks, no rear recovery provision beyond the standard receiver hitch\n- Rubicon: same front hooks, rear recovery tab on the frame (but still not a shackle point)\n\nThe rear receiver hitch on all JT trims accepts a standard 2\" receiver D-ring mount \u2014 this is the most straightforward recovery point upgrade on the JT and requires no tools.\n\n**Front recovery point options**\n\n**Factor 55 Flatlink**: The most elegant solution for owners who want to keep the factory bumper. The Flatlink drops into the tow hook opening and uses the factory hardware to provide a flat, shackle-ready connection point rated at 4.75 tons. No bumper removal, no drilling. At ~$65 per side, it's the correct answer for factory bumper setups.\n\n**Warn Epic Recovery Points**: Bolt-in replacements for the factory hooks. Rated, clean mounting, and available in red or black powder coat. The Warn option gives a larger contact surface for soft shackles and is rated clearly at 4.75 tons.\n\n**Aftermarket bumpers with integrated recovery points**: The most capable setup \u2014 Fab Fours, Poison Spyder, Rock-Slide Engineering, and ARB all offer JT-specific steel bumpers with factory-geometry recovery tabs rated at 8,000+ lbs each. If you're replacing the bumper anyway, choose one with proper recovery points built in.\n\nThe JT's long bed creates a challenge for rear recovery \u2014 the factory receiver is functional but low and close to the ground, which creates unfavorable angles for a direct pull. Options:\n\n**Receiver D-ring mount** (~$15\u201325): Slides into the 2\" receiver. Works for straightforward rearward extraction. Not for cross-body pulls \u2014 the shear load on a receiver tube at an angle can damage the hitch.\n\n**Frame-mounted rear recovery points**: Available from Synergy Manufacturing and TeraFlex for the JT. Bolt to the rear frame rails above the bumper. Better attachment geometry for steep extractions or snatch block setups.\n\nRecovery points on the JT should be rated at a minimum of 4.75 tons (9,500 lbs) for single-point pulls. The JT's max GVWR with a full load runs around 5,500 lbs \u2014 a recovery point rated below that is a failure point. Both factory hooks and proper aftermarket points meet this spec; cheap generic D-ring mounts from unknown sellers often don't. Buy name-brand and check the rating stamp.\n\nOnce you have a rated recovery point, what you connect to it matters as much as the point itself. Both steel D-ring (bow) shackles and synthetic soft shackles work on the JT \u2014 the choice is about safety and geometry, not capability.\n\nSoft shackles (Dyneema/UHMWPE rope) win for most trail recovery. They weigh a fraction of a steel shackle, so if something in the system fails, there's far less mass flying. They don't rust, they shed debris, and their flexible loop seats cleanly into the Factor 55 Flatlink or a Warn Epic point without the roll-off problem a screw-pin bow shackle can have. A quality 7/16\" soft shackle is rated well above the JT's recovery loads. The tradeoffs: they abrade on sharp edges and degrade with UV and heat, so inspect them and retire any with cuts or glazing.\n\nSteel bow shackles still earn a place \u2014 they're inexpensive, they take rough handling, and a 3/4\" 4.75-ton shackle is the standard for connecting to a snatch block or a tree-saver anchor. If you run steel, back off the screw pin a quarter-turn after hand-tight so it doesn't seize under load, and never substitute a hardware-store shackle for a rated recovery shackle. This is the connection between a two-ton truck and a shock-loaded line \u2014 the [recovery gear essentials guide](/db/gladiator/jt-recovery-gear-essentials/) covers building the rest of the kit around these points.\n\nRecovery failures on trucks usually trace to a bad attachment choice, not a broken strap. On the JT specifically, avoid these:\n\n- **The tie-down cleats in the bed and the factory bumper eyelets are not recovery points.** They're rated for securing cargo, not for a shock-loaded pull. Use only the frame-mounted hooks or purpose-built rated points.\n- **Never run a strap through a hitch pin or around a receiver tube itself.** Use a rated receiver shackle mount seated fully and pinned. A strap around a bare receiver tube can shear it.\n- **Don't cross-load a screw-pin shackle** (side-load across the pin). Shackles are rated for in-line pull only; an angled load can cut the rating roughly in half.\n- **A kinetic (snatch) rope multiplies force** \u2014 a rated point that's fine for a static winch pull can still be overloaded by an aggressive kinetic yank. Match the recovery method to the point's rating, and when a winch is the safer tool, use it (see the [JT winch guide](/db/gladiator/jt-winch/)).\n\nTreat every recovery as a system: the weakest rated component sets the limit, and the person standing nearest the line is the one who pays if that component was the cheap one.\n\nFactory hook removal (if replacing with Warn or similar):\n1. The tow hooks are held by two bolts each that thread into the frame rail.\n2. Remove the bolts (typically 13mm), pull the hook free, and install the replacement point using the same hardware unless the kit supplies upgraded fasteners.\n3. Torque to the original spec (typically 48\u201355 ft-lbs for frame-mounted components).\n\nFactor 55 Flatlink: drops into the existing hook opening with a clevis pin \u2014 no bolt removal required on most applications.\n\nReceiver D-ring mount (rear, cheap entry): ~$15\u201320. Functional for light recovery, not for serious trail extractions.\n\nFactor 55 Flatlink (pair, front): ~$130. The premium option that works with the factory bumper. The correct choice for owners not replacing the bumper.\n\nWarn Epic Recovery Points (front, pair): ~$89. Quality rating, manageable install, retains factory look.\n\nARB recovery points integrated in their JT bumper: Recovery points come included when replacing the bumper \u2014 not a separate cost. If you're already planning a bumper upgrade, factor this in and skip the standalone recovery point upgrade."},{"id":"jt-winch","title":"Winch for the Gladiator JT \u2014 10,000 lb Minimum","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"recovery","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":349,"high":1200},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","wire crimpers or connectors","electrical tape","multimeter"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","basic electrical"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["winch","recovery","synthetic-rope"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn VR EVO 10-S Winch (10,000 lb)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":449},{"name":"Smittybilt X20 10,000 lb Winch with Synthetic Rope","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":349},{"name":"Warn M10000-S Winch (10,000 lb, synthetic rope)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":749},{"name":"Warn Zeon 10-S Winch (10,000 lb)","vendor":"Warn","price_estimate_usd":1099}],"citations":[{"title":"Warn VR EVO 10-S Product Page","url":"https://www.warn.com/warn-vr-evo-10-s-winch"},{"title":"Winch Sizing Guide \u2014 Off-Road Basics","url":"https://www.warn.com/learn/winch-size-guide"}],"alternatives":["jt-front-bumper","jt-recovery-gear-essentials"],"summary":"The JT Gladiator's GVWR sits between 5,400 and 6,200 lbs depending on trim and options \u2014 a 10,000 lb winch is the correct minimum, and 10K is where most of the best-value hardware lives.","description":"The standard winch sizing formula is 1.5x GVWR. For a JT at roughly 5,600 lbs loaded, that puts you at 8,400 lbs \u2014 and 10,000 lb winches cover that with margin for difficult pulls at steep angles or through mud where load multiplies. Going under-rated on a truck this heavy is a false economy. The 10K class is also where the market concentrates: best selection, most competitive pricing, most accessory ecosystem support.\n\nSynthetic rope over steel cable is the right call for any JT going on real trails. Synthetic rope is lighter, safer when it breaks (it drops rather than whipping), and won't develop the wire burrs that shred gloves. Steel cable has a cost and abrasion advantage in very high-frequency use environments like commercial recovery work, but for trail use, synthetic is safer for everyone nearby. Every winch in this tier is available with synthetic rope \u2014 don't settle for a steel cable version unless you're specifically replacing a winch that already has a drum-worn rope.\n\nThe Warn VR EVO 10-S ($449) is the best-value entry point from the most reliable name in winches. It's not as feature-rich as the Zeon, but it's a genuine Warn product with IP68 water resistance and a three-stage planetary gear train that handles sustained pull without overheating the way budget winches do. The Smittybilt X20 ($349) is legitimate for the price \u2014 it's the entry point for owners who need recovery capability now and will upgrade later. The Warn M10000-S ($749) is the right pick for regular trail use: better thermal management, faster line speed, and a more robust motor than the VR EVO. The Zeon 10-S ($1,099) is a professional-grade winch for owners who use it hard and often.\n\nMost JT aftermarket steel front bumpers include a winch mounting plate at the correct depth for a standard 10\" winch footprint. Confirm your bumper's winch compatibility and fairlead type (hawse vs. roller) before ordering a winch \u2014 synthetic rope requires a hawse fairlead; steel cable uses a roller fairlead.\n\nA 10,000 lb winch pulls serious current under load \u2014 a hard recovery can draw 300-450 amps, and that has implications the winch box doesn't advertise. The JT's factory battery and alternator will run a winch for short, intermittent pulls, but sustained winching (repeated pulls, long drags) can pull the battery down faster than the alternator recovers, especially at idle. Keep the engine running at a fast idle during any pull, and give the charging system time to recover between long drags.\n\nRoute the heavy power leads carefully: the positive and negative cables should run in loom or conduit where they cross the frame, secured every 12\"-18\" so they can't chafe against the bumper or a frame edge. A chafed positive lead against the frame is a dead short at winch amperage \u2014 a fire risk. Use the supplied grommets at every pass-through point.\n\nFor owners who winch often, a dual-battery setup or an upgraded AGM battery is a worthwhile follow-on. It's not required for occasional trail recovery, but it removes the \"will the battery hold\" question on a hard day.\n\nSpool the rope under load before you rely on it \u2014 synthetic rope wound loosely under no tension buries itself into the lower wraps on a real pull and jams or damages the rope. Anchor to a tree, pull the truck forward slowly, and wind the drum tight.\n\nInspect synthetic rope before every trip for a flat, glazed, or fuzzy section \u2014 those are the failure points. Retire rope with any cut strand or heat-glazed section; it will let go under load, and a winch line failure under tension is dangerous to everyone nearby. Always use a line dampener during a pull.\n\nA winch is only as useful as its mount, so a [front bumper](/db/gladiator/jt-front-bumper/) with a winch plate comes first. Round out the kit with [recovery gear essentials](/db/gladiator/jt-recovery-gear-essentials/) \u2014 a snatch block, rated shackles, and a dampener turn the winch into a complete recovery system.","steps":["Confirm the front bumper's winch mounting plate dimensions and fairlead type before ordering.","Mount the winch to the bumper plate using the supplied hardware. Torque to spec.","Thread the synthetic rope through the hawse fairlead and attach the hook.","Route the winch motor leads to the battery. Most installs use ring terminals bolted to the battery \u2014 positive to positive, negative to chassis ground.","Route the control cable to the cab or leave the wireless remote receiver in an accessible location behind the bumper.","Test the winch both in and out under no-load conditions before the first trail day.","Spool the rope evenly under tension \u2014 do this by attaching to a tree and pulling the JT forward slowly until the drum is full."],"pros":["10K rating covers the JT's GVWR with recovery margin","Synthetic rope is safer and lighter than steel cable","Bumper-mounted integration is clean and doesn't sacrifice hitch access","Wide accessory ecosystem: snatch blocks, dampeners, straps are universal across brands"],"cons":["Winch without a compatible bumper is a useless purchase \u2014 bumper must come first","Adds 65\u201395 lbs to the front end \u2014 affects steering feel and front suspension load","Hitch-mounted winch receivers exist and work, but a hitch mount can't pull you forward when you're facing the obstacle","Synthetic rope requires periodic inspection for fraying and UV degradation"]},{"id":"jt-power-steering-fluid","title":"Power Steering Fluid Service \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"steering","type":"procedure","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":0.5},"cost_usd":{"low":10,"high":30},"tools_required":["turkey baster or fluid transfer pump","funnel","nitrile gloves","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic maintenance"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["steering","power-steering","maintenance","fluid"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar Power Steering Fluid (MS-10838 spec)","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Prestone AS261F Power Steering Fluid (universal compatible)","vendor":"Prestone","price_estimate_usd":8}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator JT Owner's Manual \u2014 Power Steering Fluid Spec","url":"https://www.mopar.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-steering-shaft-recall","jt-steering-stabilizer-upgrade"],"summary":"The JT Gladiator's hydraulic power steering system uses Mopar MS-10838-spec fluid. It's checked less often than engine oil and fails less dramatically \u2014 which is exactly why it gets neglected until the whining starts.","description":"The JT's hydraulic power steering (not electric \u2014 the Gladiator retained hydraulic assist where the lighter JL Wrangler moved to electric on some configurations) runs a reservoir under the hood accessible from the top. The fluid degrades over time and contaminates with metal particles from the pump and gear. Dark, murky fluid that smells burnt is past its service life. Clean power steering fluid is amber to red; spent fluid is dark brown.\n\nThere's no hard factory service interval for the power steering fluid \u2014 FCA treats it as a lifetime fill on most service schedules. In practice, owners who flush it at 50,000-mile intervals report noticeably lighter steering feel afterward. For JTs running larger tires (35s or bigger), where the power steering system works harder than designed, a shorter interval makes sense.\n\n- **Whining or groaning when turning at full lock**: Often low fluid or air in the system, not a pump failure \u2014 check the reservoir first.\n- **Steering feels heavy or inconsistent**: Degraded fluid or low level. Top off and evaluate.\n- **Fluid is dark brown or smells burnt**: Time for a flush regardless of mileage.\n- **Foam visible in the reservoir**: Air in the system \u2014 usually a low fluid level that allowed the return line to draw air. Fill to the correct level and cycle the steering lock-to-lock several times with the engine running to purge the air.\n\n1. Locate the power steering reservoir on the passenger side of the engine bay on the JT 3.6L (position may vary slightly \u2014 follow the high-pressure hose from the steering gear up to the reservoir).\n2. Wipe the reservoir cap and surrounding area clean before removing \u2014 debris falls in easily.\n3. The dipstick is integral to the cap. Check the fluid level against the MIN/MAX marks. The JT has separate marks for cold and hot fluid \u2014 check hot only after the engine has reached operating temperature.\n4. Correct level is between MIN and MAX. Never overfill.\n\n1. Use a turkey baster or fluid transfer pump to draw as much old fluid as possible out of the reservoir.\n2. Refill with fresh fluid to the MAX mark.\n3. Start the engine and turn the steering wheel lock-to-lock three times. This circulates fresh fluid through the system and pushes old fluid back to the reservoir.\n4. Shut down, draw old fluid from the reservoir again, and refill with fresh fluid.\n5. Repeat cycles 3\u20134 until the fluid flowing back into the reservoir is clean and light in color.\n6. Top off to MAX with fresh fluid. Check for leaks at the reservoir and hose connections.\n\nThis method isn't a complete flush \u2014 some old fluid remains in the system \u2014 but it's a 10-minute job that refreshes 70\u201380% of the fluid volume. A full power flush requires a pressure flushing machine and is best done at a shop if you want a complete exchange.\n\nThe JT left the factory on 33-inch tires (32-inch on non-Rubicon trims). Move up to 35s or 37s and the hydraulic system works measurably harder \u2014 more contact patch means more effort at the pump, higher fluid temperatures, and more shear on the fluid itself. The factory \"lifetime fill\" assumption was written around stock rubber. On a lifted JT running larger tires, treat the fluid as a 40,000-50,000 mile service item, not a fill-and-forget.\n\nHeat is the enemy. Fluid that runs hot on repeated low-speed lock-to-lock steering \u2014 rock crawling, tight trail work, long stretches of parking-lot maneuvering with a heavy front end \u2014 degrades faster and can foam. If you wheel a JT on [35s](/db/gladiator/jt-35-inch-tire-fitment/) or [37s](/db/gladiator/jt-tire-fitment-37s/), inspect the fluid color at every oil change and flush on the shorter interval. A cooler is rarely necessary on a street-and-trail JT, but the fluid service cadence has to come down.\n\nA whine that persists after you've confirmed the fluid is full and clean is not always the fluid. Rule out the cheap causes first \u2014 low level, air in the system, degraded fluid \u2014 because those are a ten-minute fix. If the whine continues:\n\n- **Whine that rises and falls with engine RPM, not steering input:** Often a failing pump, or a restriction in the high-pressure line. A pump on a high-mileage JT is a known wear item.\n- **Groan only at full lock:** Frequently normal on the JT's hydraulic system near the relief valve \u2014 but if it's new and getting louder, check for a kinked or chafed pressure hose.\n- **Whine plus a wandering, vague on-center feel:** Look past the pump to the steering box and linkage. A tired box or worn coupler produces symptoms fluid can't fix \u2014 the [steering shaft recall guide](/db/gladiator/jt-steering-shaft-recall/) covers the coupler side of that on early trucks.\n\nDon't throw a pump at a whine before you've confirmed the fluid and the lines. The pump is the expensive answer, and it's frequently the wrong one.\n\nMS-10838 is a dedicated hydraulic steering fluid spec, and the top-off aisle is full of bottles that don't meet it. ATF \u2014 including ATF+4, which some older Chrysler steering systems did accept \u2014 is not the answer here; transmission fluid carries a different additive package and friction modifiers the steering system doesn't want. \"Universal\" power steering fluids are acceptable only when the label explicitly lists MS-10838 among the met specifications; a bottle that says \"compatible with most makes\" without naming the spec is a guess, and the guess costs seal swelling and foaming. If the wrong fluid has already gone in, don't dilute it with correct fluid and hope \u2014 do the repeated drain-and-refill cycles until the reservoir runs clean.\n\nLow fluid with no puddle on the floor is the common presentation, and it usually means a slow weep rather than a drip. Work through the circuit: the reservoir cap o-ring and reservoir seams (look for wet dust rather than liquid), the spring clamps on the low-pressure return hose, the crimped ends of the high-pressure line, and the input and sector shaft seals on the steering gear itself. Gear-seal weepage runs down the housing and gets blown back along the axle, so a \"dry\" engine bay with an oily film on the front axle tube points at the gear. A UV dye kit \u2014 a few dollars, added to the reservoir and inspected with a blacklight after a week of driving \u2014 settles ambiguous cases. Catching a weeping return hose costs a clamp; running the pump dry costs a pump. On a truck already showing the whine symptoms above, verify level and leaks before condemning hardware.\n\nMopar MS-10838 fluid: ~$12 per quart (dealer). The spec-correct option.\n\nPrestone universal power steering fluid: ~$8 per quart. Compatible with FCA systems when labeled as universal or meeting MS-10838 equivalent. Check the label before purchasing.\n\nShop power steering flush (machine flush): ~$80\u2013120. Worth considering at 100,000+ miles or if the fluid is particularly contaminated. The machine flush exchanges more fluid than the baster method."},{"id":"jt-steering-shaft-recall","title":"Gladiator steering shaft recall (2020\u20132022)","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"steering","subcategory":"steering shaft","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":2.0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":["flashlight"],"skills_required":["visual inspection","recall verification"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2022"],"tags":["steering shaft","recall","TSB","intermediate shaft","2020","2021","2022","safety","NHTSA"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"NHTSA Recall 21V-368 \u2014 Jeep Gladiator Intermediate Steering Shaft","url":"https://www.nhtsa.gov/recalls"},{"title":"Stellantis recall center","url":"https://www.mopar.com/en-us/care/recalls.html"}],"alternatives":["jt-power-steering-fluid","jt-steering-stabilizer-upgrade"],"summary":"Stellantis issued a recall covering early Gladiators (2020\u20132022) for an intermediate steering shaft that could fracture under stress, resulting in loss of steering control. This is a safety recall \u2014 verify your VIN and confirm the repair has been completed before driving the truck.","description":"The intermediate steering shaft connects the steering column to the steering gear. On affected vehicles, the shaft assembly could fracture at the coupler under load conditions \u2014 particularly aggressive steering inputs, off-road use, or high steering loads. A fractured intermediate shaft means partial or complete loss of steering control.\n\nStellantis issued Recall 21V-368 (and a related service bulletin) covering 2020\u20132021 Gladiators manufactured within a specific production window. The fix is dealer-performed replacement of the intermediate steering shaft with an updated part.\n\nNot all Gladiators in this model year range are affected \u2014 the recall covers a specific production date range. The only way to know if your truck is in scope is to check the NHTSA recall database by VIN.\n\nGo to nhtsa.gov/recalls and enter your 17-character VIN. The site will return any open recalls associated with your specific vehicle. If the steering shaft recall appears and shows \"remedy not yet received,\" contact a Stellantis dealer and schedule the recall repair. This is covered under the recall at no cost.\n\nIf the recall shows \"remedy completed,\" the dealer has already performed the shaft replacement on your vehicle. The work should be documented in your service history.\n\nIf you own an early JT and haven't verified recall status, here's what an affected or marginal intermediate shaft may feel like:\n\n- **Loose or clunky steering:** A notchy or clunky sensation when turning, particularly at slow speeds or parking maneuvers\n- **Play in the wheel:** Noticeable dead zone at center before steering response begins\n- **Click or clunk on full lock:** A sharp sound at or near full steering lock \u2014 can indicate coupler binding or wear\n- **Steering effort change:** Sudden increase in required steering effort, particularly under load\n\nNone of these symptoms is confirmation that the shaft is about to fail \u2014 other steering components (track bar, ball joints, tie rod ends) create similar symptoms. But if your truck is in the recall range and hasn't had the recall completed, these symptoms make the verification more urgent.\n\nA dealer-replaced intermediate shaft on a 2021\u20132022 JT is the updated production part. The replacement part is not the same component that was recalled \u2014 it's the corrected design.\n\nIf you've had the recall completed and still experience steering symptoms, those are coming from elsewhere: track bar, ball joints, tie rod ends, or alignment. The shaft replacement addresses the specific fracture failure mode, not all steering feel issues.\n\nRecall status is one of the first things to verify on any 2020-2022 Gladiator you're considering. A seller who says \"it's had all its recalls done\" is not proof \u2014 get the VIN and check it yourself at nhtsa.gov/recalls before money changes hands. An open steering-shaft recall isn't a dealbreaker (the fix is free and any Stellantis dealer can perform it), but it tells you the previous owner wasn't tracking safety campaigns, which is worth factoring into how the rest of the truck was likely maintained.\n\nPull the truck onto level ground and rock the steering wheel back and forth at a standstill with the engine running. Excessive dead play at center, or a distinct clunk, can come from the intermediate shaft coupler, the track bar mounts, or worn tie-rod ends \u2014 you won't isolate which from the driver's seat, but it flags that the front end needs a closer look. If you're weighing a specific truck, the [payload and towing reality guide](/db/gladiator/payload-towing-reality/) and the [frame bend recall guide](/db/gladiator/frame-bend-towing-recall/) cover the other two early-JT items worth checking on the same walkaround.\n\nThe steering shaft recall is a single failure mode, not the whole front end. If your JT wanders, shimmies, or feels vague after the recall is confirmed complete, work through the rest of the steering system before spending on parts. Fresh, correct-spec fluid in the hydraulic assist matters more than most owners assume \u2014 see [power steering fluid service](/db/gladiator/jt-power-steering-fluid/). A worn or undersized damper is often blamed for symptoms it can't fix; the [steering stabilizer upgrade guide](/db/gladiator/jt-steering-stabilizer-upgrade/) explains when a stabilizer actually helps and when it only masks a worn component that needs replacing.\n\nThe VIN lookup answers the recall question; a physical check answers the wear question. With the engine off and the key in a position that unlocks the column, have a helper rock the steering wheel back and forth through a few degrees of rotation while you watch the intermediate shaft \u2014 follow the column through the firewall down to the steering gear input. You are looking for lag: the column turning before the gear input responds, or visible relative movement at either U-joint or at the coupler. A healthy shaft transmits motion with zero perceptible delay.\n\nTwo cautions. First, this inspection finds a worn coupler or U-joint; it cannot find the fracture risk the recall addresses, which is a material defect inside a part that looks fine until it lets go. A clean visual check is not a substitute for the VIN lookup or the dealer repair. Second, don't confuse steering-box input play with shaft play \u2014 if the shaft moves as one piece but the pitman arm lags behind it, the slack is in the gear, which is a separate (and not recall-covered) diagnosis.\n\nThe NHTSA VIN tool returns every open campaign on the truck, not only the steering shaft, so read the whole list while you're there. Early Gladiators accumulated several campaigns worth confirming: the [frame bend towing recall](/db/gladiator/frame-bend-towing-recall/) on trucks used for heavy towing, and \u2014 on six-speed manual trucks \u2014 the clutch pressure-plate campaign that FCA issued across the JL/JT manual lineup. None of these cost the owner anything to remedy, and a truck carrying multiple open campaigns tells you something about how attentively it was owned.\n\nKeep the paper trail. When a dealer completes a recall, ask for the repair order and file it with the service records \u2014 recall completion status is queryable by VIN by any future buyer, and a documented history is worth real money when you sell. If you're the buyer instead, the same lookup costs nothing and takes two minutes; run it before you negotiate, not after.","steps":["Locate your VIN (driver's door jamb sticker or dashboard through the windshield)","Go to nhtsa.gov/recalls and enter the VIN","Note whether the steering shaft recall appears and whether the remedy has been completed","If open: contact a Stellantis/Jeep dealer and schedule the recall repair","While waiting for the repair, drive carefully and avoid aggressive steering inputs at speed","After repair completion: verify the dealer recorded the completed recall in your service history"],"pros":["The recall repair is free \u2014 no cost to the owner","The replacement part resolves the specific fracture failure mode","Stellantis's recall process is accessible and straightforward"],"cons":["This is a safety recall; driving an unrepaired truck is a real risk","Dealer scheduling during recall campaigns can create wait times","Used-market JTs may have unknown recall status \u2014 always verify VIN before purchase"]},{"id":"jt-steering-stabilizer-upgrade","title":"Steering Stabilizer Upgrade \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"steering","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":280},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","18mm socket","ratchet","torque wrench","pry bar"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","safe vehicle support"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["steering","stabilizer","shimmy","suspension","handling"],"parts":[{"name":"Bilstein 24-185535 Steering Stabilizer for JT","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":139},{"name":"Fox 985-24-138 Performance Series Steering Stabilizer","vendor":"Fox","price_estimate_usd":189},{"name":"Rough Country 8740630 Steering Stabilizer for JT","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":89},{"name":"Teraflex 1951050 Falcon SC 2.1 Steering Stabilizer","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":279}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator JT Steering System \u2014 Mopar Parts Guide","url":"https://www.moparparts.com"},{"title":"Bilstein B8 Steering Stabilizer Application Guide","url":"https://www.bilstein.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-power-steering-fluid","jt-steering-shaft-recall"],"summary":"A steering stabilizer dampens high-frequency steering inputs \u2014 it's a shock absorber for the steering linkage, not a fix for worn steering components. Upgrading it matters, but know what it does and what it can't do before you buy.","description":"The Gladiator JT comes from the factory with a single steering stabilizer mounted horizontally on the front axle. The factory unit is adequate for stock tire sizes and moderate trail use. When you go to 35\" tires, add a lift, and start driving wash-corrugated desert trails, the stock damper runs out of damping range \u2014 you feel it as increased steering effort on rough surfaces and occasional shimmy at highway speeds on uneven pavement.\n\n**What a steering stabilizer does:** Absorbs small, high-frequency steering inputs from road imperfections, rocks, and tire imbalance. Reduces driver fatigue on rough surfaces. Helps prevent steering shimmy from turning into a full-speed oscillation event.\n\n**What a steering stabilizer doesn't do:** Fix death wobble caused by worn ball joints, bad tie rods, a loose track bar bolt, or a damaged wheel bearing. If the JT is experiencing a violent, uncontrollable shimmy at speed (death wobble), a stronger stabilizer will mask the symptom temporarily, not address the cause. Check and tighten every front-end steering and suspension fastener before throwing an upgraded stabilizer at the problem.\n\n**Single vs. dual stabilizer:** The JT has enough front-end geometry that a single upgraded stabilizer handles most builds correctly, even with 37\" tires. Dual stabilizers (a crossover configuration) are typically reserved for extreme rock crawler builds on heavily leveraged axle widths. For overlanders and moderate trail rigs, a quality single unit is the right call.\n\n**Budget trail rig (33\"\u201335\" tires, light trail):** Rough Country's single stabilizer runs $85\u201395 and fits the factory mounting points. It's a step up from stock without the premium price. Ride quality improvement is modest but consistent.\n\n**Overland and 35\" tire builds:** Bilstein's 24-185535 ($135\u2013145) is the most commonly recommended upgrade in this segment \u2014 genuine improvement in highway high-speed damping, and the Bilstein build quality means it won't blow out on a rock ledge. Fox's Performance Series ($185\u2013200) is in the same tier.\n\n**37\" tire and lifted builds:** Teraflex's Falcon SC 2.1 ($275\u2013290) uses an independent compression and rebound adjustment system that allows tuning for your specific tire size and use case. If you're running 37s and doing serious trail work, the adjustability matters.\n\n1. Position the vehicle on level ground, engage the parking brake. The stabilizer swap doesn't require jacking the vehicle \u2014 it's accessible from under the front end with the vehicle at ride height.\n2. Locate the factory stabilizer. It mounts horizontally between the passenger-side tie rod bracket and a mount on the driver-side frame/axle area. Note the orientation before removing \u2014 the new unit needs to install in the same direction.\n3. Remove the mounting bolts at both ends. The factory JT uses 18mm bolts at the axle end mount and a stud-mount configuration at the drag link bracket. Note which nut positions came from where.\n4. Pull the old stabilizer out. Inspect the mounting brackets and bushings while it's out \u2014 if the brackets show cracks or the rubber bushings are shredded, replace them before installing the new unit.\n5. Install the new stabilizer. Most aftermarket units come with replacement bushings \u2014 use them. Thread the bolts in hand-tight to allow the unit to align naturally before torquing.\n6. Torque the mounting bolts to 55 ft-lbs at the axle end mount and 50 ft-lbs at the drag link end (factory spec \u2014 verify against your unit's instructions, as some aftermarket kits specify different torque values).\n7. Cycle the steering lock-to-lock by hand. Confirm the stabilizer moves freely through full extension and compression without binding. If it binds, the mounting points are misaligned \u2014 loosen, reposition, and retorque.\n8. Drive on pavement and evaluate steering feel at 55\u201365 mph. The change in highway shimmy damping should be noticeable if the stock unit was worn.\n\n- **Binding at full turn**: Verify the new stabilizer doesn't go into coil bind (full compression) at full steering lock. Some longer aftermarket stabilizers bind at full turn on lifted JTs \u2014 confirm the spec matches your lift height before ordering.\n- **Masking real issues**: If shimmy returns after 500 miles with the new stabilizer, the root cause is mechanical wear elsewhere. Inspect ball joints, tie rod ends, and track bar bushing.\n- **Check the drag link**: While you have the front end accessible, inspect the drag link for bends, especially if the JT has had a previous owner or trail use. A bent drag link causes persistent shimmy that no stabilizer fixes.\n\nA steering stabilizer is a damper, not a repair, and installing a stronger one over a worn front end hides a safety problem instead of fixing it. Before spending on an upgrade, put the front axle on stands and work through the components that actually cause shimmy and death wobble on the JT. Grab each front tire at 12 and 6 o'clock and rock it - play here points to ball joints or a wheel bearing. Grab at 3 and 9 o'clock and rock - play points to the tie rod or drag link ends. Have a helper turn the wheel while you watch the track bar bolt at the axle bracket; any movement there means the bolt is loose or the hole has elongated, and that single bolt is the most common death-wobble trigger on lifted Jeeps.\n\nCheck the track bar bushings, the control-arm bushings, and the steering shaft coupler as well - early JTs had a [steering shaft recall](/db/gladiator/jt-steering-shaft-recall/) worth confirming was completed on any used truck. Torque every steering and suspension fastener to spec before you drive. Only after the front end is tight does a stabilizer upgrade do what it's meant to: smooth the small, high-frequency inputs on rough surfaces. If a violent shimmy appears at speed, slow down under control, come to a stop, and inspect - do not rely on a damper to contain it. A tired [power steering system](/db/gladiator/jt-power-steering-fluid/) can add vague on-center feel on top of all this, so rule the cheap causes out first.\n\nRough Country single stabilizer (budget): ~$85\u201395. Works as described, won't impress you, won't disappoint you.\n\nBilstein 24-185535 (recommended mid-tier): ~$135\u2013150. Available at Summit Racing, 4WD.com, most off-road shops. The right choice for most builds under 37\".\n\nFox Performance Series: ~$185\u2013205. Slight edge in feel over the Bilstein on very rough high-speed terrain.\n\nTeraflex Falcon SC 2.1 (adjustable): ~$270\u2013295. Worth the price if you're dialing in a heavily modified rig.\n\nInstallation is 30\u201345 minutes with basic tools \u2014 no shop visit required."},{"id":"jt-budget-lift","title":"Budget Lift Kit for the Gladiator (\u2264$600)","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":149,"high":599},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","pry bar"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","wheel removal"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["lift","suspension","budget","spacer"],"parts":[{"name":"Rough Country 2\" Lift Kit for Gladiator JT","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":199},{"name":"Teraflex 1.5\" Spacer Lift Kit for Gladiator JT","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":299},{"name":"Daystar 2\" Coil Spacer Kit for JT","vendor":"Daystar","price_estimate_usd":149}],"citations":[{"title":"Gladiator JT Lift Kit Guide \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/c/journal/jeep-gladiator-lift-kit-buyers-guide"},{"title":"Teraflex JT Spacer Lift Installation","url":"https://www.teraflex.com/products/gladiator"}],"alternatives":["jt-mid-lift","jt-expedition-lift"],"summary":"A budget lift gets your Gladiator off stock ride height for a better stance and a little extra tire clearance \u2014 but know what you're paying for and what you're not.","description":"Under $600 buys you coil spacers or a basic coil replacement up front, and that's roughly it. The Gladiator JT runs a coil-sprung front \u2014 same architecture as the JL Wrangler \u2014 but uses leaf springs in the rear. That rear leaf spring setup is the key detail that separates the JT from the JL, and it's why most budget kits only address the front. A 2\" front spacer on a stock JT will leave the rear sitting lower than the front unless you add AAL (add-a-leaf) packs or swap to longer rear leaf springs. Some builders intentionally run a slight nose-up rake for clearance; most prefer level. Factor that into your budget before you order.\n\nThe Rough Country 2\" kit ($199) is the most affordable complete solution, including a basic rear block to address the rake issue. It won't transform the suspension geometry, but it will clear 33s without rubbing and give you the stance upgrade most Gladiator owners want in year one. The Teraflex 1.5\" spacer kit ($299) is a better-quality option that uses CNC-machined billet spacers and includes all hardware \u2014 it's the right call if you want something you won't replace in 18 months. Daystar's 2\" coil spacers ($149) are the barebones option: front spacers only, no rear component, intended for owners who want a level look on pavement or light gravel.\n\nNone of these kits include geometry correction. That means no adjustable control arms, no alignment cam bolts, no caster correction. At 1.5\"\u20132\" of lift, the factory geometry is generally acceptable, but you will need a front-end alignment after install regardless. Budget $80\u2013$120 for the alignment shop visit \u2014 it's not optional. If you're planning to push to 35\" tires, a budget spacer kit is a reasonable starting point, but you'll want to add alignment cam bolts and potentially a track bar drop bracket to keep the handling honest.\n\nA spacer lift raises the truck; it does not improve how the suspension works. You get the stance and enough clearance for 33s, but the shocks are still the factory units now running at the top of their travel, the control arms are still fixed-length, and caster still drifts out of spec above 2\". None of that matters much at 1.5\"-2\" on a street-and-gravel JT. It matters a lot if you're headed for 35s and real trails.\n\nThe honest decision point: if 33\" tires and a level stance is the goal, a budget spacer kit is money well spent. If you already know you want 35s, a bumper, and armor, buying a spacer now means buying a [mid-tier kit](/db/gladiator/jt-mid-lift/) later and eating the cost of the spacers \u2014 go straight to the mid-tier. Don't buy a budget lift as a \"foundation\" for a build that's going bigger; it isn't one.\n\nTorque the lug nuts to 100-110 ft-lbs and re-check them after the first 50 miles \u2014 new wheel seating loosens them. If you added a rear block, watch for axle wrap under hard throttle: blocks increase the leverage that lets the [rear axle rotate](/db/gladiator/factory-leaf-spring-axle-wrap/), and a budget rear block is the setup most prone to it. A shudder under acceleration after the lift is that, not a drivetrain fault.\n\nDon't run a spacer kit past 2.5\". Beyond that the factory shocks top out, the ride gets harsh at extension, and you risk overextending the front CV and axle components. If you want more than 2.5\", it's a [mid-tier kit](/db/gladiator/jt-mid-lift/) with replacement coils and shocks, not more spacer.\n\nThe budget kits handle the front with spacers; how they handle the rear varies, and the rear choice matters more than the front on a leaf-sprung truck. A **block** between the axle and the leaf pack is the cheapest path and the one most budget kits take. It adds no spring rate \u2014 ride stays factory \u2014 but it lengthens the lever arm the axle uses to twist the pack under throttle, which is why blocks and [axle wrap](/db/gladiator/factory-leaf-spring-axle-wrap/) travel together; keep blocks to 1.5\" or less. An **add-a-leaf** gains similar height by adding a leaf to the factory pack. It raises spring rate, so the truck carries bed weight better and wraps less, at the cost of a stiffer unloaded ride \u2014 the right call for a JT that regularly hauls. **Longer shackles** are the option to skip: they change the spring's operating angle, eat droop travel, and put the extra length into a pivoting link \u2014 the height gain isn't worth the geometry cost on this truck.\n\nThe install isn't finished when the wheels go back on. Re-aim the headlights \u2014 2\" of lift moves the cutoff line enough to blind oncoming traffic and to shorten your own usable beam. Get the alignment within the first 30 miles, re-torque lug nuts at 50 miles, and re-check rear U-bolt torque between 100 and 500 miles, because fresh U-bolts seat into the spring pack and lose preload. Cycle the front suspension to full droop (jack under the frame, wheels hanging) and confirm the brake lines and ABS wiring still have slack; spacer kits rarely exceed the factory line length at 2\", but confirming costs five minutes and a failure costs a brake circuit. If the truck picks up a vibration at highway speed that wasn't there before, look at the rear driveline angle before blaming the kit \u2014 a rear block changes pinion angle slightly, and the fix is a degree shim, not a return label.","steps":["Break the lug nuts loose on both front wheels before jacking up the vehicle.","Support the front axle under the differential with a floor jack, then raise and secure the vehicle on jack stands at the frame.","Remove the front wheels.","Unbolt the front sway bar end links \u2014 you'll need slack to lower the axle enough to install spacers.","Lower the jack slightly to decompress the coil springs. Install the coil spacers per the kit instructions, seating the spacer on top of the coil spring or on the upper mount depending on kit design.","Reinstall end links (use new hardware if provided), reinstall wheels, torque lug nuts to spec (100\u2013110 ft-lbs for JT).","For the rear (if your kit includes a rear component): place the axle on jack stands, remove the rear leaf spring U-bolts, insert the supplied block between the axle and leaf pack, reinstall and torque U-bolts to spec.","Lower the vehicle and get a front-end alignment before driving more than 20\u201330 miles."],"pros":["Lowest cost entry point for improved stance and minor tire clearance","Front install is beginner-friendly \u2014 no spring compressors required for spacer kits","Reversible \u2014 returns to stock by removing spacers","Clears 33\" tires on all JT trims with 2\" of lift"],"cons":["No geometry correction \u2014 alignment required, and handling degrades more noticeably above 2\"","Most kits do not fully address the rear leaf spring height \u2014 rake is common","Ride quality unchanged or slightly worse with spacers vs. replacement coils","Not a foundation for 35s or heavier wheeling; upgrading later means buying again"]},{"id":"jt-expedition-lift","title":"Expedition Lift for the Gladiator \u2014 Long-Arm System ($2,000+)","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":20,"high":28},"cost_usd":{"low":2500,"high":5500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","breaker bar","spring compressor","angle grinder","welder or MIG access","tape measure","alignment tool"],"skills_required":["suspension work","fabrication","torque spec adherence","driveline awareness"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["lift","long-arm","suspension","expedition"],"parts":[{"name":"Rock Krawler 3.5\" Mid-Arm JT Lift Kit","vendor":"Rock Krawler","price_estimate_usd":3999},{"name":"EVO Mfg 4\" Long-Arm JT Lift Kit","vendor":"EVO Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":4299},{"name":"Synergy Manufacturing 4\" JT Long-Arm Suspension System","vendor":"Synergy Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":4499}],"citations":[{"title":"Rock Krawler JT Mid-Arm System Overview","url":"https://www.rockkrawler.com/gladiator-jt-suspension"},{"title":"EVO Mfg JT Long-Arm System \u2014 Install Manual","url":"https://evomfg.com/gladiator-jt-long-arm"}],"alternatives":["jt-mid-lift","jt-budget-lift"],"summary":"A long-arm lift system transforms the Gladiator JT from a capable truck into a full-capability rig \u2014 but the JT's 137\" wheelbase and pickup platform introduce geometry considerations that don't exist on a Wrangler.","description":"The Gladiator JT has a 137\" wheelbase \u2014 19\" longer than the JL Unlimited. That extra length is the reason you can't bolt up a JL long-arm kit and call it done. Longer lower control arms change the pinion angle and caster geometry differently on the JT's longer frame. Quality JT-specific long-arm kits account for this with JT-tuned mounting brackets, appropriately lengthed arms, and driveshaft geometry calculations that reflect the JT's extended frame rails. Before ordering, confirm the kit is specifically engineered for JT \u2014 not a JL kit with \"JT compatible\" in the title.\n\nThe Rock Krawler 3.5\" Mid-Arm system ($3,999) is the most popular entry point for serious JT builds. \"Mid-arm\" here means longer-than-stock lower arms without relocating the frame mounting points \u2014 a meaningful upgrade in wheel travel and articulation without the full crossmember surgery of a true long-arm. EVO Mfg's 4\" Long-Arm ($4,299) does relocate the lower control arm mounts forward for maximum arm length, which delivers the best wheel travel and most linear suspension feel but requires more installation time and more care around the driveshafts. Synergy's 4\" system ($4,499) takes a similar approach with Synergy's adjustable arm design, which is the right call if you're planning future fine-tuning of the geometry.\n\nAt 3.5\"\u20134\" of lift with long arms, you're looking at 37\" tires as the natural fitment target. On Rubicon and Mojave trims, the Dana 44 fronts are already there. On Sport, Overland, and Willys trims with Dana 30 fronts, a 37\" setup is possible but the front axle becomes the weak link under hard use. Budget for a front locker and upgraded axle shafts if you're wheeling the Dana 30 on 37s regularly. Driveshaft upgrades are also worth pricing in advance \u2014 at 4\" of lift, the factory driveshaft U-joint angles push the limits of durability, especially on the rear.\n\nThe two systems in this tier solve different problems, and paying for the wrong one is a common way to overspend on a JT build. A mid-arm system (like the Rock Krawler 3.5\") lengthens the control arms without relocating the frame mounts. It's a large gain in travel and articulation over a stock-arm lift, bolts in with far less fabrication, and keeps the belly closer to stock height \u2014 the right call for an overland and moderate-trail JT that also sees highway miles. A true long-arm (EVO, Synergy 4\") relocates the lower mounts forward for maximum arm length and the flattest, most linear suspension curve; it earns its cost on a dedicated rock crawler where droop and articulation are the whole point, and it accepts the tradeoffs of more install time, more belly-height loss, and closer driveshaft-angle management.\n\nIf your honest use is trails and travel on 35s or 37s, a mid-arm system is frequently the better-matched purchase. If you're building a technical rig that lives in low range, the long-arm is worth the surgery. Compare the tier below this one in the [mid-lift guide](/db/gladiator/jt-mid-lift/) before committing \u2014 many JTs are better served staying there.\n\nAt 3.5\"-4\" of lift, the driveline is where a JT build succeeds or rattles itself apart. The rear driveshaft U-joint angle climbs with lift, and the JT's long wheelbase makes this less forgiving than a Wrangler's. Check static operating angles with a digital gauge; anything past roughly 3 degrees points to a double-cardan (CV) driveshaft or a pinion-angle correction, not a shim-and-hope fix. Axle wrap under power gets worse with taller springs and bigger tires \u2014 if you feel a shudder on throttle, address it at the source rather than adding a band-aid; the [factory leaf spring axle wrap guide](/db/gladiator/factory-leaf-spring-axle-wrap/) covers the anti-wrap options for the JT's rear.\n\nCaster is the other quiet failure. Long lower arms change caster, and a JT that leaves the shop with too little caster wanders and returns to center poorly. Specify your arm lengths to the alignment tech and confirm caster lands in spec \u2014 this is not optional on a lifted JT, it's what makes the truck livable on the highway.","steps":["Document all factory suspension geometry with measurements before disassembly: caster angle, pinion angle, front axle centering.","Raise the vehicle and support at the frame on jack stands; remove all four wheels.","Remove factory control arms (upper and lower), front and rear track bars, and front coil springs.","Install the new crossmembers or frame brackets per kit instructions \u2014 this is where most installs require drilling or welding depending on kit design.","Install new long lower control arms, then upper arms, adjusting length per kit specifications for your target lift height.","Install new front coil springs (supplied with kit), reconnect shocks.","Address the rear: install new leaf springs or replacement rear suspension per kit instructions; verify rear pinion angle.","Check driveshaft U-joint angles with a digital angle gauge \u2014 if front or rear exceeds 3\u00b0 static operating angle, budget for a driveshaft modification or replacement.","Reinstall wheels, torque all suspension fasteners to spec with wheels loaded (not hanging).","Schedule a 4-wheel alignment with a shop experienced in lifted Jeeps; specify arm lengths to the aligner."],"pros":["Maximum wheel travel and articulation for the JT platform","37\" tire fitment with no rubbing and proper geometry","Long-arm geometry dramatically reduces tire wear and improves on-road handling vs. stock-arm lifts at the same height","Foundation for a long-term build \u2014 you won't need to replace this suspension system"],"cons":["Highest cost and difficulty of any suspension option on the JT","Driveshaft modifications are likely at 4\" \u2014 budget an additional $400\u2013$800 per shaft","Requires a shop with JT long-arm alignment experience to set up correctly","JT-specific fitment must be verified \u2014 JL long-arm kits will not transfer directly","Installation is a full-weekend minimum; complex installs run two weekends"]},{"id":"factory-leaf-spring-axle-wrap","title":"Rear leaf spring axle wrap and the trailer-tow tradeoff","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"suspension","subcategory":"rear suspension","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2.0,"high":5.0},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":600},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","breaker bar","3/8\" drive socket set","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["suspension work","jack stand safety","basic fabrication awareness"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-present"],"tags":["leaf spring","axle wrap","traction bar","anti-wrap","suspension","rear axle","hard acceleration"],"parts":[{"name":"Traction bar / anti-wrap bar (single or dual)","vendor":"Rancho / Rough Country / Synergy","price_estimate_usd":180},{"name":"Add-a-leaf pack (if using leaf re-arch approach)","vendor":"Deaver / Skyjacker / Old Man Emu","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Rear shock upgrade (matched to ride height)","vendor":"Bilstein / Fox / King","price_estimate_usd":250}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator JT suspension engineering overview","url":"https://www.jeep.com/gladiator"}],"alternatives":["jt-shock-upgrade","jt-mid-lift"],"summary":"The Gladiator's leaf-spring rear is a truck feature, not a flaw \u2014 but it does create axle wrap under hard acceleration. Traction bars are the standard fix. If you're on a budget and not driving aggressively, re-arching the factory leaves first may be enough. If you're running 37s and an aggressive throttle, add a traction bar.","description":"Axle wrap happens when the leaf springs temporarily distort under the torque load of acceleration. In a leaf-spring setup, the spring pack is the only thing locating the axle fore-and-aft. When the drivetrain applies torque through the axle \u2014 especially under hard acceleration or when crawling in low range with high traction \u2014 the springs attempt to \"wind up.\" The axle moves forward at the pinion, the leaf pack flexes in an S-curve, and then snaps back.\n\nThe result ranges from a mild \"shudder\" felt at low speed under hard acceleration to a violent bucking on high-traction surfaces with larger tires. With the factory tire and power combination, most Gladiator owners don't push into axle wrap territory. Once you're running 35s or 37s and have any throttle aggression on trail, it becomes a real issue.\n\nSymptoms of axle wrap:\n- Shuddering or bucking sensation during hard acceleration from a stop\n- A \"clunk\" felt through the driveline when stabbing the throttle\n- Tire hop on high-traction surfaces (pavement, packed dirt) when accelerating hard\n- Visible spring pack distortion in photos or video \u2014 the S-curve shape is diagnostic\n\nAxle wrap is more pronounced with:\n- Larger, heavier tires (increased rotating mass)\n- Soft or fatigued spring packs (less resistance to wrap)\n- High-traction surfaces (asphalt, not sand or mud)\n- Aggressive throttle inputs\n\nA traction bar (also called an anti-wrap bar) adds a separate rigid link between the axle housing and the frame. It handles the fore-aft axle location that the spring pack was struggling with under torque. The leaf springs handle vertical load and some cornering; the traction bar handles acceleration torque.\n\nTraction bars for the JT typically mount to the frame near the factory spring perch and to the axle housing near the pinion side. Installation is a 2\u20134 hour job with a jack and basic tools. Most use existing mounting points; some require drilling or welding for optimal positioning.\n\n**Single vs. dual:** A single traction bar handles most trail and street applications. Dual traction bars (parallel setup) are for high-power builds or competition trucks that see aggressive axle loading regularly.\n\nBefore buying traction hardware, assess your spring pack. Factory leaf springs sag over time, losing arch and softening their resistance to wrap. A fatigued spring pack wraps more than a fresh one.\n\nNew-archleaf springs from a quality manufacturer (Deaver makes well-regarded Gladiator packs) restore the factory geometry and can improve wrap resistance without adding separate traction hardware. This is the budget-first approach for mild trail use without big tires. It won't match a traction bar on a lifted truck with 37s, but it may eliminate the symptom on a stock-height truck with 33s.\n\nThe JT runs leaf springs partly because Stellantis engineered it to tow. Leaf springs carry load better than a coil rear for most towing scenarios \u2014 they resist squat under tongue weight and maintain geometry better under sustained load.\n\nThe traction bar doesn't compromise towing capability. The two systems coexist without conflict. You can run a traction bar and still tow at the rated capacity.\n\nThe right spend depends on how far into wrap territory your truck actually is. Don't buy a competition dual-bar setup for a stock-height truck that shudders once a month.\n\n| Path | Parts cost | Best for | Honest tradeoff |\n|------|-----------|----------|-----------------|\n| Re-arch / add-a-leaf (Deaver, Old Man Emu) | $180\u2013260 | Stock-to-mild-lift trucks on 33s with occasional shudder | Restores geometry and stiffens the pack, but doesn't add a dedicated torque link \u2014 heavy throttle on 37s will still wrap |\n| Single traction bar (Rancho, Rough Country, Synergy) | $180\u2013320 | The default fix for lifted trucks on 35s\u201337s | Bolt-on for most; a few kits want a weld-on axle bracket for correct pinion angle |\n| Dual traction bars | $450\u2013650 | High-power builds, competition, aggressive rock crawling | Overkill for street/trail; can over-constrain articulation if geometry is wrong |\n| Shock upgrade paired with any of the above | +$250\u2013500/pair | Any truck already doing suspension work | Doesn't fix wrap on its own, but a matched shock controls the rebound the bar now redirects \u2014 see [/db/gladiator/jt-shock-upgrade/](/db/gladiator/jt-shock-upgrade/) |\n\nFor most owners running a mid-lift on 35s, a single traction bar plus decent shocks is the sweet spot. If you're still at stock height, try the re-arch first \u2014 it's cheaper and may end the symptom without adding hardware.\n\nThe detail that separates a traction bar that works from one that binds is where you set the axle when you tighten everything.\n\n- **Torque at ride height, not on the jack.** Suspension bushings must be loaded in their neutral position when the bolts go tight. Torque a traction bar's frame and axle fasteners with the full weight of the truck on the tires \u2014 bolting it up while the axle hangs on the jack preloads the bushings and causes bind and premature wear.\n- **Set pinion angle first.** On a lifted JT the pinion rotates down as the axle drops, changing driveline vibration. A good adjustable traction bar lets you set pinion angle; confirm the rear pinion points slightly up toward the transfer case output under load. If you're adding lift at the same time, do the lift geometry \u2014 including any needed [/db/gladiator/jt-mid-lift/](/db/gladiator/jt-mid-lift/) shims \u2014 before you finalize the bar length.\n- **Check clearance at full flex.** Cycle the suspension (ramp or jack one rear corner) and verify the bar and its brackets clear the frame, exhaust, and spare-tire drop at full droop and full stuff. A bar that contacts at articulation limits either dents something or transmits a hard stop into the mount.\n- **Re-torque after 100 miles.** New brackets and bushings settle. Re-check every fastener after the first shakedown drive, then again after the first hard trail day.","steps":["Diagnose whether you actually have axle wrap (not driveline clunk from worn U-joints or carrier bearing)","Inspect the spring pack for sag and fatigue \u2014 compare arch to factory spec","If spring pack is fatigued, consider re-arching first before adding traction hardware","If adding a traction bar: lift the vehicle on jack stands, measure the existing axle-to-frame relationship, select a traction bar with compatible mounting","Install per manufacturer instructions; torque all fasteners with vehicle at ride height (not lifted)","Test after installation: drive a high-traction surface under hard acceleration and verify the symptom is resolved"],"pros":["Traction bars are effective and affordable","They don't change ride quality significantly on street use","Compatible with towing use","Most are bolt-on installations"],"cons":["On a stock truck with stock tires, axle wrap may not be severe enough to justify the expense","Poor-quality traction bars can bind at full flex \u2014 verify the product is designed for JT geometry","Re-arching doesn't provide the same protection as a traction bar on lifted trucks with larger tires"]},{"id":"gladiator-vs-jl-mods-compatibility","title":"JT vs JL mod compatibility: what carries over, what doesn't","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"suspension","subcategory":"platform comparison","type":"reference","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.0},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":0},"tools_required":["tape measure (wheelbase / spring length)","Mopar part-number cross-reference"],"skills_required":["research"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-present"],"tags":["JL compatibility","lift kit","suspension","mod compatibility","leaf spring","coil spring","platform"],"parts":[],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator JT Engineering \u2014 Stellantis","url":"https://www.media.stellantis.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-budget-lift","factory-leaf-spring-axle-wrap"],"summary":"The Gladiator shares its front end with the JL Wrangler \u2014 but the rear is a completely different platform. The JT is longer, leaf-sprung in the rear, and requires JT-specific lift kits for any rear suspension work. Buying a JL kit for your Gladiator will cost you money and leave you with parts that don't fit.","description":"Everything ahead of the firewall \u2014 the front suspension geometry, the Dana 44 front axle, the front coil springs, the front track bar, the front control arms \u2014 is shared between the JL Wrangler and the JT Gladiator. That's important for two reasons:\n\n**Good news:** JL front suspension parts cross-reference to the JT. JL lift coils, JL front track bars, JL upper and lower front control arms all fit the JT. The aftermarket JL component selection is huge, and most of it works on the Gladiator front end. If you're doing front-only suspension work, you have a massive parts pool.\n\n**Bad news:** Some \"JL lift kits\" sold as complete kits include rear components that don't fit the Gladiator. Verify that any \"complete\" kit is specifically listed as JT-compatible, not JL-compatible. The manufacturer needs to have explicitly called out the JT.\n\nThe JL Wrangler runs coil springs in the rear. The Gladiator runs leaf springs. This is not a minor difference \u2014 it's a different suspension architecture with different geometry, different lift mechanics, and different failure modes.\n\n**What this means practically:**\n- JL rear lift coils do not fit the JT. The mounting architecture doesn't exist.\n- JL rear track bars, JL rear control arms, JL rear shocks (for a lifted JL) are calibrated for a different rear geometry. Some may physically fit in a modified application; none are engineered for it.\n- JT-specific rear lift kits use replacement leaf spring packs, add-a-leaf packs, or block-and-shackle combinations to achieve lift height.\n\nThe Gladiator runs a longer wheelbase than the JL (137 inches vs. 118 inches in the JL two-door). The driveshafts are longer, the rear proportions are different, and the leaf spring rear means different handling characteristics under load.\n\n| Component | JL \u2192 JT? |\n|---|---|\n| Front coil springs | Yes \u2014 same spec |\n| Front track bar | Yes \u2014 same geometry |\n| Front upper control arms | Yes |\n| Front lower control arms | Yes |\n| Front shocks | Verify by application \u2014 many cross |\n| Front bumpers, skid plates | Mostly yes \u2014 verify each part |\n| Rear coil springs | No \u2014 JT is leaf-sprung |\n| Rear track bar | No \u2014 JT uses different architecture |\n| Rear shocks | Some cross; verify JT application specifically |\n| Sway bar links | Verify \u2014 front may cross, rear different |\n| Rear leaf springs | JT-only \u2014 no JL equivalent |\n\nBuy kits explicitly listed as Gladiator JT or JT/JL. Kits listed as \"fits JK/JL/JT\" have been verified by the manufacturer for all three applications and are generally safe. Kits listed as \"fits JL/JL\" where the JT is mentioned as an afterthought in a footnote deserve more scrutiny.\n\nCompanies like Teraflex, Metalcloak, Skyjacker, Rough Country, and AEV all make JT-specific kits. AEV in particular has deep Gladiator content \u2014 they were involved with the JT platform early.\n\nThe driveshaft angle consideration: the JT's longer wheelbase means driveshaft angles change differently at a given lift height compared to the JL. Kits designed for the JT account for this. JL kits don't.\n\nA few concrete cases show where the front/rear split catches owners:\n\n**Front bumpers and winch mounts:** JL front bumpers bolt to the same frame horns as the JT. A JL Rubicon steel stubby bumper, a JL winch mount, and JL-application fog light brackets all transfer. If you're planning a [front bumper and winch](/db/gladiator/jt-front-bumper/) build, shop the entire JL catalog.\n\n**Lift coils vs. leaf packs:** A JL owner running 2.5 inches of lift uses taller front coils and taller rear coils. The JT owner uses the same front coils but needs a JT rear leaf solution \u2014 an add-a-leaf, a full replacement pack, or a shackle-and-block combination. See the [JT budget lift](/db/gladiator/jt-budget-lift/) and [mid-travel lift](/db/gladiator/jt-mid-lift/) guides for what actually bolts on out back.\n\n**Shocks:** Front shocks frequently cross by length and mount style. Rear shocks do not \u2014 the JT's leaf-sprung rear runs a different shock length and lower mount than the JL's coil rear. Always match rear shocks to the JT application and your specific lift height.\n\n**Axle wrap under power:** The leaf rear introduces a failure mode the JL never has. A JL rear cannot wrap because coils and control arms locate the axle; the JT's leaf pack can. See [factory leaf spring axle wrap](/db/gladiator/factory-leaf-spring-axle-wrap/) for why this matters once you add power or a locker.\n\n- **\"Fits JL/JT\" listings with one photo:** If the product page shows a coil-sprung rear in the fitment image, the rear hardware is JL-only. Confirm the JT rear parts are pictured and listed separately.\n- **Wheelbase-sensitive parts:** Long control arms, adjustable track bars, and driveshafts are cut to length. A JL-length part will bind or run out of adjustment on the 137-inch JT wheelbase.\n- **Brake line and bump stop extensions:** A JT lift needs rear-specific brake line drop brackets and bump stop spacers sized for leaf travel, not the JL coil equivalents.\n- **Return-shipping cost:** The most common way this error costs money is a \"close enough\" JL rear part that has to be shipped back. Verify before you buy, not after.","steps":["Identify whether the modification is front-only, rear-only, or a full suspension system","For front-only work, search for JL parts and verify JT cross-compatibility with the manufacturer","For rear work, search exclusively for JT-specific parts \u2014 do not assume JL rear parts cross over","For complete lift kits, confirm the kit explicitly covers the JT application (not listed as \"compatible\")","Call the manufacturer's tech line if you're unsure \u2014 they know their fitment better than any catalog"],"pros":["The JL front end community is huge; JT owners benefit from that parts pool","JT-specific kits are well-developed from established manufacturers","The leaf-spring rear is actually advantageous for heavy towing and overland use"],"cons":["Incomplete kits sold as \"JL/JT compatible\" sometimes include JL-specific rear components that won't fit","The JT aftermarket is smaller than the JL \u2014 some parts require longer lead times","Driveshaft angles after lift need JT-specific consideration, not JL-borrowed specs"]},{"id":"jt-shock-upgrade","title":"Shock Absorber Upgrade \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"suspension","subcategory":"shocks","type":"kit","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":349,"high":1800},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set (metric)","torque wrench","spring compressor (if replacing coils simultaneously)","pry bar"],"skills_required":["suspension work","torque-to-spec discipline"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["shocks","suspension","lift","performance","shock-absorber"],"parts":[{"name":"Fox 2.0 Performance Series Shocks \u2014 JT Gladiator (set of 4)","vendor":"Fox Racing Shox","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"Bilstein 5100 Series Shocks \u2014 JT Gladiator Front (pair)","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":299},{"name":"Rancho RS5000X Shocks \u2014 JT (set of 4)","vendor":"Rancho","price_estimate_usd":349},{"name":"King OEM Performance Shocks \u2014 JT Gladiator (set of 4)","vendor":"King Shocks","price_estimate_usd":1200},{"name":"Teraflex Falcon SP2 3.3 Shocks \u2014 JT (set of 4)","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":1799}],"citations":[{"title":"Fox 2.0 Performance Gladiator JT Shock Application","url":"https://www.ridefox.com"},{"title":"Bilstein 5100 JT Gladiator Fitment Guide","url":"https://www.bilstein.com"},{"title":"Teraflex JT Falcon Shock Selection Guide","url":"https://www.teraflex.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-budget-lift","jt-mid-lift"],"summary":"Shock absorbers control how fast the suspension moves \u2014 not how high it sits. New shocks don't add lift, but they determine how well the JT handles whatever lift height you're running. Stock shocks blown out or running 2\"+ of lift means the suspension is working against itself.","description":"The JT Gladiator's factory shocks are adequate for moderate use on a stock-height truck. Once you add lift, increase tire weight, or start doing repeated trail articulation, the factory shocks are undersized for the job. Aftermarket shocks with more fluid volume and better damping rates handle the added stroke from lifted suspension without blowing through their stroke or overheating.\n\n**Front vs. rear shock requirements**\n\nThe JT's front suspension is coil-sprung (same architecture as JL Wrangler). Front shocks are monotube or twin-tube units that mount between the lower control arm and the frame. The rear uses leaf springs with shock absorbers in a separate mounting location. Front and rear shocks have different travel and stroke requirements \u2014 confirm fitment for your specific lift height when ordering.\n\n**Shock categories: what each tier delivers**\n\n**Budget to mid-tier (Rancho RS5000X, ~$349 set):** A meaningful upgrade over factory at the entry price point. The RS5000X handles mild trail use and 2\" lifts well. Not tunable, not rebuildable \u2014 when they wear, you replace them. Correct for owners who want improvement without committing to a premium shock budget.\n\n**Bilstein 5100 (~$150 per shock, ~$600 set):** The industry standard for quality monotube shocks at a non-premium price. The 5100 is a monotube design with a higher oil capacity than most twin-tube budget shocks \u2014 this translates to consistent damping when the shocks are working hard. The Bilstein 5100 series has an adjustable ride height on the front for some applications. Excellent for lifted JTs running 33\"\u201335\" tires on trail use.\n\n**Fox 2.0 Performance (~$699 set):** Significant step up in build quality, internal volume, and fade resistance. The 2.0 body diameter means more oil capacity and better heat management for extended off-road use. Fox shocks can be rebuilt and revalved \u2014 a cost that pays off over time for serious trail users. The right choice for a JT that's frequently on technical terrain.\n\n**King OEM Performance / Teraflex Falcon SP2 3.3 (~$1,200\u2013$1,800 set):** Remote reservoir designs that separate the oil and nitrogen charge into a separate canister, dramatically increasing heat capacity. These shocks perform where standard shocks fade \u2014 sustained off-road driving at speed, deep rutted terrain, repeated articulation events. Overkill for casual trail use; appropriate for a JT built for serious off-road or overland travel.\n\n1. Jack the vehicle and support on stands at the frame. Place a floor jack under the lower control arm to support it at normal ride height.\n2. Loosen and remove the upper shock mount bolt (at the frame) and lower shock mount bolt (at the lower control arm). On the JT, the lower mount is typically a through-bolt \u2014 remove the nut and slide the bolt out.\n3. Remove the shock. Note the orientation and length \u2014 new shocks should be close to the same length.\n4. Install the new shock, threading upper mount first (hand-tight), then lower.\n5. With the suspension at normal ride height (jack supporting the control arm), torque both mounts to spec: upper mount 40 ft-lbs, lower mount 60 ft-lbs. Never torque suspension components with the axle drooping \u2014 this pre-loads the bushings incorrectly and shortens their life.\n\n1. Support the rear axle on a floor jack.\n2. Remove the upper shock mount bolt (at the frame rail) and lower shock mount bolt (at the axle bracket).\n3. The rear leaf spring JT setup means the shock may be partially constrained by the spring \u2014 work carefully to avoid straining the spring hardware.\n4. Install new shock, upper mount first. Torque to spec: upper 40 ft-lbs, lower 40 ft-lbs with the axle at ride height.\n\n- **Shock length vs. lift height**: A 2\" lift that adds coil height requires a longer-travel shock. Verify that the shock you're ordering is rated for your lift height \u2014 installing a stock-length shock on a 4\" lift causes the shock to bind or bottom out.\n- **Alignment after install**: Replacing shocks alone doesn't require alignment. But if you're combining shock replacement with a spring change or lift, get an alignment before driving more than 50 miles.\n- **Top hat condition**: On the JT front, the top hat (upper spring perch) can corrode and bind. Inspect before reinstalling \u2014 a corroded top hat causes the coil spring to load incorrectly and can damage the new shock.\n\nRancho RS5000X set of 4: ~$349. The starting point for shock upgrades on the JT \u2014 better than stock, affordable.\n\nBilstein 5100 set of 4 (front + rear): ~$560\u2013600. The honest value answer for most JT owners running 33\"\u201335\" tires and moderate trail use.\n\nFox 2.0 Performance set of 4: ~$699. Correct for frequent trail use, 35\"+ tires, and owners who want durability over several seasons of hard use.\n\nKing OEM Performance or Teraflex Falcon SP2 3.3: ~$1,200\u20131,800. Premium reservation for dedicated trail machines and overland builds with serious off-road ambitions.\n\nShop labor for four-corner shock replacement: ~$250\u2013400. Straightforward labor that most intermediate wrenchers can handle \u2014 the savings are real and the job is repeatable."},{"id":"jt-long-arm-suspension-upgrade","title":"Long-Arm Suspension Upgrade \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"suspension","type":"upgrade","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":8,"high":16},"cost_usd":{"low":2200,"high":5500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands (heavy-duty)","impact wrench","torque wrench","breaker bar","BFH (big hammer)","thread chaser","spring compressor if needed","tape measure","alignment gear or shop alignment afterward"],"skills_required":["advanced wrenching","suspension geometry understanding","alignment basics"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["suspension","lift","long-arm","control-arms","geometry","articulation"],"parts":[{"name":"Teraflex 4.5\" ST3 Long-Arm Lift Kit for JT","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":3299},{"name":"Synergy MFG 4-5\" JT Long-Arm System with Bracket","vendor":"Synergy MFG","price_estimate_usd":4499},{"name":"Rubicon Express 4.5\" Long-Arm Upgrade Kit for JT","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":2399},{"name":"JKS Manufacturing J-Ventures 3.5\" Long-Arm Kit","vendor":"JKS","price_estimate_usd":2799}],"citations":[{"title":"Teraflex JT ST3 Long-Arm Kit Installation Manual","url":"https://www.teraflex.com"},{"title":"Synergy MFG JT Long-Arm System Build Notes","url":"https://www.synergymfg.com"}],"alternatives":["factory-leaf-spring-axle-wrap","gladiator-vs-jl-mods-compatibility"],"summary":"A long-arm suspension upgrade moves the front and rear control arm pivot points rearward via frame brackets \u2014 longer arms flatten the arc of wheel travel, which adds articulation and dramatically improves high-speed off-road handling compared to short-arm geometry.","description":"The JT Gladiator's factory suspension is a 5-link coil setup \u2014 solid Dana 44 front and rear, with coil springs and three-link geometry in the rear and a four-link in the front. It's a capable platform from the factory, and the Rubicon ships with 33-inch tires and factory lockers. But the factory geometry is optimized for stock ride height. Once you add 3\u20134+ inches of lift, the control arm angles get steep, pinion angles change, and caster correction becomes a project.\n\nLong-arm kits solve this by replacing the short OEM control arms with arms 4\u20136 inches longer, relocated to new frame brackets welded or bolted lower and farther back on the frame. The longer arm means a flatter arc of wheel travel \u2014 more suspension travel before you bind, better articulation at speed, and better caster geometry at lift heights where short-arms can't keep up.\n\n**What you're giving up:** Cost and complexity. A long-arm kit is a major installation \u2014 budget a full day minimum, likely two if you're working alone. Every kit requires frame modification (new brackets \u2014 some bolt-on, some require welding) and a full alignment afterward. This is not a first-suspension-project upgrade.\n\n**Short-arm vs. long-arm:** If you're building a daily driver with 3 inches of lift and occasional light trails, short-arm is correct \u2014 less cost, more manageable to install, adequate for the use case. Long-arm pays off when you're running 35s or bigger, 4+ inches of lift, and using the JT for technical rock crawling or high-speed desert running where control arm geometry matters.\n\n1. **Preparation**: Strip the front and rear suspension completely \u2014 shocks, track bars, control arms, sway bar links. Working in sequence (front complete before starting rear, or rear first) prevents confusion. Label or photograph every connection before disassembly.\n2. **Frame bracket installation**: Most kits use bolt-on brackets that clamp to existing frame rails. Some kits (Synergy, high-end) use weld-on brackets for maximum strength. Bolt-on brackets are DIY-friendly; weld-on requires a MIG welder and someone who knows how to use one. Follow the kit's torque sequence for bracket bolts \u2014 these carry the entire suspension load.\n3. **Control arm installation**: Thread in the new long arms, leaving hardware loose until the system is at ride height. Setting control arm length at full droop will cause bind at ride height.\n4. **Coil spring installation**: Most long-arm kits include matched coils. Install per kit instructions \u2014 coil spring perches and orientation matter.\n5. **Shock installation**: Long-arm kits include shocks matched to the travel range. Install with vehicle at ride height.\n6. **Set at ride height**: With the vehicle on the ground at ride height (tires inflated, driver weight, half tank), set all adjustable control arm lengths to factory spec starting points per the kit's guide.\n7. **Track bar setup**: Adjust the front and rear track bars to center the axles under the vehicle. Most kits include adjustable track bars \u2014 you'll need a measuring tape and a helper to check axle centering.\n8. **Pinion angle**: Check front and rear pinion angles with a digital angle finder. Target is approximately 0\u20132 degrees negative pinion angle (pointing slightly down) under loaded conditions. Adjust lower control arm angle to set pinion angle.\n9. **Alignment**: Drive to an alignment shop with a known-quality four-wheel alignment machine and a tech familiar with lifted Jeeps. Caster, camber, and toe all need setting after a long-arm install. This is not optional.\n10. **Break-in torque check**: After 500 miles, re-torque all suspension hardware at ride height. Control arm bolts that weren't torqued at ride height will have loosened as the rubber bushings compress to their final position.\n\n- **Driveshaft length**: Long-arm kits change the pinion angle and axle position enough that both front and rear driveshafts may need replacement or modification. Confirm with the kit manufacturer before ordering \u2014 many include extended driveshafts or call out required shaft upgrades.\n- **Bump stop extension**: With longer travel, the factory bump stops no longer contact at the right point. Most kits include bump stop extensions \u2014 install them to prevent the coil from going coil-bound at full compression.\n- **Geometry bind at extremes**: Even well-designed long-arm systems can bind at extreme articulation if control arm length isn't set correctly. Check for bind before heading to the trail by cycling the suspension through full travel on jack stands.\n- **Warranty implications**: Any frame modification (bracket install) affects the factory warranty on frame-related components. This matters more on a new JT than a used one \u2014 know where your warranty stands before drilling or welding.\n\nThe bracket is where a long-arm kit lives or dies, and the choice between bolt-on and weld-on is the real decision, not the brand on the box. Bolt-on brackets clamp to the factory frame rails with grade-8 hardware and let an owner with hand tools do the install in a driveway - the tradeoff is that clamping load, not a metallurgical bond, is holding the entire suspension. Those bolts must be checked at the first 500-mile service and periodically after, because a bracket that walks loose changes your control-arm geometry and your caster on the highway. Weld-on brackets (Synergy and other high-end systems) bond to the frame and never loosen, but they require a competent MIG welder, they permanently alter the frame, and they end any question of returning the truck to stock.\n\nThe reversibility question matters more than most buyers expect. Drilling or welding frame brackets affects the factory warranty on frame-related components, and on a late-model JT still under powertrain or frame coverage that is a real cost, not a footnote. A used truck out of warranty has nothing to lose; a two-year-old Rubicon does. Decide where your truck sits before the drill comes out - this is a one-way door on a weld-on kit.\n\nThe sticker price of a long-arm kit is rarely the price of the project. An owner who only needs 3-3.5 inches for 35s is often better served staying on short arms with a [mid-height lift](/db/gladiator/jt-mid-lift/) and skipping this entirely. Go long-arm and taller, though, and relocating the control-arm pivots changes the angle the driveshafts work at - the factory shafts on a JT can exceed their safe operating angle once the geometry moves, so budget for a heavy-duty rear shaft or a slip-yoke correction on taller builds, $200-600 depending on what the kit calls out. Set the pinion angle wrong and you trade death wobble for driveline vibration, a buzz through the floor at highway speed that no alignment fixes.\n\nTwo related services get more important, not less, after a long-arm install. Regearing to recover the gearing you lost under 37s keeps the drivetrain in its powerband and reduces the shock load the taller, heavier setup sends through the case - see [regearing the axles](/db/gladiator/jt-regear/). And the [transfer case fluid](/db/gladiator/jt-transfer-case-fluid/) service interval tightens, because a lifted, articulating rig runs the case hotter and works the clutch packs harder than the factory schedule assumed. Fold both into the long-arm budget rather than discovering them later.\n\nRubicon Express 4.5-inch long-arm: ~$2,400. The budget entry point in the long-arm space. Good geometry, less adjustability than premium kits.\n\nTeraflex ST3 4.5-inch: ~$3,300. The middle tier \u2014 well-engineered, solid parts, good instructions, strong community support.\n\nSynergy MFG long-arm system: ~$4,500+. Premium brackets, maximum adjustability, preferred by serious rock crawlers who want dialed geometry.\n\nAdd alignment ($100\u2013180) and driveshaft work ($200\u2013600 if needed) to any of these numbers. Long-arm suspension is a $2,500\u20136,000 project all-in, not counting shocks if not included. It earns that cost on a rig that will do serious trails regularly."},{"id":"jt-mid-lift","title":"Mid-Tier Lift for the Gladiator \u2014 2.5\"-3.5\" with Geometry Correction ($700\u2013$2,000)","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":10},"cost_usd":{"low":699,"high":2000},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","breaker bar","spring compressor","pry bar","alignment tool"],"skills_required":["suspension work","basic wrenching","torque spec adherence"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["lift","suspension","geometry","leaf-spring"],"parts":[{"name":"EVO Mfg Enforcer 2.5\" JT Lift Kit","vendor":"EVO Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":1299},{"name":"JKS Manufacturing 2.5\" JT Lift Kit","vendor":"JKS Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":899},{"name":"Teraflex 2.5\" Performance Spacer Lift JT","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"Mopar 2\" Lift Kit for Gladiator JT","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":995}],"citations":[{"title":"EVO Mfg JT Enforcer Install Guide","url":"https://evomfg.com/gladiator-jt-suspension"},{"title":"JKS Gladiator JT Lift \u2014 Product Page","url":"https://jksmfg.com/jeep-gladiator"}],"alternatives":["jt-budget-lift","jt-expedition-lift"],"summary":"The 2.5\"\u20133.5\" range is where the Gladiator JT's suspension setup pays off \u2014 enough lift for 35s on any trim, enough height to address departure angle on the truck bed, and the geometry correction that separates a good lift from a frustrating one.","description":"At this price tier you're buying replacement coils up front, not spacers, plus control arm corrections and track bar adjustments that keep the front axle centered and the handling predictable on-road. The Teraflex 2.5\" Performance kit ($699) leads the value conversation \u2014 it's replacement coils with alignment cam bolts and an adjustable front track bar, which addresses most of the geometry shortcomings of a 2.5\" lift without going to full adjustable control arms. The JKS 2.5\" kit ($899) adds better shocks and a slightly more complete geometry package. EVO Mfg's Enforcer at $1,299 is the proper build: front and rear addressed together, quality control arms, and EVO's reputation for JL/JT fitment accuracy.\n\nThe Mopar 2\" kit ($995) deserves a mention specifically for owners who want to preserve their factory powertrain warranty. It's dealer-installed, FIAT/Chrysler-approved, and Mopar will stand behind it if anything goes sideways with warranty claims. It won't give you the wheel travel or geometry correction of the aftermarket kits, but the warranty peace-of-mind is real for newer trucks still under coverage.\n\nThe JT's rear leaf spring suspension is the ongoing compatibility headache with this platform. Every JL-based lift kit you find on the shelf was designed around the JL's rear coil springs. Some manufacturers sell JT-specific versions of their JL kits; others retrofit the front components and include JT-specific rear leaf spring packs or AAL kits separately. Before you order anything, confirm the listing says JT \u2014 not JL \u2014 and verify it includes a rear component. Running a leveled front with stock rear leaves on a truck that hauls gear will look wrong and handle worse than stock.\n\nThe JT's rear leaf pack is the detail that trips up most first-time lift buyers, so it's worth understanding the three approaches before you order. **Add-a-leaf (AAL):** a supplemental leaf inserted into the factory pack. It's the cheapest way to gain 1\"-1.5\" of rear height and stiffen the pack for load, but it also stiffens the ride and can accelerate axle wrap under throttle. **Replacement leaf packs:** full aftermarket springs (EVO, Deaver, Alcan) that give the correct lift height with a spring rate tuned for it \u2014 the right call for a truck that hauls gear or tows, and the smoothest-riding option. **Spacer/block on the rear:** a block between the axle and the factory pack. It gains height cheaply but increases the leverage that causes axle wrap, and is the least desirable structurally on a leaf-sprung rear.\n\nWhichever you choose, a JT that carries weight in the bed or tows benefits from addressing [axle wrap](/db/gladiator/factory-leaf-spring-axle-wrap/) at the same time \u2014 the stock geometry lets the axle rotate under load, and lift makes it worse.\n\nTorque the leaf spring U-bolts and control arm bolts with the suspension at ride height, not hanging. Bushings set at full droop bind and wear prematurely once the truck settles. Lower the JT onto its wheels, roll it a few feet to settle the suspension, then do the final torque pass.\n\nDon't skip the alignment, and specifically ask the shop to set caster \u2014 a 2.5\"-3.5\" lift pulls caster out of spec, and low caster on a solid-axle truck feels vague and wandering at highway speed. If the shop can't bring caster back with the cam bolts in your kit, you need adjustable front control arms. Stepping down to a [budget spacer lift](/db/gladiator/jt-budget-lift/) avoids the geometry question only because it stays under 2\".\n\nThe Gladiator's 137.3\" wheelbase is nearly 19\" longer than a four-door JL's, and the long rear driveshaft is the quiet benefit: the same 2.5\" of lift produces a shallower change in driveshaft operating angle, which is why JTs at this height rarely develop the driveline vibration that plagues short-wheelbase Jeeps. That forgiveness has a limit. At 3.5\", especially with a rear block or AAL rather than a replacement pack, check rear pinion angle after the install. The symptom of an angle problem is a drone or vibration at 65\u201375 mph under light throttle that changes when you lift off the accelerator. The fix on a leaf-sprung rear is a tapered degree shim between the axle perch and the spring pack \u2014 a $30 part \u2014 or leaf packs with the correction built in, which is one more argument for replacement packs over stacked hardware.\n\nMid-tier kits differ less in their coils than in what else is in the box, and the omissions surface at full droop or full compression. Before ordering, confirm the kit includes (or budget separately for): **bump stop extensions** \u2014 at 2.5\"+ on 35s, the factory bump stops allow the tire into the fender liner at full compression; **front brake line and ABS wire slack** \u2014 cycle the axle to full droop on the jack and verify nothing pulls taut, since extended lines are cheap insurance at 3\"+; **sway bar end links** \u2014 longer links restore the bar to its designed operating angle, and a kit without them leaves the bar preloaded at ride height; and **shock length** \u2014 a 2.5\" lift on factory-length shocks limits droop to less than stock travel, which wastes the lift off-road. The shock question is the most common follow-on spend; the [shock upgrade guide](/db/gladiator/jt-shock-upgrade/) covers matching extended length to lift height so the second purchase is the last one.","steps":["Break lug nuts loose on all four corners before lifting.","Raise the vehicle and support on jack stands at all four frame rails.","Remove all four wheels.","Front: disconnect sway bar end links, unbolt shock absorbers top and bottom, remove the front trackbar from the axle bracket.","Lower the front axle on the floor jack to decompress front coil springs. Remove and replace front coil springs per kit instructions.","Install adjustable front track bar and alignment cam bolts per kit instructions.","Reinstall front shocks (new shocks if included), reconnect sway bar end links, reinstall wheels.","Rear: support rear axle, unbolt rear shocks, loosen U-bolts on rear leaf springs.","Install rear leaf spring packs or AAL per kit instructions, reinstall U-bolts and torque to spec.","Reinstall rear shocks and wheels.","Torque all fasteners to spec \u2014 track bar bolts, control arm bolts, and U-bolts all have specific torque values; use the kit's torque sheet.","Schedule a 4-wheel alignment immediately after install."],"pros":["Clears 35\" tires on all JT trims with no rubbing","Geometry correction included \u2014 adjustable track bar and cam bolts prevent handling problems","Full kit approach addresses both front coil and rear leaf spring in one purchase","Mopar option preserves factory warranty coverage"],"cons":["Rear leaf spring compatibility requires careful verification before ordering \u2014 JL kits will not work","Higher cost and complexity vs. budget spacer kits","Professional alignment required after install ($80\u2013$150)","Aftermarket shocks in mid-tier kits are adequate but not exceptional \u2014 shock upgrade is a common follow-on purchase"]},{"id":"mojave-fox-shock-service","title":"Mojave-specific Fox 2.5 internal bypass shock service","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"suspension","subcategory":"shocks","type":"how_to","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4.0,"high":8.0},"cost_usd":{"low":200,"high":600},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","breaker bar","19mm socket","shock spring compressor (if coilover)","nitrogen fill equipment or shop access"],"skills_required":["suspension service","shock rebuild experience or shop delegation"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-present"],"tags":["mojave","fox","2.5 internal bypass","shock service","desert","suspension"],"parts":[{"name":"Fox 2.5 internal bypass shock rebuild service (per shock)","vendor":"Fox Racing Shox","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Fox shock oil (FLOAT fluid, for bypass shock use)","vendor":"Fox Racing Shox","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Nitrogen fill service (at a qualified shop)","vendor":"local suspension shop","price_estimate_usd":40}],"citations":[{"title":"Fox Racing Shox \u2014 service center locator","url":"https://www.ridefox.com/fox17/content.php?page=service-center"},{"title":"Jeep Gladiator Mojave product page","url":"https://www.jeep.com/gladiator"}],"alternatives":["jt-shock-upgrade","jt-expedition-lift"],"summary":"The Mojave's Fox 2.5-inch internal bypass shocks are the most sophisticated suspension hardware on any factory Jeep. They need professional service every 30,000\u201350,000 miles or after any serious abuse. Don't skip this \u2014 a neglected bypass shock loses its calibration well before it shows visible damage.","description":"Standard shocks control damping through a single piston with calibrated valving. The Fox 2.5 Internal Bypass shock runs additional bypass tubes alongside the main shock body. As the shock compresses through its travel, the bypass tubes progressively restrict flow \u2014 the shock becomes firmer as it approaches full compression. This gives the Mojave an extremely supple ride at small suspension inputs (highway, light trail) while providing firm resistance at the end of travel (preventing bottoming on high-speed terrain).\n\nThis calibration is engineered specifically for the Mojave. The bypass positions (where the bypass tubes open and close) are set for the Mojave's ride height, travel, and the specific desert-running use case. A standard Fox 2.5 reservoir shock \u2014 even from the same diameter family \u2014 is not the same product.\n\nFox's general recommendation for performance shocks in trail use: inspect every 10,000\u201315,000 miles, service every 30,000\u201350,000 miles. For a Mojave used in actual desert running \u2014 Sonoran desert, Baja-style terrain, high-speed washboard \u2014 shorten that to 20,000\u201330,000 miles.\n\nSigns your shocks need service before the mileage interval:\n- Reduced damping feel \u2014 the truck feels \"floaty\" over terrain it used to manage firmly\n- Oil residue or weeping around the shock body or reservoir\n- Unusual noise (clunking, cavitation) from the shock during compression\n- Loss of the distinct firmness-at-end-of-travel characteristic\n\nFox recommends their factory-certified service centers for internal bypass shocks. The bypass tube positioning requires specialized knowledge \u2014 it's not a generic shock rebuild. Incorrect reassembly changes the shock's calibration profile, which defeats the point of having bypass shocks.\n\nLocate a Fox-certified service center via their website before shipping or bringing the shocks in. Some high-end off-road shops have bypass rebuild experience and appropriate equipment. Ask specifically if they've serviced Fox internal bypass shocks, not Fox reservoir shocks \u2014 they're different processes.\n\n**What the service includes:** Disassembly, inspection of seals, piston, bypass tubes, and body; cleaning; replacement of seals and oil; nitrogen recharge to spec; recalibration of bypass positions; reassembly and testing.\n\nThe Mojave's shocks are calibrated for speed and compliance over large undulations \u2014 desert washes, high-speed dirt roads, Baja-style terrain. The valving and bypass positions favor this use case.\n\nThis calibration is not ideal for slow-speed rock crawling. At low speed, the bypass tubes are mostly open, which means the shock is relatively underdamped for technical rock terrain. The Mojave is genuinely better in the desert than on a technical rock trail \u2014 that's by design.\n\nIf you primarily run rocks and bought a Mojave, that's workable \u2014 it's not undriveable on rocks \u2014 but understand that the suspension is optimized for a different environment.\n\n**DIY:** Removing and reinstalling the shocks is within reach for someone comfortable with suspension work. It requires removing the wheels, unbolting the shock ends, and dealing with the coilover spring if applicable. The spring compressor is mandatory.\n\n**Send out:** The rebuild itself is shop work for most people. Sending the shocks to Fox or a certified rebuilder and reinstalling the rebuilt units is the practical approach for anyone who doesn't have bypass-specific rebuild experience.\n\nGetting the shocks off the truck is DIY-doable; the rebuild is the shop's job. Working one corner at a time:\n\n1. Loosen the wheel lug nuts, lift the corner, support the frame on a stand, and remove the wheel.\n2. Support the axle with a floor jack to take spring tension off the shock. On the coilover front, the spring is captive on the shock body \u2014 do not unbolt anything until the axle is supported.\n3. Remove the lower shock bolt (19mm), then the upper mount hardware. The reservoir is mounted remotely on a bracket \u2014 unclip the reservoir before pulling the shock free.\n4. Bag and label each shock by corner. Fox needs to know corner position and your truck's loaded weight to recalibrate correctly.\n5. Reinstall in reverse, and torque the lower bolt with the suspension at ride height, not hanging \u2014 torquing at full droop preloads the bushings wrong.\n\nIf you're comparing this to a full replacement instead of a rebuild, the [JT shock upgrade](/db/gladiator/jt-shock-upgrade/) and [expedition lift](/db/gladiator/jt-expedition-lift/) guides cover aftermarket options.\n\n| Path | What you get | Cost |\n|---|---|---|\n| Fox rebuild, you R&R | Factory calibration restored, 4 shocks | $480\u2013600 + your labor |\n| Shop R&R + rebuild | Turnkey, no tools needed | $900\u20131,300 |\n| Replace with aftermarket 2.5 | New long-travel or reservoir shocks | $1,600\u20133,500 |\n\nThe rebuild is the value play if you want to keep the Mojave's engineered desert calibration. Aftermarket replacement makes sense only if you are changing the truck's mission \u2014 for example, moving toward a [mid-travel lift](/db/gladiator/jt-mid-lift/) setup tuned for mixed use rather than pure desert running.","steps":["Inspect shocks at each oil change \u2014 look for weeping and check damping feel","At 30,000 miles or when performance drops, schedule service","Locate a Fox-certified service center or qualified bypass shock rebuilder","Remove shocks from the vehicle (this is DIY-doable; see your vehicle-specific removal procedure)","Ship or bring shocks to the service center with your weight/application info","Reinstall rebuilt shocks; torque to spec with truck at ride height"],"pros":["The Fox 2.5 internal bypass is genuinely superior suspension hardware for desert running","Proper service restores calibration \u2014 the shock performs like new after a rebuild","Fox's service center network is well-established"],"cons":["Service cost per shock is significant \u2014 plan $400\u2013$600 for a full set rebuild","Finding a qualified local rebuilder may require shipping the shocks out","The desert calibration is a real limitation for technical rock use"]},{"id":"jt-obd2-diagnostics","title":"OBD2 Diagnostics and Scan Tools \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"tools_diagnostic","type":"reference","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":350},"tools_required":["OBD2 scan tool or Bluetooth OBD2 adapter"],"skills_required":["smartphone operation or basic computer use"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["diagnostic","obd2","check-engine-light","tools"],"parts":[{"name":"Autel AutoLink AL319 OBD2 Scanner","vendor":"Autel","price_estimate_usd":33},{"name":"BlueDriver Bluetooth OBD2 Scanner","vendor":"BlueDriver","price_estimate_usd":99},{"name":"Autel MaxiCOM MK808 Bi-Directional Scanner","vendor":"Autel","price_estimate_usd":269},{"name":"OBDLink MX+ Bluetooth Adapter","vendor":"OBDLink","price_estimate_usd":109}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator JT OBD2 Port Location and Protocol","url":"https://www.stellantis.com"},{"title":"BlueDriver JT Gladiator Enhanced Diagnostics Support","url":"https://www.bluedriver.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-obd2-diagnostic","jt-alternator-battery-upgrade"],"summary":"The JT Gladiator's OBD2 port is under the driver's side dash, left of the steering column. Plug in a scanner and you can read fault codes, live sensor data, and run basic tests \u2014 without paying a shop $150 to do it.","description":"The OBD2 port is standard across all 2020\u20132024 Gladiator trims. What varies is how deep your scanner can go. Generic OBD2 readers (the $25\u201335 units at every auto parts store) read and clear POWERTRAIN codes: engine and transmission fault codes that fall under the universal OBD2 standard. They miss chassis, ABS, airbag, and TIPM codes \u2014 which are manufacturer-specific and require enhanced software to access.\n\nFor a JT used for trail work, enhanced access matters. A P0xxx code is visible to a basic reader; a C1234 (chassis/ABS) or U1xxx (network communication) code requires enhanced Chrysler/Stellantis protocol support. The BlueDriver ($99) covers enhanced codes for Chrysler-platform vehicles including the JT and handles most of what a trail owner needs. The OBDLink MX+ ($109) is faster and more reliable over Bluetooth for extended live data sessions. If you're doing serious wrenching \u2014 testing actuators, resetting TPMS, calibrating sensors after a suspension swap \u2014 the Autel MK808 ($269) is the right tool.\n\n**P0128 \u2014 Coolant Temperature Below Thermostat Regulating Temperature**: The thermostat is stuck open. The 3.6L JT runs a 203\u00b0F thermostat \u2014 if coolant temperature never climbs above 185\u00b0F, the thermostat is the culprit. Replace it.\n\n**P0171 / P0174 \u2014 System Too Lean (Banks 1 and 2)**: The engine is running lean. On the JT, this is commonly a vacuum leak, a degraded oxygen sensor, or a clogged fuel injector. Examine vacuum lines first \u2014 they're visible and cheap to replace. If fuel pressure tests normal, scan the O2 sensor live data.\n\n**P062F \u2014 Internal Control Module EEPROM Error**: Appears occasionally after disconnecting the battery or a TIPM glitch. Clear it. If it returns repeatedly, the TIPM (Totally Integrated Power Module) may be developing an issue \u2014 a known weak point on FCA platforms.\n\n**U0100 \u2014 Lost Communication with ECM/PCM**: Network communication fault. Usually a wiring issue or loose connector rather than a failed module. Check the ECM connectors first.\n\n**B1B03 / B1B04 \u2014 TPMS sensor codes**: Appear after tire rotations where the TPMS ID sequence hasn't been updated. Reset TPMS via the instrument cluster menu or with an enhanced scanner after rotation.\n\nWith a live data-capable scanner connected:\n- **Fuel trims (STFT, LTFT)**: Short-term and long-term fuel trims exceeding \u00b110% indicate a fueling or vacuum issue.\n- **O2 sensor waveform**: A cycling waveform (rich-lean-rich) indicates a healthy upstream O2 sensor. A flat line means a dead sensor.\n- **Coolant temp**: Should reach 200\u2013210\u00b0F and stabilize. Overheating begins at 230\u00b0F on the gauge.\n- **Battery voltage**: Should read 13.8\u201314.4V at idle with the alternator charging. Below 13.2V suggests an alternator or connection issue.\n\nClearing a code does not fix the underlying problem \u2014 it resets the fault memory and turns off the check engine light. If the root cause is still present, the code returns within one to three drive cycles. Clearing codes before an emissions test also resets the readiness monitors \u2014 the vehicle must complete several drive cycles before the monitors set \"ready,\" which is required to pass testing in most states. Don't clear codes before an emissions inspection unless you have time to drive 100+ miles in varied conditions.\n\nBasic code reader (Autel AL319): ~$33. Reads and clears P-codes. Enough to diagnose most check engine lights on a stock JT. Not enough for suspension/ABS/airbag work.\n\nEnhanced Bluetooth adapter (BlueDriver or OBDLink MX+): $99\u2013109. Pairs with a phone app and covers manufacturer-specific codes. The right choice for a JT owner who does their own maintenance and trail repairs.\n\nBi-directional scanner (Autel MK808): ~$269. Adds the ability to command actuators, reset learned parameters, and perform module-specific tests. Worth it if you're doing suspension swaps, sensor replacements, or TIPM-adjacent work.\n\nDealer-level scan tool (Stellantis StarScan/wiTECH2): Not available for consumer purchase. A dealer or well-equipped shop is required for calibration procedures and software updates that require OEM-level access."},{"id":"jt-obd2-diagnostic","title":"OBD2 Scanning and Diagnostics \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"tools_diagnostic","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":200},"tools_required":["OBD2 scanner","smartphone (for Bluetooth scanners)"],"skills_required":["basic phone operation"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["tools_diagnostic","obd2","diagnostic","check-engine","codes","scanner"],"parts":[{"name":"BlueDriver Pro Bluetooth OBD2 Scanner (iOS/Android)","vendor":"BlueDriver","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Autel MaxiCheck MX808 Scan Tool","vendor":"Autel","price_estimate_usd":189},{"name":"Veepeak OBD2 Bluetooth Scanner (budget)","vendor":"Veepeak","price_estimate_usd":25},{"name":"OBDLink MX+ Bluetooth Scanner","vendor":"OBDLink","price_estimate_usd":100}],"citations":[{"title":"SAE J1979 OBD2 Standard Reference","url":"https://www.sae.org"},{"title":"BlueDriver Pro Compatibility \u2014 Gladiator JT","url":"https://www.bluedriver.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-obd2-diagnostics","jt-dual-battery-setup"],"summary":"Every Gladiator JT since 2020 has a standard OBD2 port under the driver's side dash. A $25 Bluetooth scanner and your phone will read most codes; a professional-grade tool adds live data, bi-directional tests, and Stellantis-specific subsystems.","description":"OBD2 scanning is the first step in diagnosing any check engine, stability control, or four-wheel-drive warning light. Before paying a shop's diagnostic fee, pull the code yourself \u2014 most common faults are identifiable from the code number and a bit of research.\n\n**Port location:** The OBD2 port on the JT is under the driver's side dash, to the left of the steering column, facing down. It's a standard 16-pin trapezoid connector.\n\n**What a basic scanner reads:** Generic P-codes (powertrain), B-codes (body), C-codes (chassis). These are standardized across all vehicles and any $25 scanner reads them.\n\n**What a professional scanner reads in addition:** Manufacturer-specific codes (U-codes for network faults, proprietary Stellantis codes), live sensor data, freeze frame data, bi-directional actuator tests (can command the throttle body open, fire individual injectors, test ABS modulators). The BlueDriver Pro and Autel MX808 bridge the gap between consumer and professional.\n\n1. Plug the scanner into the OBD2 port with the ignition off.\n2. For Bluetooth scanners: pair the scanner to your phone and open the companion app. For standalone scanners: turn the ignition to ON (not cranked) and the scanner powers up.\n3. Select \"Read Codes\" or \"Diagnostic Scan.\" The JT's ECM communicates over CAN bus \u2014 most scanners connect within 10\u201315 seconds.\n4. Record all stored codes, pending codes, and freeze frame data. A pending code hasn't triggered the check engine light yet but has detected a fault. Don't ignore pending codes.\n5. Look up each code using the scanner's database, the BlueDriver app's integrated repair reports, or an online resource like OBD2Pros.com.\n\n**P0128** \u2014 Thermostat performance (coolant not reaching operating temperature fast enough). Common on the Pentastar with a failing thermostat, especially after 60,000 miles. Thermostat replacement is a 1\u20132 hour DIY job.\n\n**P0300/P0301\u2013P0306** \u2014 Misfire detected (P0300 random, P030X cylinder specific). Often coil pack failure on the Pentastar \u2014 common after 60\u201380K miles. Individual coil packs run $25\u201345 and swap in 15 minutes.\n\n**P0456** \u2014 Small evap leak. Frequently a loose or cracked gas cap. Check and tighten the gas cap first. If the code returns, an evap smoke test is needed to locate the leak.\n\n**P0087** \u2014 Fuel system too lean / low fuel pressure. Could be a clogged fuel filter-pump module or weak pump. Check fuel pressure at the rail before condemning the pump.\n\n**C1431 / C1432** \u2014 ABS wheel speed sensor codes. The JT's wheel speed sensors live at each hub \u2014 trail use with mud and debris is hard on them. Inspect the sensor harness and connector for damage before replacing the sensor.\n\n**U0100** \u2014 Lost communication with ECM. Often a transient fault from a dead battery, jump start, or module reset. If it recurs consistently, investigate the CAN bus network.\n\nClearing a code doesn't fix the problem \u2014 it resets the fault counter and turns off the check engine light. If the fault condition is still present, the code will return within 1\u20133 drive cycles. Clear codes only after you've addressed the root cause.\n\nTo clear: select \"Clear Codes\" in your scanner app. The scanner sends a reset command to the ECM. All stored faults, pending codes, and readiness monitors reset.\n\n**Emissions testing note:** After clearing codes, readiness monitors take several drive cycles to complete. If the JT's emissions test is coming up, don't clear codes right before \u2014 you'll likely fail for incomplete monitors even without an active fault.\n\n- **Stellantis-specific codes**: Some JT faults generate codes that begin with a Stellantis manufacturer prefix (like P1XXX or U1XXX). Generic scanners may read the code number but not have a definition. BlueDriver's app database covers most Stellantis-specific codes.\n- **4XE models**: The 4xe plug-in hybrid adds a separate high-voltage battery management system and its own fault codes. A basic OBD2 scanner cannot fully interrogate the 4xe's HV battery system \u2014 for 4xe-specific battery and hybrid faults, an Autel MX808 or better is needed.\n- **Live data vs. codes**: A code tells you a fault was detected. Live data tells you what the sensor is reading right now. Intermittent faults that don't store a code are often caught only by watching live sensor data during the problem conditions.\n\nVeepeak mini Bluetooth OBD2: ~$25. Reads standard P/B/C codes, connects to Torque Pro or OBD Fusion apps. Good for a first scanner.\n\nOBDLink MX+: ~$100. Faster, more reliable Bluetooth, compatible with most professional apps. The step up for owners who want live data and serious diagnostics.\n\nBlueDriver Pro: ~$120. The integrated repair reports (showing verified fixes from other users with the same code on the same vehicle) are genuinely useful and set it apart from generic scanners.\n\nAutel MaxiCheck MX808: ~$189. Full bi-directional control, oil reset, ABS bleeding, and deeper Stellantis coverage. Worth it for DIYers who work on multiple vehicles or want near-professional capability."},{"id":"jt-35-inch-tire-fitment","title":"35\" Tire Fitment Guide \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"wheels_tires","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":800,"high":2200},"tools_required":["torque wrench","floor jack","jack stands"],"skills_required":["wheel swap","basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["tires","wheels","fitment","35s","lift"],"parts":[{"name":"BF Goodrich KO2 All-Terrain T/A \u2014 35x12.50R17","vendor":"BF Goodrich","price_estimate_usd":299},{"name":"Falken Wildpeak AT3W \u2014 285/70R17 (33-inch alternative)","vendor":"Falken","price_estimate_usd":229},{"name":"Nitto Ridge Grappler \u2014 35x12.50R17","vendor":"Nitto","price_estimate_usd":289},{"name":"Mickey Thompson Baja Boss A/T \u2014 35x12.50R17","vendor":"Mickey Thompson","price_estimate_usd":259}],"citations":[{"title":"Quadratec JT Tire Fitment Guide \u2014 What Fits Without a Lift","url":"https://www.quadratec.com"},{"title":"Teraflex JT Gladiator Tire Fitment \u2014 35s Guide","url":"https://www.teraflex.com"},{"title":"BF Goodrich KO2 JT Gladiator Application","url":"https://www.bfgoodrichtires.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-budget-lift","jt-tire-fitment-37s","jt-regear","jt-mid-lift"],"summary":"35-inch tires are the inflection point for the JT Gladiator \u2014 where the truck stops looking like it rolled off a dealer lot and starts looking like it belongs on a trail. Getting there cleanly requires knowing the trim, the lift, and the wheel offset interaction before you order.","description":"The Gladiator JT's factory wheel arch dimensions are wider than the JL Wrangler's \u2014 the JT is a truck with a longer wheelbase and a wider stance. This matters for tire fitment: you can fit a 35\" tire on a JT in stock trim conditions that would require a lift on the JL, depending on the specific trim and offset combination.\n\n**What fits stock (no lift):**\n\n- **Sport and Willys 17\" wheels (6\u00d75.5 bolt pattern):** A 285/70R17 (roughly 32.8\") fits without rubbing at full articulation on stock suspension. A 35x12.50R17 will rub the front inner fender liner and the lower control arm skid at full articulation. Mild trimming of the inner fender liner (5 minutes, utility knife) gets you there without a lift on many builds, but it's not clean.\n- **Rubicon 17\" wheels:** Same result \u2014 factory offset on the Rubicon places the tire slightly better than the Sport, but 35s still contact at full lock and full droop.\n\n**With 2\" of lift:**\n\nA 2\" spacer lift (or coil lift) with a 285/70R17 or 35x12.50R17 on stock offset wheels (factory JT offset is around +44mm) fits on most Sport and Willys JTs without rubbing at normal articulation. Full-lock turns may still contact. Running a wider wheel with a slight positive offset (0 to -25mm) gives additional clearance from the inner fender.\n\n**With 3.5\"\u20134\" of lift:**\n\n35x12.50R17 or 35x12.50R18 (for Rubicon 18\" options) fits cleanly on all JT trims with no rubbing at full articulation and no trimming. This is the sweet spot for 35s on the JT \u2014 enough lift to clear the tire under compression, with correct geometry correction components (a track bar drop bracket is recommended, especially for Rubicon trims with rear coil suspension rather than factory leaf).\n\n**Tire selection for the JT**\n\nThe JT's longer wheelbase creates a different on-road experience with aggressive mud terrain tires than the shorter JL. On-road noise is amplified by the longer time the tire spends in contact with road surface per mile. All-terrain tires (KO2, Wildpeak AT3W, Ridge Grappler, Baja Boss A/T) hit the best balance of trail capability and livability on the JT's street/trail split use case.\n\n**BFG KO2 35x12.50R17** (~$299 each): The benchmark all-terrain. Works on both daily driving and moderate trails. Treadwear rating is excellent \u2014 500 UTQG, which translates to 60,000\u201380,000 miles of normal use. The width makes it a good visual match on the JT.\n\n**Falken Wildpeak AT3W 285/70R17** (~$229 each): The slightly narrower footprint improves on-road behavior and fuel economy versus a 12.50\" wide tire. \"285/70R17\" measures out to approximately 33.0\" \u2014 not technically a 35, but often the better real-world choice for owners prioritizing highway comfort.\n\n**Nitto Ridge Grappler 35x12.50R17** (~$289 each): Hybrid AT/MT design with aggressive sidewall lugs. Better technical trail grip than the KO2; noisier on the highway. Correct for owners who trail-drive more than they highway-drive.\n\nFactory JT wheel offset is approximately +44mm (Sport/Willys and Rubicon alike). Running the same offset with a 35\" tire works with a lift; a lower offset (e.g., +18mm down to -12mm) pushes the tire outboard and creates more clearance between the sidewall and the inner fender at full lock. Most aftermarket 17\" wheels for the JT run between +18mm and -12mm. Stay in that range \u2014 going past about -25mm adds real leverage on the unit bearings and steering, widens the scrub radius enough to feel in every rut, and on a 12.50-wide tire will poke past the flares (a legality problem in several states).\n\nThe JT's factory gear ratio is 3.73:1 (Sport, Willys, Overland \u2014 4.10 with the Max Tow package) or 4.10:1 (Rubicon and Mojave). Running 35\" tires on 3.73s creates a drive feel issue \u2014 the engine is over-revved at freeway speed and power delivery feels sluggish off-road in 4H. Re-gearing to 4.56 corrects this on Sport/Willys trims (4.10 works but leaves nothing in reserve); the full ratio decision is walked through in the JT re-gear guide. Rubicon owners are already at 4.10 and can run 35s without re-gearing. If you're pushing to 37\" tires, re-gearing to 4.88 or 5.13 applies across all trims.\n\nThe JT stores its spare under the bed, not on a tailgate carrier like the JL \u2014 and Jeep designed the under-bed well to accept up to a 35-inch spare. A 35x12.50R17 fits, but drop the carrier and test-fit yours before a trip: with the factory winch-style carrier a wide 35 is a tight, deliberate fit, and some 35s on aftermarket wheels (different backspacing) refuse to seat flat. If yours will not fit, the options are a bed-mounted tire carrier (~$250-400), accepting a 33-inch spare as a get-home tire (fine for short distances with an open diff, risky with a locker or limited slip \u2014 mismatched diameters load the clutch packs), or leaving the spare home and betting on plugs. Carry a plug kit either way; see the recovery gear guide for what belongs in the bed.\n\n35s throw the speedometer off by roughly 6% \u2014 enough to matter for the odometer, shift points, and your next speeding ticket. A Tazer JT Mini (~$220-250) writes the correct tire size (and axle ratio, if you re-gear) into the ECU in five minutes through the OBD2 port. On a 35-inch build this is the difference between a truck that drives like the factory intended and one that always feels slightly off.\n\nTire cost (per tire): $229\u2013299 for quality AT options on 35s. A set of four runs $916\u2013$1,196 in tires alone.\n\nAdd mount and balance: ~$15\u201325 per tire at most shops.\n\nAdd lift (if needed): See the JT lift kit guide \u2014 budget lift starts at $199, quality mid-lift at $800\u2013$1,200.\n\nAdd re-gear (if needed on Sport/Willys): $1,200\u2013$2,000 at a drivetrain shop for front and rear.\n\nTotal realistic cost for 35s done right on a stock Sport JT: $1,400\u2013$3,400 depending on whether you need a lift, re-gear, and new wheels."},{"id":"jt-tire-fitment-37s","title":"37s on a Gladiator: what it really takes","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"wheels_tires","subcategory":"tire fitment","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":8.0,"high":20.0},"cost_usd":{"low":3500,"high":8000},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","breaker bar","torque wrench","angle grinder (for fender trimming)"],"skills_required":["tire mounting","gear oil change","alignment basics","fender modification awareness"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2020-present"],"tags":["37 inch tires","tire fitment","gear ratio","4.88","rubicon","fender trim","lift kit","payload"],"parts":[{"name":"37-inch tire (e.g., 315/70R17 or 37x12.5R17)","vendor":"BFGoodrich / Falken / Mickey Thompson / Nitto","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Re-gear ring and pinion \u2014 front Dana 44 (4.88 ratio)","vendor":"Yukon Gear / Motive Gear / Nitro Gear","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Re-gear ring and pinion \u2014 rear Dana 44 (4.88 ratio)","vendor":"Yukon Gear / Motive Gear / Nitro Gear","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"JT-specific lift kit (2.5\" minimum for 35s; 3.5\" for 37s)","vendor":"Teraflex / Metalcloak / Skyjacker","price_estimate_usd":800},{"name":"Differential bearing kit (front and rear, for re-gear)","vendor":"Timken / Motive Gear","price_estimate_usd":120}],"citations":[{"title":"Gladiator JT Rubicon factory spec \u2014 Jeep Media","url":"https://www.media.stellantis.com"}],"alternatives":["jt-35-inch-tire-fitment","jt-regear","jt-mid-lift","jt-shock-upgrade"],"summary":"A Rubicon leaves the factory on 33-inch tires with 4.10 gears. You can bolt 35s on with a 2.5-inch lift. 37s require gearing, fender modification, possibly brake upgrades, and a hard look at the payload math \u2014 the Rubicon's already tight at 1,200 lb. Do this upgrade with your eyes open.","description":"Rubicon: 33-inch LT285/70R17 mud-terrains, 4.10 axle gears, Rock-Trac 4:1 transfer case.\nSport/Willys: 32-inch LT255/75R17 all-terrains, 3.73 axle gears.\nMojave: 33-inch Falken Wildpeaks, 4.10 axle gears (the desert-runner trim gets the short gears from the factory).\n\nThe Rubicon's 4.10 gears are designed for 33-inch tires. The relationship between tire size and gear ratio determines how hard the engine works at highway speed and how much torque multiplication you have in the lower ranges. Going to 35s on 4.10s is noticeable but livable. Going to 37s on 4.10s will strain the engine at highway speed, kill fuel economy, and put stress on the transmission.\n\nWith a 2.5-inch lift, 35-inch tires (315/70R17 metric \u2014 about 34.4 inches \u2014 or a true 35x12.50R17) fit a Gladiator without fender trimming. Most JT-specific 2.5-inch lift kits include the right hardware to clear this size. Stick with a proper JT-specific kit \u2014 the rear is leaf-sprung and requires JT-specific components, not JL parts.\n\nThe 4.10 gears on a Rubicon are borderline acceptable with 35s. If you highway-drive at freeway speeds regularly, budget for a re-gear to 4.56 or 4.88 when you put on 35s. It's not catastrophic without it, but the truck performs better with appropriate gearing.\n\n37-inch tires change the build equation in several ways simultaneously:\n\n**Gearing (mandatory):** 37s on 4.10 gears will make the Gladiator sluggish on highway, strain the drivetrain, and reduce fuel economy significantly. 4.56 is the minimum; 4.88 is better. The re-gear means pulling both front and rear differentials, installing new ring and pinion sets, and setting up lash and backlash correctly. This is a shop job unless you have a ring-and-pinion setup background.\n\n**Lift:** 37s need 3\u20133.5 inches of lift for clearance. This moves the driveshaft angles beyond the factory range and requires a full-length JT-specific kit with control arm corrections. Long-arm kits improve the geometry further at this lift height.\n\n**Fender trimming:** The JT Rubicon has plastic fender flares that extend 4 inches from the body. At full articulation with 37s, even with a 3-inch lift, the tire can contact the fender flare. The fix is trimming the inner fender well liner and possibly trimming the flare itself. This is a cut-and-not-uncut decision \u2014 verify fitment at full stuff before you cut.\n\n**Brake consideration:** The factory brake system is sized for 33-inch tires. Going to 37s means more rotating mass and a longer lever arm to stop. The factory brakes can stop a stock truck on 37s, but stopping performance degrades. High-performance brake pads are a worthwhile addition before you start running 37s on trail.\n\n**Payload impact:** The Rubicon already runs 1,200 lb payload. 37-inch tires weigh approximately 15 lb more each than 33s \u2014 that's 60 lb added to the unsprung rotating weight. Every pound of tire and wheel added reduces your available payload. On a Rubicon loaded for trail with passengers and gear, this math gets tight quickly.\n\nThe JT's computer believes it still has 33s and 4.10s. On 37s with 4.88s, the speedometer, odometer, transmission shift points, and the ABS/ESP yaw math are all working from wrong numbers. The speedometer reads roughly 10% slow on 37s, the 8-speed hunts between gears because its shift maps assume a different effective final drive, and the stability control intervenes on bad data.\n\nThe fix is a programmer that writes the correct tire size and axle ratio into the ECU. The Tazer JT Mini (~$220-250) is the tool most JT owners use \u2014 it plugs into the OBD2 port and star connector, corrects tire size in 0.5-inch increments and the gear ratio to whatever you installed, and unlocks a few conveniences (TPMS threshold adjustment matters once you air down on trail). Budget it into the build; a 37-inch build without recalibration is an unfinished build. The re-gear itself is covered in the JT re-gear guide \u2014 the recalibration happens after the gears are in.\n\nThe Dana 44s (technically the AdvanTEK M210 front / M220 rear) carry 37s fine at the housing and shaft level. The wear moves to the perimeter parts:\n\n- **Unit bearings (front hubs):** The extra rotating mass and scrub radius load the front unit bearings hardest. Expect shortened life \u2014 a humming front end at 40,000-60,000 miles instead of 100,000+. Replacement runs ~$120-180 per side for quality Timken/SKF units, and it is a bolt-on job.\n- **Ball joints:** Same leverage problem. If you are re-gearing anyway, adding HD ball joints (Dynatrac, ~$400/set) while the axle is apart saves a second labor bill later.\n- **Steering:** The factory aluminum tie rod flexes visibly on 37s in rocks. An HD tie rod and drag link (Steer Smarts, Synergy \u2014 $400-700) is the difference between crisp steering and wander. Watch for the early symptoms in the death wobble guide before assuming it is alignment.\n- **Front driveshaft:** At 3.5+ inches of lift the front shaft operating angle approaches its limit. A cheap lift with no geometry correction accelerates CV/boot wear at the transfer case end.\n\nNone of this is a reason to skip 37s \u2014 it is the maintenance reality that separates a planned build from a string of surprise repair bills.","steps":["Confirm current axle ratio (door sticker or manual)","Price a re-gear: 4.56 for 35s, 4.88 for 37s \u2014 get quotes from two shops","Select a JT-specific lift kit for the height required (not a JL kit)","Mount one tire test-fitment before buying the full set \u2014 verify clearance at full lock and full articulation","Trim fender liner/flare as needed \u2014 minimum cuts first, test fitment after each cut","After re-gear: run break-in procedure per the gear oil manufacturer's instructions (typically avoid extended load for first 500 miles)","Get an alignment after lift installation"],"pros":["37s dramatically increase trail capability on the JT's already-solid platform","The Dana 44s handle 37s without heroic axle upgrades (unlike XJ 8.25 builds)","The aftermarket has well-developed JT-specific 37-inch fitment solutions"],"cons":["Full cost including re-gear, lift, tires, and alignment approaches $5,000\u2013$8,000","Re-gearing the front and rear is shop-level work for most owners","Rubicon payload tightens further with the additional tire/wheel weight","Fender trimming is irreversible \u2014 measure twice"]},{"id":"jt-tire-rotation-balancing","title":"Tire Rotation and Balancing \u2014 Jeep Gladiator JT","vehicle":"gladiator","category":"wheels_tires","type":"procedure","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":0,"high":80},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","breaker bar or impact wrench","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["wheel removal","safe vehicle support"],"build_tiers":["stock","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["2020-2024"],"tags":["wheels_tires","maintenance","tires","rotation","balancing"],"parts":[{"name":"Clip-on / stick-on wheel weights (for balancing)","vendor":"Perfect Equipment","price_estimate_usd":10},{"name":"TPMS-safe valve stems / caps (replace if corroded)","vendor":"Dorman","price_estimate_usd":12}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Gladiator JT Owner's Manual \u2014 Tire Maintenance","url":"https://www.jeep.com/content/dam/fca-brands/na/jeep/en_us/2022/owner-manuals/2022-jeep-gladiator-owners-manual.pdf"},{"title":"Michelin Tire Rotation Patterns \u2014 Technical Guide","url":"https://www.michelin.com/en-us/auto/tips-and-advice/tire-care/"}],"alternatives":["jt-35-inch-tire-fitment","jt-tire-fitment-37s"],"summary":"The Gladiator JT's front and rear tires wear at different rates \u2014 the front Dana 44 with steering loads wears the front edges faster. Rotate on schedule and you'll get 40,000\u201360,000 miles out of a quality all-terrain tire.","description":"The JT is a truck-based vehicle with a solid front axle. Tire wear patterns differ from a unibody SUV with independent front suspension \u2014 the front tires take more lateral stress from steering inputs, and the rear tires carry more of the static payload weight when the bed is loaded. Both factors make regular rotation important for even wear across the set.\n\n**Factory rotation interval:** Every 5,000\u20137,500 miles, or every oil change. If you run the truck hard on trails between oil changes, rotate every oil change regardless of mileage \u2014 off-road use creates uneven wear that accumulates faster than highway driving.\n\n**Rotation pattern:** The standard pattern for a non-directional tire on the JT is forward-cross (fronts go straight back, rears cross to the opposite side front). If you carry a full-size spare on the factory spare carrier, include it in the rotation as a 5-tire pattern \u2014 every 5,000 miles, one tire gets to rest and the spare enters rotation. Over time this evens wear across all five tires and extends the full set's life.\n\nFor directional tires (a tread pattern that rotates only one way): fronts go straight to rear on the same side; rears go straight to front on the same side. You cannot cross-rotate a directional tire without dismounting it from the wheel and remounting \u2014 most owners skip the 5-tire rotation with directional tires for this reason.\n\n**Balancing:** Tires should be balanced when mounted new, and rebalanced if you notice vibration at highway speeds (typically 55\u201370 mph). Off-road debris, mud packed into the wheel, and rim dings can all cause imbalance. After any rock-heavy trail run, check for mud packed in the wheel and clean it before attributing vibration to wheel balance.\n\nValve stem replacement at every tire rotation is cheap insurance \u2014 especially on a trail rig where valves take abuse from rocks and debris.\n\n1. Position the vehicle on a flat, level surface. Engage the parking brake.\n2. Break the lug nuts loose while the wheels are still on the ground \u2014 the JT's lug nuts are 7/8\" (14mm x 1.5 thread pitch) and torqued to 130 ft-lbs from the factory. Breaking them without a breaker bar or impact is difficult. Do this before jacking.\n3. Jack one axle at a time or work corner-by-corner with jack stands. For a full rotation, work from one end to the other: remove both front wheels, move the front tires to the rear positions, then remove both rear tires and move them to the front (crossing them per the forward-cross pattern).\n4. Reinstall wheels and thread lug nuts hand-tight before torquing.\n5. Torque lug nuts to 130 ft-lbs in a star pattern. The JT's 6-lug wheel pattern requires a proper star sequence to seat the wheel evenly \u2014 work across the circle, not in sequence around it.\n6. After torquing, drive 25\u201350 miles and re-check torque. Wheels can settle slightly after the first drive, especially with aluminum alloy wheels.\n\n- **Vibration starts at 55\u201365 mph and fades above 70**: Classic imbalance pattern. Have the tires balanced at a shop ($10\u201320 per tire).\n- **Vibration present at all speeds, especially over bumps**: Likely a worn shock absorber or wheel bearing, not tire balance.\n- **Vibration after a trail run**: Check for packed mud in the wheel first \u2014 remove the wheel, spray the inner barrel clean, reinstall, and see if the vibration resolves.\n\nBig all-terrain tires (35s, 37s) are more sensitive to balance than smaller tires because the rotational mass is higher. A 35\" tire that's 0.5 oz out of balance produces more vibration than a 31\" tire with the same imbalance. This is why larger tires sometimes require re-balancing sooner \u2014 the forces at speed are amplified.\n\nRotation evens out wear, but a rotation won't fix a wear pattern that's telling you something is wrong \u2014 it only spreads the damage around the set. Look at the tires before you swap them:\n\n- **Both front outer edges scrubbed:** Common on a lifted JT running big tires. Usually caster/toe knocked out of spec, or aggressive cornering. Rotation hides it for a while; an alignment fixes it. If you've lifted the truck, confirm the alignment was corrected for the new geometry.\n- **Center wear (crown worn, edges fine):** Overinflation, or a big all-terrain running more pressure than it needs unloaded. Set pressure to the door-jamb figure adjusted for your actual tire, not the sidewall max.\n- **Both edges worn, center fine:** Chronic underinflation \u2014 the tire is running on its shoulders. Also the fast track to a sidewall failure on the trail.\n- **Cupping/scalloping (scalloped dips around the tread):** Worn shocks or a wheel-bearing/balance issue, not a rotation problem. On a JT that's seen hard trail miles, suspect the shocks first \u2014 see the [shock service and upgrade notes](/db/gladiator/jt-shock-upgrade/).\n- **One-corner feathering:** Bent component or a specific alignment angle out on that corner. Rotating a feathered tire to a different corner spreads the noise and vibration to the whole set.\n\nCatching the pattern first means you rotate a healthy set and fix the cause, instead of rotating the symptom.\n\nThe JT is a heavy solid-front-axle truck, and once you're on 35s or 37s the standard spin-balance a quick-lube shop does often isn't enough. A basic spin balancer only corrects for weight distribution \u2014 it assumes the tire and wheel are perfectly round. Large off-road tires frequently aren't; a stiff sidewall or a slightly out-of-round carcass produces a vibration that stays no matter how many weights get added.\n\nA road-force balancer presses a loaded roller against the spinning tire to simulate the vehicle's weight, measuring the actual force variation and identifying the stiffest spot. The tech then \"match-mounts\" \u2014 rotating the tire on the rim so its stiff spot lines up with the wheel's low spot \u2014 which cancels the runout mechanically instead of masking it with lead. On a lifted JT with a phantom highway vibration that spin-balancing never cured, road-force balancing (often $25\u201335 per tire) is usually the real fix. Confirm your fitment and load rating are correct too \u2014 the [35-inch](/db/gladiator/jt-35-inch-tire-fitment/) and [37-inch fitment guides](/db/gladiator/jt-tire-fitment-37s/) cover what actually clears on the JT without rubbing, which itself can mimic a balance problem.\n\nDIY rotation (your own time and jack stands): $0 beyond equipment. The main cost is 45\u201360 minutes and a torque wrench.\n\nShop tire rotation: $20\u201350 at most tire stores. Often free with a tire purchase. Worth paying for if you're also having tires balanced in the same visit.\n\nTire balancing (per tire): $12\u201320. Budget $50\u201380 to balance all four on a full set service."}];